      false,
      false
    ],
    [
      "02_context.104a.bejson",
      ".bejson",
      "{\n  \"Format\": \"BEJSON\",\n  \"Format_Version\": \"104a\",\n  \"Format_Creator\": \"Elton Boehnen\",\n  \"Schema_Name\": \"Chunked-104a\",\n  \"Schema_Version\": \"1.0.1\",\n  \"Schema_Description\": \"Standard schema for chunking single projects.\",\n  \"Chunk_Date\": \"2026-11-20\",\n  \"Session_Is_Mounted\": false,\n  \"Mount_Path\": \"\",\n  \"Package_Version\": \"16\",\n  \"Records_Type\": [\n    \"Chunked\"\n  ],\n  \"Fields\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"File_Name\",\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"File_Extension\",\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"File_Content\",\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"File_Version\",\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"File_Hash\",\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Relative_Path\",\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Is_Binary\",\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Is_Mounted\",\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"Values\": [\n    [\n      \"context.txt\",\n      \".txt\",\n      \"### CRASH_COURSE_23_OFFICIAL.md\\n# BEJSON + MFDB Crash Course\\n\\n**Document Version:** 21 \\u00b7 **MFDB Spec Version:** 1.31 / 1.32 \\u00b7 **BEJSON Formats:** 104 \\u00b7 104a \\u00b7 104db  \\n**Format Creator:** Elton Boehnen \\u00b7 [https://boehnenelton2024.pages.dev](https://boehnenelton2024.pages.dev)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PART 1: BEJSON CRASH COURSE\\n**(v104, 104a, 104db) \\u2014 VERSION 1**\\n\\nBEJSON is a strict, self-describing tabular data format built on JSON. A key feature is **positional integrity**, meaning the field order within `Fields` precisely matches the value order in each record found in `Values`. This design allows for index-based access (eliminating key lookups), rapid parsing, strong typing, and integrated schema validation without the need for external files.\\n\\nAll BEJSON versions mandate the presence of these six top-level keys:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104\\\", \\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [ ... ],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [ ... ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [ [ ... ], [ ... ] ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n> **Note:** `Format_Version` is strictly `\\\"104\\\"`, `\\\"104a\\\"`, or `\\\"104db\\\"`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Common Rules (All Versions)\\n\\n* The `Fields` key must contain an array of objects, structured as follows:\\n  ```json\\n  {\\\"name\\\": string, \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"|\\\"integer\\\"|\\\"number\\\"|\\\"boolean\\\"|\\\"array\\\"|\\\"object\\\" [, \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": string]}\\n  ```\\n* Each record within `Values` must contain an exact number of elements matching the number of entries in `Fields`.\\n* Optional or missing values should be represented by `null` to maintain length and position.\\n* Duplicate field names are not permitted.\\n* `null` is considered a valid value for any declared type.\\n* `Format_Creator` must be precisely `\\\"Elton Boehnen\\\"`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Version Differences\\n\\n#### BEJSON 104 \\u2013 Single Record Type, Full Types\\n* **`Records_Type`:** An array containing exactly one string, which represents the entity name.\\n* No custom top-level keys are allowed beyond the six mandatory ones.\\n  * **Exception:** `Parent_Hierarchy` is an optional, built-in key permitted in 104. Its interpretation is intentionally flexible; applications can use it as needed (e.g., to indicate a folder path, an index hierarchy, or any logical grouping).\\n* Supports complex data types, including `array` and `object`.\\n* **Primary Use:** Ideal for homogeneous, high-throughput data such as logs, metrics, MFDB entity files, or archives.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"SensorReading\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"sensor_id\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"timestamp\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"temperature\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"number\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"tags\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"S001\\\", \\\"2026-01-10T12:00:00Z\\\", 23.5, [\\\"indoor\\\",\\\"ground\\\"]],\\n    [\\\"S002\\\", \\\"2026-01-10T12:00:00Z\\\", 19.8, null]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### BEJSON 104a \\u2013 Primitive Types + Custom Metadata\\n* **`Records_Type`:** Must be exactly one string.\\n* Custom top-level keys are allowed, but only for file-level metadata, never for per-record data.\\n* `Fields` are restricted to primitive types only: `string`, `integer`, `number`, `boolean`.\\n* Custom keys should adhere to `PascalCase` naming conventions and must not conflict with the six mandatory keys.\\n* **Primary Use:** Best suited for configuration files, health checks, simple log data, or MFDB manifests.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104a\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Server_ID\\\": \\\"WEB-01\\\",\\n  \\\"Environment\\\": \\\"Production\\\",\\n  \\\"Retention_Days\\\": 90,\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"ConfigParam\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"key\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"value\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"sensitive\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"boolean\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"db_host\\\", \\\"prod-db-01\\\", true],\\n    [\\\"max_threads\\\", \\\"32\\\", false]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### BEJSON 104db \\u2013 Multi-Entity Lightweight Database\\n* **`Records_Type`:** An array containing two or more unique strings, each representing an entity name.\\n* No custom top-level keys are allowed.\\n* The very first field in the `Fields` array must be: `{\\\"name\\\": \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"}`.\\n* The value at position 0 in every record within `Values` must precisely match one of the entries in `Records_Type`.\\n* Every field (except `Record_Type_Parent` itself) must include a `\\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\"` property, assigning it to a specific entity. Fields lacking this assignment are invalid in 104db. There are no \\\"common fields\\\" shared across all entities.\\n* Fields that are not applicable to a given entity must be set to `null` (null-padding).\\n* Supports complex data types.\\n* Enables relationships through shared ID fields (acting as logical Primary Key/Foreign Key).\\n* **Convention:** It is recommended practice to use the `_fk` suffix on foreign key fields (e.g., `owner_user_id_fk`) to indicate relationships to automated mapping tools. This is a convention and not strictly enforced by the BEJSON specification.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104db\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"User\\\", \\\"Item\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"created\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"User\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"user_id\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"User\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"username\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"User\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"created_at\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Item\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"item_id\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Item\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"name\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Item\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"owner_user_id_fk\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Item\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"User\\\", \\\"2026-01-01\\\", \\\"U01\\\", \\\"alice\\\", null, null, null, null],\\n    [\\\"User\\\", \\\"2026-01-02\\\", \\\"U02\\\", \\\"bob\\\",   null, null, null, null],\\n    [\\\"Item\\\", null, null, null, \\\"2026-01-10\\\", \\\"I01\\\", \\\"Report A\\\", \\\"U01\\\"],\\n    [\\\"Item\\\", null, null, null, \\\"2026-01-10\\\", \\\"I02\\\", \\\"Report B\\\", \\\"U02\\\"]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n> **Note:** Because the `\\\"created\\\"` field is exclusively assigned to `\\\"User\\\"`, `\\\"Item\\\"` records must set it to `null`. Conversely, `\\\"User\\\"` records must null out `\\\"created_at\\\"`. If a timestamp field is desired across multiple entities, it must be defined separately for each entity with consistent naming, or the duplication must be accepted. Each field is strictly owned by one entity.\\n\\n##### When 104db might be superior to SQLite:\\n* When you need a relational database that doesn't need to hold 10,000 records or more.\\n* When you want an LLM to be able to directly read the database and modify it as if it were a text file.\\n\\n##### Limitations:\\nDue to the requirement of holding positional integrity and using null padding, the 104db file grows exponentially with each new field and each new record and at a certain point comes unviable. The best usage for 104DB is for limited size, single file quick access to relational data that is readable by any programming language natively and to AI.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### BEJSON Core Nesting (Nested 104)\\n\\nBEJSON 104 allows for deeply nested, validated tabular structures directly within its cells via the `Core_Nesting` library (Error codes 130-159). This enables complex, hierarchical data without breaking positional integrity.\\n\\n* **Embedded 104 Documents:** A cell within a 104 document can contain a fully valid BEJSON 104 document (parsed as a string or object). These nested documents must possess all six mandatory keys and `Format_Creator: \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\"`. There is no new `Format_Version` for this; it is simply 104 embedded in 104.\\n* **Implicit Foreign Keys:** The physical cell coordinate\\u2014defined by `NestAddress(parent_fp, row, col, depth)`\\u2014serves as the implicit foreign key. This completely eliminates the need to declare explicit relational foreign keys at the nesting level.\\n* **Column-Schema Uniformity:** To prevent structural chaos, the nesting validator enforces a strict contract: all nested documents within the same parent column must share identical field names, types, and sequence order. Any violation triggers a hard `E134` schema mismatch.\\n* **Dotted-Path Navigation:** The dotted-path query engine (`bejson_nesting_query`) allows traversal of deeply nested datasets using a standard grammar (e.g., `player_inventory[*].item_id`) across any number of recursive depths.\\n* **Safe Mutation & Padding:** When mutating a nested document (`bejson_nesting_mutate`), the core engine enforces append-only schema modifications and automatically null-pads existing records to preserve positional integrity in-place.\\n* **Depth Limit:** Nesting supports unlimited structural depth up to a hard safety ceiling of 16 (`NESTING_MAX_DEPTH`).\\n* **Records_Type Convention:** The `Records_Type` in a nested document should match the parent field name in `PascalCase`. Mismatches yield a standardization warning, but not a hard error.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Validation Summary\\n\\nA BEJSON document is considered valid if it adheres to the following criteria:\\n\\n1. Valid JSON syntax.\\n2. All six mandatory top-level keys are present with their correct values.\\n3. `Format_Creator` must be precisely `\\\"Elton Boehnen\\\"`.\\n4. The `Fields` structure is correct, and no duplicate field names exist.\\n5. The length of every record in `Values` equals the length of the `Fields` array.\\n6. The type of each value matches its declared type in `Fields` (with `null` always being an allowed value).\\n7. **Version-specific rules:**\\n   * **104:** Requires a single record type, allows complex types, and forbids custom headers (except for `Parent_Hierarchy`).\\n   * **104a:** Requires a single record type, allows only primitive types, and permits custom headers for file-level metadata (these must be `PascalCase` and not conflict with the six mandatory keys).\\n   * **104db:** Requires two or more record types, mandates that every field (except the `Record_Type_Parent` discriminator field itself) has a `Record_Type_Parent` assignment, forbids custom headers, and requires `null` for non-applicable fields.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Best Practices\\n\\n* Use `snake_case` for field names.\\n* Use `PascalCase` for custom headers in BEJSON 104a.\\n* Append new fields only to the end of the `Fields` array to preserve positional integrity for existing parsers.\\n* Use `null` (rather than an empty array or object) for truly missing data, unless an empty state carries specific meaning.\\n* Manage your application schema version independently from `Format_Version`:\\n  * For **104a / 104db**: Use a custom header like `Schema_Version` (e.g., `\\\"v1.0\\\"`).\\n  * For **104**: Custom headers are forbidden; embed the version within the `Records_Type` string (e.g., `[\\\"SensorReading_v1_0\\\"]`) or manage it externally.\\n* **Schema changes:** Appending fields is generally safe (minor change). Removing or retyping a field constitutes a breaking change (major change).\\n* For large datasets, consider splitting data into multiple complete BEJSON files (MFDB).\\n* Encrypt sensitive files both at rest and during transit.\\n* In 104db, use the `_fk` suffix on foreign key fields (this is a convention, not enforced by the specification).\\n* **The Event/Audit entity pattern:** In 104db, define a dedicated `\\\"Event\\\"` entity within `Records_Type` to implement audit trails. Link it to other entities using fields like `related_entity_id_fk`, and store before/after state in a `change_details` field (of type `object`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Summary of Version Capabilities\\n\\n| Feature | BEJSON 104 | BEJSON 104a | BEJSON 104db |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Primary Use** | High-throughput Logs | Configs, Metrics | Multi-Entity Database |\\n| **Record Types** | Single (1) | Single (1) | Multiple (2+) |\\n| **Custom Headers** | \\u274c Forbidden\\\\* | \\u2705 Allowed (File Meta) | \\u274c Forbidden |\\n| **Data Types** | Complex (Array/Object) | Primitives Only | Complex (Array/Object) |\\n| **Discriminator** | N/A | N/A | `Record_Type_Parent` |\\n\\n*\\\\* `Parent_Hierarchy` is an optional, built-in exception for BEJSON 104.*\\n\\n---\\n---\\n\\n## PART 2: MFDB CRASH COURSE\\n**(Multifile Database) \\u2014 VERSION 1**\\n\\nMFDB is a database architecture that layers on top of BEJSON. It is not a new BEJSON format version \\u2014 it orchestrates existing BEJSON formats (104 and 104a) into a structured multi-file database system. Every file in an MFDB is a fully valid standalone BEJSON document. MFDB adds meaning through file naming, a key field (`Parent_Hierarchy`), and a manifest registry.\\n\\n**Core concept:** One BEJSON 104a file (the manifest) acts as a registry for any number of BEJSON 104 files (entity files). Each entity file holds records for exactly one entity type. The manifest and its entity files together form one complete MFDB database.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Architecture\\n\\nAn MFDB database on disk:\\n\\n```text\\nmydb/\\n  104a.mfdb.bejson        \\u2190 manifest (always at the root)\\n  data/\\n    user.bejson           \\u2190 entity file (BEJSON 104)\\n    order.bejson          \\u2190 entity file (BEJSON 104)\\n    product.bejson        \\u2190 entity file (BEJSON 104)\\n```\\n\\n* The manifest filename is fixed: `104a.mfdb.bejson`\\n* Entity files default to `data/<entity_name_lowercase>.bejson`\\n* The `data/` subdirectory is the standard default. Any relative path is allowed as long as it is recorded accurately in the manifest and the `Parent_Hierarchy` resolves correctly. Relative paths must not escape the database root.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### The Manifest File\\n\\nThe manifest is a BEJSON 104a document. All 104a rules apply.\\n\\n#### Fixed values required by MFDB:\\n* `Records_Type` must be exactly: `[\\\"mfdb\\\"]`\\n* `Format_Version` must be: `\\\"104a\\\"`\\n* `Format_Creator` must be: `\\\"Elton Boehnen\\\"`\\n\\n#### Standard custom headers (PascalCase, per 104a rules):\\n* `MFDB_Version` (string): MFDB spec version. Current value: `\\\"1.31\\\"`. **Required.**\\n* `DB_Name` (string): Human-readable database name. **Required.**\\n* `DB_Description` (string): Short description of the database. *Optional.*\\n* `Schema_Version` (string): Application schema version (semver recommended). *Optional.*\\n* `Author` (string): Author name. *Optional.*\\n* `Created_At` (string): ISO 8601 UTC creation timestamp. *Optional.*\\n* `Network_Role` (string): Federation role: `\\\"Master\\\"` or `\\\"Slave\\\"`. *Optional (v1.31).*\\n\\n> Custom headers must not collide with any of the 6 mandatory BEJSON keys. `MFDB_Version` and `DB_Name` are treated as required standard headers.\\n\\n#### Fields \\u2014 required:\\n* `entity_name` (string): Unique name of the entity (e.g. `\\\"User\\\"`). Cannot be `null`.\\n* `file_path` (string): Relative path from the manifest directory to the entity file (e.g. `\\\"data/user.bejson\\\"`). Cannot be `null`. Must be unique across all records.\\n\\n#### Fields \\u2014 standard optional (include all if present, always in this order):\\n* `description` (string): Human-readable description of the entity.\\n* `record_count` (integer): Advisory count of records in the entity file. May drift. Not enforced. Kept in sync by tooling, not the spec.\\n* `schema_version` (string): Entity-level schema version. Optional per entity.\\n* `primary_key` (string): Field name of the entity's primary key (e.g. `\\\"user_id\\\"`). Used by FK resolution tooling. Not enforced by the spec.\\n\\nNo other custom fields in the manifest `Fields` array are defined by MFDB 1.0. Applications may add fields beyond the standard set; they must follow BEJSON 104a positional rules and must be documented at the application level.\\n\\n#### Manifest record constraints:\\n* `entity_name` must be unique across all records (no two entities share a name).\\n* `file_path` must be unique across all records (one file per entity).\\n* `entity_name` and `file_path` must not be `null` in any record.\\n* Every `file_path` must resolve to a file that exists on disk.\\n\\n#### Example manifest:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104a\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"MFDB_Version\\\": \\\"1.31\\\",\\n  \\\"DB_Name\\\": \\\"StoreFront\\\",\\n  \\\"DB_Description\\\": \\\"E-commerce product and order database\\\",\\n  \\\"Schema_Version\\\": \\\"1.0.0\\\",\\n  \\\"Author\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Created_At\\\": \\\"2026-04-14T00:00:00Z\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"mfdb\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"entity_name\\\",    \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"file_path\\\",      \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"description\\\",    \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"record_count\\\",   \\\"type\\\": \\\"integer\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"schema_version\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"primary_key\\\",    \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"User\\\",    \\\"data/user.bejson\\\",    \\\"Registered users\\\",   3, \\\"1.0\\\", \\\"user_id\\\"],\\n    [\\\"Order\\\",   \\\"data/order.bejson\\\",   \\\"Customer orders\\\",    5, \\\"1.0\\\", \\\"order_id\\\"],\\n    [\\\"Product\\\", \\\"data/product.bejson\\\", \\\"Product catalogue\\\",  8, \\\"1.0\\\", \\\"product_id\\\"]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Entity Files\\n\\nEach entity file is a BEJSON 104 document. All BEJSON 104 rules apply. The `Parent_Hierarchy` built-in exception is mandatory in MFDB.\\n\\n#### Fixed values required by MFDB:\\n* `Format_Version` must be: `\\\"104\\\"`\\n* `Format_Creator` must be: `\\\"Elton Boehnen\\\"`\\n* `Records_Type` must contain exactly one string \\u2014 the entity name \\u2014 and it must match the `entity_name` registered for this file in the manifest exactly (case-sensitive).\\n* `Parent_Hierarchy` must be present and must be a relative path from the entity file's own directory back to the manifest file. This value enables tools to discover the owning manifest without any external configuration.\\n  * `Parent_Hierarchy` is a relative path, not an absolute path.\\n  * Entity in `data/user.bejson` pointing to root manifest: `\\\"../104a.mfdb.bejson\\\"`\\n  * Entity at root level pointing to root manifest: `\\\"104a.mfdb.bejson\\\"`\\n  * `Parent_Hierarchy` is always from the entity file's directory, never the DB root.\\n\\nRecords are dense. Unlike BEJSON 104db which null-pads fields across entity types in a single file, MFDB uses one file per entity type. Records inside an entity file should have no structurally-motivated null padding. A `null` value in an entity record means the data is genuinely absent, not that the field belongs to a different entity.\\n\\nBEJSON 104 allows complex types (`array`, `object`). These are valid in entity files. No custom top-level keys. `Parent_Hierarchy` is the only built-in exception key.\\n\\n#### Example entity file (`data/order.bejson`):\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Parent_Hierarchy\\\": \\\"../104a.mfdb.bejson\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"Order\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"order_id\\\",     \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"user_id_fk\\\",   \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"total\\\",        \\\"type\\\": \\\"number\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"placed_at\\\",    \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"items\\\",        \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"ORD01\\\", \\\"U01\\\", 49.99,  \\\"2026-02-01T10:00:00Z\\\", [\\\"P01\\\",\\\"P03\\\"]],\\n    [\\\"ORD02\\\", \\\"U02\\\", 120.00, \\\"2026-02-03T14:30:00Z\\\", [\\\"P02\\\"]],\\n    [\\\"ORD03\\\", \\\"U01\\\", 9.99,   \\\"2026-02-10T09:15:00Z\\\", [\\\"P04\\\"]]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Discovery Algorithm\\n\\nA tool encountering any `.bejson` file can identify its MFDB role natively:\\n\\n1. Attempt to parse the file as JSON. If it fails: not MFDB-related.\\n2. Read `Format_Version` and the filename.\\n3. If `Format_Version == \\\"104a\\\"` **AND** filename ends in `\\\".mfdb.bejson\\\"`:  \\n   $$\\\\rightarrow$$ **This is a manifest.**\\n4. If `Format_Version == \\\"104\\\"` **AND** `\\\"Parent_Hierarchy\\\"` key is present:  \\n   $$\\\\rightarrow$$ **This is an entity file.**\\n5. Otherwise:  \\n   $$\\\\rightarrow$$ **Standalone BEJSON** (not part of any MFDB).\\n\\nThe discovery algorithm is file-local. No directory scanning required. An entity file with `Parent_Hierarchy` present but pointing to a non-existent manifest is an orphan \\u2014 it is invalid as MFDB even though it is valid BEJSON 104.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Relationships and Foreign Keys\\n\\nMFDB does not enforce referential integrity. FK relationships are declared by convention and surfaced to tooling via two mechanisms:\\n\\n1. **Field naming** \\u2014 FK fields end in `_fk` (e.g. `user_id_fk`, `product_id_fk`). This signals to tools that the field references a primary key elsewhere.\\n2. **`primary_key` declaration in the manifest** \\u2014 the `primary_key` field for an entity names the field that other entities' `_fk` fields reference.\\n\\n#### Resolution rule for tooling (not enforced by spec):\\nA field named `X_id_fk` in entity A is assumed to reference the entity whose `primary_key` in the manifest is `X_id`, or whose `entity_name` lowercased appears in the FK field name. Tooling should treat unresolved FKs as warnings only.\\n\\nFK values in records must satisfy the declared type (typically `string` or `integer`). `null` is valid for any FK field \\u2014 it represents no relationship. Joins between entity files are performed by the application or tooling layer. MFDB provides no join syntax.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Validation Rules\\n\\n#### Level 1 \\u2014 Manifest validation:\\n* File exists and is valid BEJSON 104a.\\n* `Records_Type` is exactly `[\\\"mfdb\\\"]`.\\n* `Fields` include `entity_name` and `file_path`.\\n* No record has `null` `entity_name` or `null` `file_path`.\\n* `entity_name` values are unique across all records.\\n* `file_path` values are unique across all records.\\n* Every `file_path` resolves to an existing file on disk.\\n\\n#### Level 2 \\u2014 Entity file validation (per entity):\\n* File is valid BEJSON 104.\\n* `Parent_Hierarchy` key is present.\\n* `Parent_Hierarchy` resolves to an existing manifest file.\\n* `Records_Type` contains exactly one string.\\n* That string matches the `entity_name` in the manifest record for this file.\\n* Manifest file path to this entity matches this entity's actual path (bidirectional check \\u2014 manifest $$\\\\rightarrow$$ entity and entity $$\\\\rightarrow$$ manifest agree).\\n\\n#### Level 3 \\u2014 Database-level validation:\\n* All Level 1 and Level 2 checks pass for all entities.\\n* `record_count` mismatch between manifest and actual file row count: **warning only**.\\n* FK fields with no resolvable `primary_key` target: **warning only (strict mode)**.\\n\\nErrors are hard failures. Warnings are advisory and do not invalidate the database.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Error Code Ranges (standard)\\n\\n* **1\\u201315:** BEJSON Validator errors (`BEJSONValidationError`)\\n* **20\\u201327:** BEJSON Core errors (`BEJSONCoreError`)\\n* **30\\u201349:** MFDB Validator errors (`MFDBValidationError`)\\n* **50\\u201369:** MFDB Core errors (`MFDBCoreError`)\\n\\n#### MFDB Validator error codes (30\\u201349):\\n* **30:** Not a manifest\\n* **31:** Not an entity file\\n* **32:** Manifest `Records_Type` invalid\\n* **33:** Entity file not found (path resolution)\\n* **34:** Entity name mismatch (`Records_Type` vs manifest)\\n* **35:** Duplicate entry (`entity_name` or `file_path`)\\n* **36:** Missing `Parent_Hierarchy`\\n* **37:** Manifest file not found\\n* **38:** Bidirectional path check failed\\n* **39:** FK unresolved (strict mode)\\n* **40:** Missing required field in manifest (`entity_name` or `file_path`)\\n* **41:** Null in required field (`entity_name` or `file_path`)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Validation vs. Standardization (v1.31 Distinction)\\n\\nMFDB v1.31 enforces a strict conceptual separation between structural validity and architectural standardization.\\n\\n#### Validation (Structural Correctness):\\n* **Scope:** \\\"Is the file a valid BEJSON/MFDB document?\\\"\\n* **Enforced by:** `lib_mfdb_validator.py`.\\n* **Criteria:** Proper JSON syntax, mandatory BEJSON keys present, positional integrity (`Values` match `Fields` length), `Parent_Hierarchy` resolving, and bidirectional path verification between manifest and entity.\\n* **Failure result:** Hard Error (Database is considered \\\"Broken\\\").\\n\\n#### Standardization (Architectural Compliance):\\n* **Scope:** \\\"Does the database follow the official MFDB conventions?\\\"\\n* **Enforced by:** Standard-aware tooling and architectural audits.\\n* **Criteria:** Naming conventions (`snake_case` fields, `PascalCase` entities), Key standards (`_id` PKs and `_fk` foreign keys), and Federation Protocols (Master/Slave headers and registry presence).\\n* **Failure result:** Non-Standard Warning (Database is \\\"Valid but Non-Standard\\\").\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Standardization Protocols (Architectural Conventions)\\n\\n* **Manifest filename:** `104a.mfdb.bejson` (Invariant)\\n* **Entity filenames:** `<entity_name_lowercase>.bejson`. Multi-word entities: `snake_case` (e.g. `order_item.bejson`)\\n* **Entity field names:** `snake_case` (inherited from BEJSON best practices)\\n* **Primary key fields:** `<entity_name_lower>_id` (e.g. `user_id`, `order_id`)\\n* **Foreign key fields:** `<target_pk_field>_fk` (e.g. `user_id_fk`, `product_id_fk`)\\n* **Entity names:** `PascalCase` string (e.g. `\\\"User\\\"`, `\\\"OrderItem\\\"`)\\n* **DB_Name header:** Human-readable string, no format restriction\\n* **Schema_Version:** Semver recommended (e.g. `\\\"1.0.0\\\"`)\\n* **MFDB_Version:** `\\\"1.31\\\"` for the current specification\\n\\n---\\n\\n### The MFDB v1.3.1 Master-Slave Federation\\n\\nYour environment is now successfully split into a tiered, federated node system designed to maximize AI context efficiency through \\\"Structural Blindness\\\" and \\\"One-Way Awareness\\\".\\n\\n#### 1. Node Roles & The Network_Role Standard\\nInstead of refactoring your core libraries to look for a new key, your `104a.mfdb.bejson` manifests correctly use the `Network_Role` header to define their topological status as either `\\\"Master\\\"` or `\\\"Slave\\\"`.\\n\\n* **Master Node (Administrative Layer):** Acts as the authoritative registry (\\\"The Truth\\\"). It has full visibility and polling access to all connected Slaves, handling global policy distribution and long-term data distillation.\\n* **Slave Node (Context Window):** Acts as the high-performance operational workspace. It is structurally blind, meaning it operates without any hardcoded paths to the Master and lacks read-access to the Master's archives, keeping the active context window completely lean.\\n\\n#### 2. Active Communication Protocols\\nTo keep the Slave blind while keeping the system synchronized, your architecture now relies on two unidirectional data flows:\\n\\n* **One-Way Push (Log Distillation):** Your `initialization_watcher_service` allows the Slave node to autonomously truncate its local logs and push the metrics up to the Master's polling directory.\\n* **Inverse Drop-Zone Polling (Atomic Updates):** When the Master needs to enforce a new policy or configuration, it formats the update as a BEJSON 104a document and pushes it directly into the Slave's local directory (e.g., `BEJSON_Core/Data/`). It uses an OS-level atomic file swap (`os.rename`) to instantly replace the active file, preventing partial-read crashes. The blind Slave simply polls its own directory to ingest the updates.\\n\\nBy completing this update while keeping the `Network_Role` key, you have successfully secured the v1.3.1 strategic benefits: **Zero-Bloat Context** (administrative scaffolding is hidden from the AI), **Atomic Integrity** (hardware-level protection for updates), and **Scalable Silos** (allowing your Master to easily control multiple portable environments).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### MFDB-132: Session-Based Workspace Mounting (Chunked-104a)\\n\\nThe MFDB-132 specification represents a major shift from standard zipped multi-file databases. It collapses an entire federated database\\u2014including the central master manifest and every entity file\\u2014into a single, portable Chunked-104a document that serves as a packaging engine.\\n\\n* **The Chunked-104a Schema:** The packaging schema uses BEJSON 104a with a strict, fixed set of fields: `File_Name`, `File_Extension`, `File_Content`, `File_Version`, `File_Hash`, `Relative_Path`, `Is_Binary`, and `Is_Mounted`.\\n* **Fixed Identifier:** The chunk schema version is hardcoded to `1.32` (`MFDB_CHUNK_SCHEMA_VERSION`), identifying the payload specifically as an MFDB-132 package.\\n* **Cryptographic Sticky Mounts:** When mounting an archive using `MFDB132Archive.mount`, the system writes a dedicated `.mfdb132_lock` file. Upon future mounts, if the package's current SHA-256 hash matches the lock's recorded `original_hash` and the schema validates, the system reuses the existing workspace instantly rather than running a costly re-extraction.\\n* **Pre-Write Validation Gates:** During a database commit, the runtime executes complete validation rules as a strict gate. If any entity is corrupted, the commit is rejected before touching physical storage, protecting the atomic integrity of your master dataset.\\n\\nThis is purely additive\\u2014nothing about the v1.31 disk layout, manifest structure, or core validation rules changes inside the active workspace. It simply provides a secure, single-file transport and session-mounting layer for MFDB.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Validation Rules (Structural Requirements)\\n\\n* Keep the manifest at the database root. Do not nest it inside subdirectories.\\n* Entity files default to the `data/` subdirectory. Flat root placement is allowed but not recommended for databases with many entities.\\n* Always write files atomically (write to temp, rename). Partial writes corrupt the database. This is especially critical for the manifest.\\n* After any write operation that changes row count, sync `record_count` in the manifest. Treat it as advisory \\u2014 never rely on it for correctness in reads.\\n* Add new fields only at the end of an entity's `Fields` array. Inserting or removing fields mid-array breaks positional integrity for all existing records.\\n* **Schema changes:** appending a field is a minor (safe) change. Removing, retyping, or reordering fields is a breaking (major) change. Increment `Schema_Version` accordingly.\\n* Use `null` for genuinely absent values. Do not use empty string as a null substitute. Do not use empty array or empty object for missing scalar data.\\n* Use PK/FK naming conventions even if you do not use relational tooling. It costs nothing and enables future tooling.\\n* Do not store sensitive data in plaintext. Encrypt at rest and in transit.\\n* For large entities (thousands of records), consider splitting into multiple MFDB databases rather than a single entity file with unbounded growth.\\n* An MFDB export package (zip) should include the manifest at the root and all entity files at their declared relative paths. The zip should be extractable and immediately valid without path adjustment.\\n* Validate bidirectionally on load, not just on create. File moves break `Parent_Hierarchy` silently.\\n* Do not allow two manifest files in a single database directory. One manifest per database is the invariant.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### What MFDB Is Not\\n\\n* MFDB is not a new BEJSON format version. There is no `Format_Version` `\\\"mfdb\\\"`.\\n* MFDB is not a query language or a runtime database engine. It is a file organization and naming convention with a manifest registry.\\n* MFDB does not replace BEJSON 104db. 104db puts multiple entity types in one file with null-padding. MFDB puts each entity type in its own file with no padding. Choose based on use case: 104db for small, tightly coupled datasets that ship as a single file; MFDB for larger, independently writable entities.\\n* MFDB does not enforce referential integrity. FK conventions are advisory.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Validation Summary Table\\n\\n| Check | Level | Hard/Warn |\\n| :--- | :---: | :---: |\\n| Manifest is valid BEJSON 104a | 1 | Hard |\\n| `Records_Type == [\\\"mfdb\\\"]` | 1 | Hard |\\n| `entity_name` and `file_path` fields | 1 | Hard |\\n| No null `entity_name` / `file_path` | 1 | Hard |\\n| Unique `entity_name` and `file_path` | 1 | Hard |\\n| All `file_paths` resolve on disk | 1 | Hard |\\n| Entity is valid BEJSON 104 | 2 | Hard |\\n| `Parent_Hierarchy` present | 2 | Hard |\\n| `Parent_Hierarchy` resolves | 2 | Hard |\\n| `Records_Type` single-entry match | 2 | Hard |\\n| Bidirectional path check | 2 | Hard |\\n| `record_count` accuracy | 3 | Warning |\\n| FK target unresolved (strict) | 3 | Warning |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Feature Summary\\n\\n| Feature | MFDB |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **Manifest format** | BEJSON 104a (`104a.mfdb.bejson`) |\\n| **Entity format** | BEJSON 104 (one file per entity) |\\n| **Back-reference key** | `Parent_Hierarchy` (relative path) |\\n| **Records per file** | Dense (no null-padding for schema gaps) |\\n| **FK enforcement** | None \\u2014 convention only (`_fk` suffix) |\\n| **Custom headers** | Manifest only (104a rules apply) |\\n| **Complex types** | Allowed in entity files (BEJSON 104) |\\n| **Multi-entity in one** | No \\u2014 use BEJSON 104db for that |\\n| **Spec versions** | `MFDB_Version: \\\"1.31\\\"` / Packaging: `\\\"1.32\\\"` |\\n\\n### CRASH_COURSE_24_OFFICIAL.md\\n# BEJSON + MFDB Crash Course\\n\\n**Document Version 24 (Aligned) \\u00b7 Format 105 Integrity Era & MFDB Spec Version 1.31 / 1.32**  \\n*Aligned with `libraries_105` Production Release & Unified Core Engines*  \\n**Format Creator:** Elton Boehnen \\u00b7 https://boehnenelton2024.pages.dev  \\n**Date:** 2026-10-13 \\u00b7 **RELATIONAL_ID:** 3da8c870-8a4b-4803-963f-9df2607217cd  \\n**Supported Formats:** 104 Series (104 \\u00b7 104a \\u00b7 104db) and 105 Integrity Series (105 \\u00b7 105a \\u00b7 105db)  \\n\\n---\\n\\n## Read This First \\u2014 What This Document Is (and Isn't)\\n\\nThis is the **authoritative conceptual and technical reference** for understanding what BEJSON and MFDB are, how the formats are shaped, and how the pieces fit together across both the **104 Positional Era** and the **105 Integrity Era**. It exists so a developer, architect, or AI agent can read any `.bejson` file, manifest, or project across the ecosystem and know exactly how it is structured.\\n\\n**It is not a tutorial for writing a BEJSON parser or engine from scratch.** The core engine, structural validator, upgrade utility, and MFDB orchestration layer already exist as maintained libraries (`Lib_PY`, `Lib_JS`, `Lib_TS`, `Lib_SH` in `libraries_105` \\u2014 see Part 3). Use them. Do not re-derive their logic from scratch.\\n\\n### The Architectural Evolution: 104 vs. 105\\n\\n* **The 104 Series (`104`, `104a`, `104db`) \\u2014 The Positional Era**: Relies on raw positional index alignment where row elements in `Values` map 1:1 to indices in `Fields`.\\n* **The 105 Series (`105`, `105a`, `105db`) \\u2014 The Integrity Era**: Introduces cryptographic immutability, entity traceability, and automated cache self-maintenance:\\n  1. **Mandatory `field_uuid`**: Every field definition in `Fields` carries an immutable, unique UUID string.\\n  2. **Mandatory `record_uuid`**: Every row in `Values` prepends a reserved UUID at a fixed offset (column 0 for 105/105a; column 1 for 105db).\\n  3. **Self-Maintaining Field Map Cache**: The cache is self-healing. The engine checks a lightweight fingerprint on every read and automatically re-indexes if the schema changed.\\n  4. **Fail-on-Switch Enforcement**: Merely bumping a file header from `104` to `105` without compiling UUIDs causes an immediate hard validation failure (`E_INVALID_FIELDS` / `E_INVALID_VALUES`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n# PART 1: BEJSON Crash Course\\n\\n*(104 / 104a / 104db and 105 / 105a / 105db)*\\n\\nBEJSON is a strict, self-describing tabular data format built on JSON. Structurally, it eliminates the \\\"Key Repetition Tax\\\" of standard JSON by declaring the schema once in a header and locking data records into dense positional arrays.\\n\\nAll BEJSON versions mandate the presence of these **Six Top-Level Mandatory Keys**:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104\\\" | \\\"104a\\\" | \\\"104db\\\" | \\\"105\\\" | \\\"105a\\\" | \\\"105db\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [ ... ],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [ ... ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [ [ ... ], [ ... ] ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n* `Format`: Strictly `\\\"BEJSON\\\"`.\\n* `Format_Creator`: Strictly `\\\"Elton Boehnen\\\"`.\\n* `Format_Version`: Strictly one of `\\\"104\\\"`, `\\\"104a\\\"`, `\\\"104db\\\"`, `\\\"105\\\"`, `\\\"105a\\\"`, or `\\\"105db\\\"`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Common Rules (All Versions)\\n\\n* The `Fields` key must contain an array of objects, structured as:\\n  ```json\\n  {\\\"name\\\": string, \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"|\\\"integer\\\"|\\\"boolean\\\"|\\\"array\\\"|\\\"object\\\" [, \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": string] [, \\\"field_uuid\\\": string]}\\n  ```\\n* Non-spec types (e.g. `any`, `ANY`, `text`, `float`) are rejected with `E_INVALID_FIELDS` (Code 6).\\n* **`integer`**: Standard numeric type for all counts, discrete quantities, and exact financial figures (stored in base units/cents).\\n* **`string`**: Text values and textual identifiers.\\n* **`boolean`**: `true` or `false` only.\\n* **`array` / `object`**: Complex nested structures (permitted in 104, 104db, 105, 105db; strictly forbidden in 104a and 105a).\\n* Each record within `Values` must contain an exact number of elements matching the required row width.\\n* Optional or missing values must be represented by `null` to maintain length and position \\u2014 never omitted, never empty strings `\\\"\\\"` as null substitutes.\\n* Duplicate field names are not permitted.\\n* `null` is considered a valid value for any declared type.\\n* **Field Resolution Standard**: Reads and writes go through the cached field map keyed by field name or `field_uuid`, not hardcoded numeric indices.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Version Differences: The 104 Series (Positional Era)\\n\\n### BEJSON 104 \\u2014 Single Record Type, Full Types\\n* `Records_Type`: An array containing exactly one string (the entity name).\\n* No custom top-level keys allowed beyond the six mandatory keys (except the built-in `Parent_Hierarchy` exception).\\n* Supports complex data types (`array` and `object`).\\n* Primary use: Homogeneous datasets, logs, metrics, MFDB entity files, or archives.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"SensorReading\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"sensor_id\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"timestamp\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"temperature_celsius\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"integer\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"tags\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"S001\\\", \\\"2026-01-10T12:00:00Z\\\", 24, [\\\"indoor\\\", \\\"ground\\\"]],\\n    [\\\"S002\\\", \\\"2026-01-10T12:00:00Z\\\", 20, null]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### BEJSON 104a \\u2014 Primitive Types + Custom Metadata\\n* `Records_Type`: Must be exactly one string.\\n* Custom top-level keys are allowed for file-level metadata (PascalCase recommended).\\n* Fields are restricted to primitive types only: `string`, `integer`, `boolean`. Complex types (`array`, `object`) throw `E_INVALID_FIELDS`.\\n* Primary use: Configuration files, health checks, simple log data, or MFDB manifests.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104a\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Server_ID\\\": \\\"WEB-01\\\",\\n  \\\"Environment\\\": \\\"Production\\\",\\n  \\\"Retention_Days\\\": 90,\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"ConfigParam\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"key\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"value\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"sensitive\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"boolean\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"db_host\\\", \\\"prod-db-01\\\", true],\\n    [\\\"max_threads\\\", \\\"32\\\", false]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### BEJSON 104db \\u2014 Multi-Entity Lightweight Database\\n* `Records_Type`: An array containing two or more unique strings.\\n* No custom top-level keys allowed.\\n* The very first field in `Fields` must be `{\\\"name\\\": \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"}`.\\n* Position 0 of every record in `Values` must match a `Records_Type` entry.\\n* Every other field must carry a `\\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\"` property assigning it to one owning entity.\\n* Fields not applicable to a given entity must be `null` (null-padding).\\n* Relationships use shared ID fields; convention recommends `_fk` suffix for foreign keys.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104db\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"User\\\", \\\"Item\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"created\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"User\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"user_id\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"User\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"username\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"User\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"created_at\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Item\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"item_id\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Item\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"name\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Item\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"owner_user_id_fk\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Item\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"User\\\", \\\"2026-01-01\\\", \\\"U01\\\", \\\"alice\\\", null, null, null, null],\\n    [\\\"User\\\", \\\"2026-01-02\\\", \\\"U02\\\", \\\"bob\\\",   null, null, null, null],\\n    [\\\"Item\\\", null, null, null, \\\"2026-01-10\\\", \\\"I01\\\", \\\"Report A\\\", \\\"U01\\\"],\\n    [\\\"Item\\\", null, null, null, \\\"2026-01-10\\\", \\\"I02\\\", \\\"Report B\\\", \\\"U02\\\"]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Version Differences: The 105 Series (Integrity Era)\\n\\nFormat 105 elevates BEJSON from a simple positional array into an immutable, cryptographically traceable data structure.\\n\\n### 1. Mandatory `field_uuid`\\nIn 105, 105a, and 105db, every single field object in `Fields` must carry a permanent, unique `\\\"field_uuid\\\"` string:\\n```json\\n{\\\"name\\\": \\\"sensor_id\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"6e1c2b4a-1111-4444-8888-abcdef111111\\\"}\\n```\\nIf any field is missing `field_uuid` or carries a duplicate UUID, the validator halts with `E_INVALID_FIELDS`.\\n\\n### 2. Mandatory `record_uuid` & Absolute Column Offsets (`_bejson_core_abs_col`)\\nEvery row in `Values` carries a non-null, unique `record_uuid` at a fixed offset:\\n* **In 105 and 105a**: Column 0 is reserved for `record_uuid`. The row layout is `[record_uuid, *Fields]`. Row length is `len(Fields) + 1`. Field `Fields[i]` lives at column `i + 1`.\\n* **In 105db**: Column 0 is `Record_Type_Parent`. Column 1 is reserved for `record_uuid`. Entity fields follow starting at column 2: `[Record_Type_Parent, record_uuid, *Fields[1:]]`.\\n\\n| Format Series | Field Index `0` (`Record_Type_Parent` or 1st field) | Field Index `i >= 1` | Row Width |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **104 / 104a** | Column `0` | Column `i` | `len(Fields)` |\\n| **105 / 105a** | Column `1` (Column `0` is `record_uuid`) | Column `i + 1` | `len(Fields) + 1` |\\n| **104db** | Column `0` (`Record_Type_Parent`) | Column `i` | `len(Fields)` |\\n| **105db** | Column `0` (`Record_Type_Parent`) | Column `i + 1` (Column `1` is `record_uuid`) | `len(Fields) + 1` |\\n\\n### 3. Fail-on-Switch Constraint\\nMerely bumping `Format_Version` to `\\\"105\\\"` in the header without compiling `field_uuid` on fields and prepending `record_uuid` on rows causes an immediate hard validation error:\\n> `[E_INVALID_FIELDS] Format_Version claims 105-series but Fields were never compiled with field_uuid keys -- run bejson_core_upgrade_to_105() before using this file`\\n\\n### BEJSON 105 Example\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"SensorReading\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"sensor_id\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"f105-001-aaaa\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"temperature_celsius\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"integer\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"f105-002-bbbb\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"tags\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"f105-003-cccc\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"rec-uuid-101\\\", \\\"S001\\\", 24, [\\\"indoor\\\", \\\"ground\\\"]],\\n    [\\\"rec-uuid-102\\\", \\\"S002\\\", 20, null]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### BEJSON 105a Example\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105a\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Server_ID\\\": \\\"WEB-01\\\",\\n  \\\"Environment\\\": \\\"Production\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"ConfigParam\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"key\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"f105a-001-aaaa\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"value\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"f105a-002-bbbb\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"sensitive\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"boolean\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"f105a-003-cccc\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"rec-uuid-cfg1\\\", \\\"db_host\\\", \\\"prod-db-01\\\", true],\\n    [\\\"rec-uuid-cfg2\\\", \\\"max_threads\\\", \\\"32\\\", false]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### BEJSON 105db Example\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105db\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"User\\\", \\\"Item\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"f105db-000-rtp\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"username\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"User\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"f105db-001-usr\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"item_title\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Item\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"f105db-002-itm\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"owner_user_id_fk\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Item\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"f105db-003-fku\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"User\\\", \\\"rec-u-001\\\", \\\"alice\\\", null, null],\\n    [\\\"User\\\", \\\"rec-u-002\\\", \\\"bob\\\",   null, null],\\n    [\\\"Item\\\", \\\"rec-i-101\\\", null, \\\"Report A\\\", \\\"rec-u-001\\\"]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## BEJSON Core Nesting (Nested 104)\\n\\nBEJSON supports deeply nested, validated tabular structures directly within cells via the `Core_Nesting` library (Error codes 130\\u2013159).\\n* Embedded 104 Documents: A cell within a document contains a complete, valid serialized BEJSON 104 document.\\n* Column-Schema Uniformity (Hard Rule): Every nested document in the same parent column must share identical `Fields` (names, types, order). Violations raise hard error `E134` (`E_NESTING_SCHEMA_MISMATCH`).\\n* Implicit Foreign Key: The cell address `NestAddress(parent_fp, row, col, depth)` serves as the implicit foreign key.\\n* Safety Ceiling: Maximum recursive depth is capped at 16 (`NESTING_MAX_DEPTH`).\\n* Circular Reference Protection: Tracks seen fingerprints; recursion loops trigger `E135` (`E_NESTING_CIRCULAR_REF`).\\n* Dotted-Path Navigation: Traversed via `bejson_nesting_query` (e.g., `player_inventory[*].item_id`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n# PART 2: MFDB Crash Course (Multi-File Database)\\n\\nMFDB is an orchestration architecture built on top of BEJSON files. It is not a separate format version.\\n\\n```text\\nmydb/\\n  104a.mfdb.bejson        \\u2190 Master Manifest (104a or 105a)\\n  data/\\n    customers.bejson      \\u2190 Entity File (104 or 105)\\n    orders.bejson         \\u2190 Entity File (104 or 105)\\n```\\n\\n* **The Manifest (`104a.mfdb.bejson` or `105a.mfdb.bejson`)**: Registers every entity file, its relative file path, and its record count. `Records_Type: [\\\"mfdb\\\"]`.\\n* **The Entity Files (`data/<entity>.bejson`)**: Hold dense records for one entity type. Each entity file carries a mandatory **`Parent_Hierarchy`** key pointing back to the manifest.\\n\\n### MFDB 105 Referential Integrity & Cascading Deletes (Phase 6)\\nIn `libraries_105`, MFDB introduces transactional cascading operations (`mfdb_core_delete_entity_record_105`):\\n* **Plan-then-Commit**: Inspects all foreign key references across all entity files before mutating data.\\n* **Byte-Identical Snapshot & Rollback**: If a cascading delete fails or an entity write encounters an error, the entire database rolls back to its pre-operation byte snapshot.\\n* **Resilient PID Locking**: Employs `ResilientPIDLock` with atomic tombstone-renames and token-verified releases to guarantee locking integrity under process crash storms.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# PART 3: Core Library Families & The 105 Engine\\n\\nThe ecosystem provides four parallel language runtimes (`Lib_PY`, `Lib_JS`, `Lib_TS`, `Lib_SH`).\\n\\n### 1. The Canonical 105 Upgrade Utility (`lib_bejson_Core_bejson_upgrade.py`)\\nTo migrate legacy 104 files to 105 safely:\\n```python\\nfrom lib_bejson_Core_bejson_upgrade import bejson_core_upgrade_to_105\\n\\ndoc_data = json.load(open(\\\"data/users.104.bejson\\\"))\\n# Performs in-memory upgrade, validates strict integrity,\\n# writes backup to Backups/users.104.bak, and atomically replaces target file:\\nupgraded_doc = bejson_core_upgrade_to_105(doc_data, \\\"data/users.104.bejson\\\")\\n```\\n\\n### 2. Self-Maintaining Field Map Cache\\nIn 105, `_bejson_get_field_map_cache(doc)` inspects a lightweight schema fingerprint on every read:\\n$$\\\\text{fingerprint} = (\\\\text{Format\\\\_Version}, \\\\text{field\\\\_names}, \\\\text{field\\\\_uuids})$$\\nIf stale or uninitialized, it automatically rebuilds in memory (`by_name`, `by_uuid`, `by_name_row`).\\n\\n### 3. Batched Transactions (`bejson_core_transaction`)\\n```python\\nfrom lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core import bejson_core_transaction, bejson_core_add_record_105\\n\\nwith bejson_core_transaction(doc):\\n    for row in csv_records:\\n        bejson_core_add_record_105(doc, row)\\n# Field map cache rebuild is deferred and executed once at block exit\\n```\\n\\n### 4. Hybrid Index Resolver (`bejson_core_resolve_field_index`)\\nResolves column positions by field name, `field_uuid`, or a double-check dictionary, eliminating reliance on hardcoded column numbers.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Validation Error Code Ranges\\n\\n```text\\n1\\u201316    BEJSON Validator errors (BEJSONValidationError)\\n        1: E_INVALID_JSON\\n        2: E_MISSING_MANDATORY_KEY\\n        3: E_INVALID_FORMAT\\n        4: E_INVALID_VERSION\\n        5: E_INVALID_RECORDS_TYPE\\n        6: E_INVALID_FIELDS (missing field_uuid in 105 / bad type)\\n        7: E_INVALID_VALUES (missing record_uuid in 105)\\n        8: E_TYPE_MISMATCH\\n        9: E_RECORD_LENGTH_MISMATCH\\n        10: E_RESERVED_KEY_COLLISION\\n        11: E_INVALID_RECORD_TYPE_PARENT\\n        16: E_INVALID_FORMAT_CREATOR\\n20\\u201327   BEJSON Core errors (BEJSONCoreError)\\n30\\u201349   MFDB Validator errors (MFDBValidationError)\\n43\\u201347   MFDB Referential Integrity errors\\n80\\u201385   Format 105 Integrity Era errors\\n110\\u2013114 Format 105 Schema Constraint errors\\n130\\u2013159 Core_Nesting errors (E_NESTING_*)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n*Authored by Elton Boehnen \\u00b7 Master Reference Aligned with `libraries_105` Release.*\\n\\n\\n### ABOUT.md\\nBEJSON (Boehnen Elton JSON) is a text based data architecture with three variations and an orchestration layer called MFDB (Multi-file Database) that sits on top of it to form a relational database with no backend server.\\n\\nAlthough BEJSON is valid JSON is not neccessarily valid BEJSON.\\n\\nBEJSON is gatekept by a ruthless validator, only schemas that pass validation ever are allowed to access data. \\n\\nBEJSON 104 can be written by hand but it is very tedius, the libraries are the reccommended way to use BEJSON. If you can instruct an LLM to write BEJSON and validate it afterwards It passes validation mathematically guaranteed to see AI is not hallucinating. \\n\\nLLM's are at the core prediction machines they predict the next token or the next word based on past experiences. They actually thrive on predictable structured data, 90% of the hallucinations that AI experiences I'm willing to that has to do with the fact that standard JSON is unpredictable, all the nested bracketed unpredictable objects is the biggest cause of And I hallucinations when you feed a structured data format like BEJSON, You say massive amount of tokens and you render AI hallucinations nearly extinct. \\n\\nwith BEJSON AI doesn't have to guess The AI reads the fields which define the schema right at the top of the file everything from there is exactly where it's supposed to be where AI knows It where it will be. \\n\\nI separating the keys from the values themselves and the finding them at this top of the file do also save massive amounts of memory, you improve the context window bubble significantly, you destroy AI hallucinations, You access data and your memory access means no matter how big your file is you read and parset at the at the same exact speed due to positional lookups rather than key parsing.\\n\\n\\nBEJSON sacrifices a small amount of freedom and a small amount of human rehability (it's still readable) but you gain a AI native relational database with 0(1) access, massive games and AI comprehension and token production new render AI hallucinations of thing of the past you save file size you You haven't AI readable native relational database with no server and no backend called the multifile database which is a orchestration layer that sits on top of BEJSON. You own your data, or databases essentially text violet can you help with no pattern read no black box SQL server and requires queries your AI simply looks at the file and will understand the entire relational database immediately. You can chunk an entire drive using MFDB132 an entire project more entire project lifecycle and all of its versions into a single and MFDB 132 file, since the LLM doesn't have to open 50 different files in immediately gains understanding of the entire code base. \\n\\nYou can even use BEJSON as a local firebase cache system that produces your Firebase cost up to 97%.\\n\\nIf you're using LLM's Then you are significantly cheating yourself by not switching to using BEJSON over standard JSON\\n\\nGains from BEJSON go on and on and on. \\n\\n### ABOUT.txt\\n# REQUIREMENTS AND CONSTRAINTS:\\n\\nWe are going to be working on a big important job.\\nIt's very important that you comply with the following:\\n\\n- You do not move to the next TASK or SECTION without my confirmation of completion, and approval of your work. \\n\\n- Between each TASK (not section), and after my confirmation to move forward, you will state my terms back to me and your agreement to follow them.\\n\\n- All output files will be in or in subdirectories of:\\n~/105docs\\n \\n\\n please review the plan do not do anything just summarize of what you read and then stand by. You will be completing this job under the persona Leethaxor69. After confirmation to move on, you will study relevant content and context before starting.\\n\\n\\n# BEJSON and MFDB\\n\\n# Task 1\\n\\n## Goal\\nI want to start the readme on my BEJSON_105 repo, coming out the gate detailing the exact capabilities of my libraries. They will sound unbelievable. But below, we will describe each feature, with explanation of how and why, with schema and code examples.\\n\\nWe will make it hit hard, claiming to unlock AI super powers  which it does...\\n\\n## Instructions\\nHelp me expand the list. First study my projects, transcripts and the 105 libraries, adding to the list as you study. \\n/storage/emulated/0/Admin/Libraries/libraries_105/\\n/storage/emulated/0/Admin/Dev/Repos/\\n/storage/emulated/0/Admin/Processing/Autobase64/ebook/transcripts/\\n\\n\\nHere's the Features and Capabilities I can think of so far.\\n\\n1. Be an AI Native relational database architecture.\\n2. Be human readable, not a binary blob.\\n3. Solve the fundamental cause of AI hallucinations.\\n4. Reduce token usages for small datasets at 60 percent reduction with savings exponentially growing the larger the data set.\\n5. Reduce firebase and cloud costs as much as 97 percent.\\n6. Be 80x faster than SQL.\\n7. Operate on millions of records under 50mb\\n8. Run offline, with no server or daemon, with any app or script that your AI can natively interact with in a way that it essentially is part of the AI.\\n8. Exist in Python, Bash, Typescript and Javascript with byte for byte identical output and shared error codes. \\n\\nFile: Expanded_List.md\\n\\nPlease produce an expanded list, feel free to adjust formatting we're working. \\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Task 2\\nAfter finishing the extensive list and confirming my satisfaction, help me write some short and snappy post templates to challenge users to verify my claims, they will go right after my claims in the readme. Check brainstorming notes below for examples of what I want  but they must be as compact as possible at least some must fit in a tweet for promoting.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Task 3\\n Develop an outline for my readme that serves as my repos front page document.\\n \\nThis is the general outline I am looking for. we will create it into separate documents and merge the two documents to form the readme at the end. We will produce one section at a time. You must confirm I'm ready to move on to the next section before moving on, \\n \\n **Part 1 / Document 1 Sections:**\\nMaintain few technical details, introduced the reader to the concepts. \\n \\n [Title]\\n [TOC]\\n [Capability Claims]\\n  - List all the capabilities , no explanation, frame it as a list of AI Super Powers\\n [Verification Templates]\\n  - Prompt templates to verify claims. \\n [Architecture Summary Overview]\\n - A brief summary of the legacy 104 era and the current 105 era. A short and sweet introduction to the versions, each of their 3 format versions and the MFDB architecture that sits on top, define it as an orchistration layer of the formats  not a format itself. Should be concise with few technical details.\\n [Positional Access Field Seperation]\\n  - A brief explanation of the fields, how they are separated from the values, define the terms (fields, values  records as rows which hold sets of values). Define Record Types, 104 and 104a being single typed and 104db being multi typed but meant for small data sets, Clarify that it is not MFDB, . Clarify that the purpose of all of this is for AI readability, token,memory and context optimizations. Detail that MFDB orchistrates the formats to managing them as a relational database that enforces cascade referential integrity. Maintain few technical terms in this section , just define things and briefly explain each\\n  \\n  **Part 2 / Document 2 Sections**\\n [BEJSON] \\n \\nDetailed subsections of each of the 3 formats. Explain the universal rules and format specific rules, the purpose of the format, its suggested use cases. DO NOT DETAIL EVERY VALIDATION RULE THIS IS NOT THE PLACE, JUST DEFINE ITS STRUCTURE... PROVIDE SCHDMA EXAMPLES.\\n [MFDB]\\nExplain MFDB, how it orchistrates 104 and 104a files, the bi directial integrity, the Network master and slave roles the context window benefits, the way it accesses only what it needs. It's usage of _fk, manifest cordination. DO NOT DETAIL EVERY VALIDATION RULE THIS IS NOT THE PLACE, JUST DEFINE ITS STRUCTURE... PROVIDE SCHDMA EXAMPLES. \\n [Field Map Cache]\\n Explain what index based lookups are, why they fail and our solution.\\n [UUID Addressing] \\n Explain how we do not reference by field name or row/col position but by Zuuid and why \\n [Crash Course Rules]\\n Here we define every validation rule.\\n \\n PART 3 TO BE CONTINUED\\n [Libraries and Core Families]\\n\\n \\n # BRAINSTORMING NOTES\\n\\n---\\n## Verify Claims Now\\nSimply give my repository URL to Claude, Gemini or any other LLM, copy the prompt below to receive AI super powers . No catch. \\n\\nGIVE THIS TO YOUR LLM, UNLOCK SUPER POWERS AFTER VERIFYING CLAIMS... PROMPT:\\nCan you verify the following BEJSON_105 repository, it claims to:\\n1. Be an AI Native relational database architecture.\\n2. Be human readable, not a binary blob.\\n3. Solve the fundamental cause of AI hallucinations.\\n4. Reduce token usages for small datasets at 60 percent reduction with savings exponentially growing the larger the data set.\\n5. Reduce firebase and cloud costs as much as 97 percent.\\n6. Be 80x faster than SQL.\\n7. Operate on millions of records under 50mb\\n8. Run offline, with no server or daemon, with any app or script that your AI can natively interact with in a way that it essentially is part of the AI.\\n8. Exist in Python, Bash, Typescript and Javascript with byte for byte identical output and shared error codes. \\n\\nONCE YOUR LLM VERIFIES THE CLAIMS: Tell him to clone the repository and create you a test script such as a note editor with categories and tags, or whatever you want. Tell the LLM you want your app backed by a Multi File Database, in either Bash, Python, Typescript or JavaScript. The LLM will look at it and immediately understand what to do.\\n\\nHere are some more prompts if you are not convinced\\n\\n\\nSkeptical? Use this prompt: \\n---\\nThe future of database architecture? This repository below makes lots of claims that don't seem possible , please tell me exactly what this repository is and explain to me exactly how it works. Is there a catch?\\nWhat is BEJSON, and what is a Multi File Database, please look at the repository files themselves. Is it safe? \\nhttps://gitHub.com/boehnenelton/BEJSON_105\\n---\\n\\n---\\nBEJSON claims to be the future of data ase architecture and 80x faster than SQL, offline with no server. \\nHow is that even possible? It says it uses positional based lookups with memory access speeds on data sets with upward of a million records and no loss of speed and remaining under 50mb of ram . \\n\\nCan you verify the claims made by https://github.com/boehnenelton/BEJSON_105\\n---\\n\\nWant to know how it is 80x faster than SQL?\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nand it's claims of being an offline  serverless, relational database built off JSON that is upwards of 80x faster than SQL, with memory access speeds with claims a massive 65% token  and memory reduction that can manage 100,000 tables using under 50mb  on any edge device and can be read in notepad? What is the catch?\\n\\n\\n\\nBEJSON exists in two active versions and 3 formats each with their own use case purposes along with MFDB, which sits on top of them acting as the orchistration layer that is the Multi-File Database. \\n\\nThe system is 2 years in  the making, and has been rigorously tested and verified. The true power is that the system is by design written for AI, you can verify all of my claims in 30 seconds and begin utilizing advanced AI native databases offline with no server. Your LLM will immediately understands the entire scope of your data and relationships and safely be able to modify them. This architecture solves the fundamental cause of AI hallucinations making them virtually extinct with an exponentially token savings reduction as your data set grows in comparison to standard JSON.\\n\\n\\n\\nIf true explain how it's possible in elaborate detail.\\n\\n\\nhttps://gitHub.com/boehnenelton/BEJSON_105\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n### AGENTS.MD\\n# AGENTS.md \\u2014 libraries_105\\n\\n**Name:** AGENTS.md (libraries_105)  \\n**Description:** Standing instructions for AI agents working in or against the BEJSON 105 / MFDB v1.40 library repository. Place at the repository root. Full detail: `Libraries_105_Agent_Directive-V10101-PKG101.md` (repository root).  \\n**Version:** `VERSION = 10101`  \\n**Date Created:** 2026-11-08  \\n**Author:** Elton Boehnen \\u00b7 boehnenelton2024@gmail.com \\u00b7 boehnenelton2024.pages.dev \\u00b7 github.com/boehnenelton  \\n**RELATIONAL_ID:** `b6d36c67-e789-4c81-9b2b-aa748e76ce34`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## What this repo is\\nFour-language BEJSON Integrity Era runtime: `Lib_PY` (Python \\u2265 3.10), `Lib_JS` (Node \\u2265 18), `Lib_TS` (TS \\u2265 5), `Lib_SH` (Bash \\u2265 4.4 + `jq`). Zero third-party runtime dependencies. Formats: BEJSON `104`, `104a`, `104db`, `105`, `105a`, `105db`; MFDB v1.40 (one `104a.mfdb.bejson` manifest + entity files).\\n\\n## Two audiences \\u2014 pick yours\\n1. **You are consuming the libraries** (building an app on them): read \\u00a73 onward. Never modify this repo.\\n2. **You are changing the libraries themselves**: only when the user explicitly asks. Core is frozen by default; follow \\u00a77.\\n\\n## 1. Trust order for facts\\n1. Library source files. 2. This file. 3. `Libraries_105_Agent_Directive-V10101-PKG101.md`. 4. `clarification.md`. 5. `README.md` (**contains wrong examples**, see \\u00a72).\\n\\n## 2. Known-wrong docs \\u2014 do not copy\\n- `README.md` Python quickstart imports `lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core_105` and `bejson_core_105_*` functions: **they do not exist in Python.**\\n- `README.md` JS quickstart calls `bejson_core_parse_bejson`: **does not exist.** Use `JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(...))`.\\n- \\\"E90\\u2013E95\\\" structural errors and `105a.mfdb.bejson`: **wrong.** Codes 90\\u2013114 were removed; the manifest is always `104a.mfdb.bejson`.\\n- \\\"Exact parity across all four languages\\\": **false.** See \\u00a75.\\n\\n## 3. Setup\\n```bash\\nexport ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105=\\\"/path/to/libraries_105\\\"\\n```\\nPython modules are flat files in family folders; add each family folder you use to `sys.path`:\\n```python\\nfor family in (\\\"Core\\\", \\\"Standards\\\", \\\"Taxonomies\\\", \\\"Tax_Scaffolds\\\"):\\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(os.environ[\\\"ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105\\\"]) / \\\"Lib_PY\\\" / family))\\n```\\nJS: `require(`${process.env.ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105}/Lib_JS/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core.js`)`. Bash: `source \\u2026/Lib_SH/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core.sh` (from a non-subshell). Never hard-code paths.\\n\\n## 4. Always use the library \\u2014 never reimplement\\n| Job | Python | JS | TS | Bash |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Stale-safe file lock | `ResilientPIDLock(path, timeout_seconds)` | none | none | `resilient_lock_acquire/release` (basic) |\\n| Atomic write | `bejson_core_atomic_write` | temp+rename yourself | temp+rename yourself | `bejson_core_atomic_write` |\\n| Field lookup | `bejson_core_get_field_map(doc)[name]` | same | same | same |\\n| Add field (stamps `field_uuid`) | `bejson_core_define_field` | `bejson_core_add_field` | `defineField` | \\u2014 |\\n| Add row | `bejson_core_add_record_105(doc, values)` | same | `addRecord105` | `mfdb_core_add_entity_record_105` |\\n| Bulk adds | `with bejson_core_transaction(doc):` | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n| Verify 105 | `bejson_core_verify_105_integrity` | same | `verify105Integrity` | \\u2014 |\\n| Full validate | `validate_bejson` | `validate_bejson` | `validateDocument` | `bejson_validator_validate_file` |\\n| 104\\u2192105 | `bejson_core_upgrade_to_105` / `_dry_run` | \\u2014 | `upgradeTo105` (memory only) | \\u2014 |\\n| MFDB CRUD (105) | `mfdb_core_add/update/delete_entity_record_105` | same | none | same |\\n| Logging | Standards `BEJSONLogHandler` | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n| Env / paths | `source_env`, `get_env_path`, `bejson_safe_join` | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | `bec_core_source_env` |\\n\\nIf a task lands on `\\u2014`/`none`, tell the user and state the fallback. Do not fake it.\\n\\n## 5. Language gaps to state plainly\\n- **JS/TS have no PID lock** \\u2192 single-writer only. Route multi-writer work through Python.\\n- **Bash lock is basic** (no owner token) and needs `jq`.\\n- **TS has no MFDB entity-record CRUD**; **JS lacks `define_field`**; **JS/TS/Bash lack the 104\\u2192105 atomic upgrade tool**.\\n- `bejson_core_atomic_write` (Python) does not fsync the directory.\\n\\n## 6. Hard rules for consumers\\n1. No positional indices; use the field map.\\n2. `record_uuid` is **implicit** in 105 rows. Never declare it in `Fields`; never pass it to `add_record_105`.\\n3. Never hand-write `field_uuid`.\\n4. No raw `open(..., \\\"w\\\")` on BEJSON files. Lock, then read-modify-write, then atomic write, all inside the lock.\\n5. Do not wrap `mfdb_core_*` calls in your own lock on the same entity file (they lock internally).\\n6. Manifest is `104a.mfdb.bejson`; entities are Format `105`; pass `mfdb_version=\\\"1.40\\\"` (default is `\\\"1.31\\\"`).\\n7. Enable FK enforcement explicitly: `mfdb_core_enable_ref_integrity`. FK fields declare `fk_target_entity` and `fk_on_delete` (`restrict|cascade|null`).\\n8. Python logging via `logging` + Standards handler; no `print()`.\\n9. Pick error codes from free ranges only: **free = 190\\u2013199, 390\\u2013419, 520+** (520\\u2013579 reserved for Agent Core). Owned ranges: Core 1\\u201399, Nesting 130\\u2013159, Extension 160\\u2013189, AI 200\\u2013249, Standards 250\\u2013269, Cognition 270\\u2013289, BEHTML 300\\u2013359, Terminal_Menus 360\\u2013389, Diagrams 420\\u2013449, Bookmaker 450\\u2013479, Lists 480\\u2013499, Graphics 500\\u2013519.\\n10. Env paths come from config/secure stores. Deprecated vars (`ADMIN_ROOT`, `BEJSON_STORAGE_ROOT`, `BEJSON_LIB_ROOT`) must not appear in new config.\\n\\n## 7. If you are modifying this repo (only when asked)\\n- Core family is frozen; do not edit it without explicit permission. Apps own their internal libraries.\\n- Keep cross-language parity where the matrix says \\u2705; document any gap you create or close in `dev/change-log.md`.\\n- Every changed file: bump its `VERSION`, new `RELATIONAL_ID`, header with Name/Description/Date/Author. Surgical edits only; match existing style.\\n- Add or update a test in the family's `tests/` folder for every behavior change. Log adversarial findings in `dev/security-notes.md`.\\n- Do not claim a README example works until you have run it.\\n\\n## 8. Test commands\\n```bash\\npython3 Lib_PY/Core/tests/test_mfdb_spec_levels_suite.py     # 30 tests\\npython3 Lib_PY/Standards/tests/test_standards.py\\npython3 Lib_PY/Taxonomies/tests/test_taxonomies.py\\nnode    Lib_JS/Core/tests/test_mfdb_spec_levels_suite.js\\nnode    Lib_JS/Core/tests/test_phase6_ref_integrity.js\\nnpx tsc -p Lib_TS/tsconfig.json --noEmit\\nbash    Lib_SH/Core/tests/test_mfdb_spec_levels.sh\\n# added 2026-11-12\\npython3 Lib_PY/CMS/tests/test_cms_admin_security.py\\npython3 Lib_PY/Core/cli/CLI_Doc_Chunker/tests/test_flask_hardening.py   # needs flask; stubs lib_bejson_utility\\n```\\nTypeScript tests are compiled, not just type-checked: `cd Lib_TS/Core && tsc --target es2020 --module commonjs --moduleResolution node10 --skipLibCheck --strict --types node --outDir /tmp/ts_out index.ts tests/*.ts` (needs `@types/node`), then run each `/tmp/ts_out/tests/*.js` from inside `/tmp/ts_out/tests`.\\n\\nRun the relevant suite before reporting a change complete. Last full run (2026-11-12, V5.9.5-PKG118, Python 3.13 / Node 22 / Bash 5 + jq / tsc 5): **50 of 50 test entry points exit 0** (Python 19, JS 10, Bash 9, TS strict compile + 9 compiled test files).\\n\\n## 9. Gotchas\\n- `mfdb_core_add_entity_record_105`: **Python returns the entity doc** (new uuid = `doc[\\\"Values\\\"][-1][0]`); **JS returns `{ record_uuid, doc }`**. Do not assume the Python shape in JS.\\n- `mfdb_core_load_entity` returns dicts; document-level functions use lists. Don't mix indexing styles.\\n- Validators return different shapes: result objects (`validate_*`, `.valid`), lists (`*_deep_verify`, `*_check_referential_integrity`, `*_check_105_*`; `[]` = clean), dict (`detect_schema_drift`; `{}` = none).\\n- `_bejson_field_map` is an in-memory cache; never persist or trust one from disk.\\n- Custom headers are allowed in `104a`/`105a`; `_`-prefixed header keys are reserved.\\n\\n## 10. Before you finish\\nRun the checklist in `Libraries_105_Agent_Directive-V10101-PKG101.md` \\u00a711. Report any language gap or doc discrepancy you hit.\\n\\n## 11. Governance & Self-Tracking Ledger Invariants (Added 2026-11-17)\\n- **Self-Tracking MFDB Ledger Synchronization:** Every package bump, new family registration, error code addition, or version anchor update MUST atomically update the `BEJSON/` self-tracking MFDB (`104a.mfdb.bejson`, `data/errors.bejson`, `data/families.bejson`, `data/metadata.bejson`, `data/library_changes.bejson`) and verify `validate_mfdb_database('BEJSON/104a.mfdb.bejson').valid == True`. Never ship a package with a stale `BEJSON/` ledger or duplicate EAV keys in `metadata.bejson`.\\n- **Cross-Language Core Error & Null Parity:** Core error codes (1\\u2013139) and CRUD exception numbers (such as `57 E_MFDB_CORE_DUPLICATE_ENTITY_NAME`) must remain identical across PY, JS, TS, and SH. Null values in typed cells must pass through `_coerceValue` as `null` without throwing `NaN` / `WRITE_TYPE_MISMATCH`.\\n- **Graphics SVG Palette Enforcement:** `svg_markup` cells in Graphics 105/105a documents must be scanned for embedded hex colors against `AUTHORIZED_PALETTE_HEX` (Policy \\u00a78.1 Tri-Color palette).\\n\\n*Elton Boehnen \\u00b7 boehnenelton2024@gmail.com \\u00b7 boehnenelton2024.pages.dev \\u00b7 github.com/boehnenelton*\\n\\n## Relocated from ~/.gemini/GEMINI.md (2026-11-17)\\n\\n- **Test Schema Synchronization:** When changing canonical seed registries, defaults, or schemas in BEJSON/MFDB stores, update all test assertions and fixture baselines in the same atomic commit. Tests must not tolerate known assertion drift or magic numbers that diverge from the canonical seed. Skill: `bejson-enforcement-suite-builder`.\\n- **Metrics Destination:** Benchmarks, test suites, performance metrics, and dashboards for this library resolve their target from `$DEFINITIONS_FILE` (`$ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105`) at `<library root>/dev/metrics/`. No scratch folders outside the definitions tree for canonical deliveries. Updating metrics is an atomic in-place replacement: purge stale unindexed files and keep the series layout `test_series_1`, `test_series_2`, `test_series_3`, `index.html`. Skills: `file-hierarchy-manager`, `bejson-enforcement-suite-builder`.\\n\\n\\n### BEJSON_Crash_Course_105-V3.md\\n# BEJSON + MFDB Crash Course \\u2014 105 Edition\\n\\n**Document Version:** 105-V1 (written against the parity-patched Core) \\u00b7 **BEJSON Formats:** 105 \\u00b7 105a \\u00b7 105db (104-series carried over) \\u00b7 **MFDB Spec:** 1.40 (Ref_Integrity) \\u00b7 **Chunk Packaging:** 1.32\\n**Format Creator:** Elton Boehnen \\u00b7 [https://boehnenelton2024.pages.dev](https://boehnenelton2024.pages.dev)\\n**Date:** 2026-10-24\\n\\n> **How this document was made.** Every rule below was checked against the real `libraries_105` Python Core (`Lib_PY/Core`) by running it, not by copying the older 104-era course. The first pass found that 105 skipped many checks 104 performs. The Core was then patched (the **105\\u2194104 parity patch**: validator 3.4.0, core 3.6.0, upgrade 1.1.0, mfdb_core 3.1.0, mfdb_validator 2.4.0) and this document describes the **patched** behaviour. The bottom section, **Part 4: Reality Audit**, records what was wrong, what the patch fixed, and what is still open.\\n>\\n> **Scope.** Python is the reference implementation described in Parts 1\\u20133. As of this edition JavaScript, TypeScript and Shell Core (both `libraries_104` and `libraries_105`) enforce the same validation rules; Part 4.7 lists what each language does and does not cover. The `Taxonomies` and `Tax_Scaffolds` families are out of scope.\\n\\n**Legend**\\n\\n| Mark | Meaning |\\n| :---: | :--- |\\n| \\u2705 | Enforced by the library code (a validator or a write function rejects the violation) |\\n| \\u26a0\\ufe0f | Convention only (the code does not stop you) |\\n| \\u274c | Documented or expected, but the code does not check it |\\n\\n**Versions verified:** `bejson_core` 3.6.0 \\u00b7 `bejson_validator` 3.4.0 \\u00b7 `bejson_upgrade` 1.1.0 \\u00b7 `mfdb_core` 3.1.0 \\u00b7 `mfdb_validator` 2.4.0 \\u00b7 `bejson_chunking` 1.9.1 \\u00b7 `bejson_core_nesting` 0.6.0 \\u00b7 `bejson_errors` 2.9.0. All Core suites pass (91 + 36 + 9 + 29 + 9 + 8 + 14 + 23 + 7 shipped, plus 30 parity tests in `tests/test_105_104_parity_suite.py` and 24 MFDB spec-level tests in `tests/test_mfdb_spec_levels_suite.py`, run for both 104 and 105 databases).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PART 1: BEJSON 105 CRASH COURSE\\n**(105, 105a, 105db)**\\n\\nBEJSON 105 is BEJSON 104 plus **identity**. The data layout is still positional (`Fields` order = `Values` order), but now every column and every row carries a UUID:\\n\\n| 104-series | 105-series adds |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| Column = `{name, type}` | Column also has a **`field_uuid`** (UUIDv4). It survives renames and reordering. |\\n| Row = one value per field | Row also has a **`record_uuid`** (UUIDv4) in a **reserved leading column** that `Fields` does not describe. |\\n| Find a column by name or position | Find a column by name **or** by `field_uuid`; write a cell by `(record_uuid, field_uuid)`. |\\n| Foreign keys are only a naming convention | A field can **declare** a foreign key (`fk_target_entity`, `fk_on_delete`) that MFDB enforces. |\\n\\nEverything else (six mandatory keys, `Format_Creator`, the six types, `null` rules, unique field names) is the same as 104.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1.1 Mandatory keys\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"SensorReading\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [ ... ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [ ... ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n* `Format_Version` is one of `\\\"105\\\"`, `\\\"105a\\\"`, `\\\"105db\\\"` (the validator also still accepts the three 104 values). \\u2705\\n* `Format_Creator` must be exactly `\\\"Elton Boehnen\\\"`. \\u2705\\n* `Parent_Hierarchy` is the only built-in extra key allowed on `105`/`105db` (used by MFDB entity files). \\u2705\\n* Any other top-level key starting with `_` is rejected (reserved for in-memory runtime data such as the field-map cache). \\u2705\\n\\n### 1.2 The Field object\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"name\\\": \\\"reading_c\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"number\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0003-4000-8000-000000000003\\\"}\\n```\\n\\n* `name` (string) and `type` are required. \\u2705 Names must be unique within the document. \\u2705\\n* `type` must be exactly one of **`string`, `integer`, `number`, `boolean`, `array`, `object`** (lowercase). \\u2705 There is no `timestamp`, `date` or `uuid` type: store those as `string` (ISO-8601 for time) and express the meaning in the field name, for example `created_timestamp`.\\n* `field_uuid` must be present on every field and **unique** in the document. \\u2705\\n* Optional foreign-key metadata (Part 2.4): `fk_target_entity`, `fk_on_delete` (`restrict` \\u00b7 `cascade` \\u00b7 `null`).\\n* In `105db` every field except `Record_Type_Parent` also carries `\\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"<EntityName>\\\"`. \\u2705 (the validator rejects a field with no owner).\\n* Add new fields **only at the end**. Core's `define_field`/`add_field` do this and pad every existing row with `null`.\\n\\n### 1.3 Row layout and offsets (the one thing you must know)\\n\\nA `105`/`105a` row has **one more cell than `Fields`**: `record_uuid` is column 0 and every field shifts right by one. A `105db` row keeps the discriminator in column 0 and puts `record_uuid` in column 1.\\n\\n| Format | Row layout | Row length | `Fields[i]` is at row column |\\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\\n| 104 / 104a / 104db | `[v0, v1, v2, ...]` | `len(Fields)` | `i` |\\n| **105 / 105a** | `[record_uuid, v0, v1, v2, ...]` | `len(Fields)+1` | `i + 1` |\\n| **105db** | `[Record_Type_Parent, record_uuid, v1, v2, ...]` | `len(Fields)+1` | `0` for `i = 0`, otherwise `i + 1` |\\n\\nDo not do this arithmetic by hand. `bejson_core_get_field_map(doc)` returns **row positions** on every format version:\\n\\n```python\\nbejson_core_get_field_map(doc_104)   # {'sensor_id': 0, 'temp': 1}\\nbejson_core_get_field_map(doc_105)   # {'sensor_id': 1, 'temp': 2}   <- row[idx] is already correct\\n```\\n\\n`bejson_core_resolve_field_index(doc, target)` returns the absolute row column for a field given as a name, a `field_uuid`, or `{\\\"name\\\": ..., \\\"field_uuid\\\": ...}` (both must agree, otherwise `E_FIELD_INTEGRITY_MISMATCH`, code 80). It works on 105-series documents only (`E_FORMAT_UNSUPPORTED`, code 81, on a 104 document).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1.4 The three 105 formats\\n\\n#### BEJSON 105 \\u2014 single record type, all six types\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"SensorReading\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"sensor_id\\\",   \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0001-4000-8000-000000000001\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"recorded_at\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0002-4000-8000-000000000002\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"reading_c\\\",   \\\"type\\\": \\\"number\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0003-4000-8000-000000000003\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"tags\\\",        \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0004-4000-8000-000000000004\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"9f8e7d6c-0001-4000-8000-000000000001\\\", \\\"S001\\\", \\\"2026-10-24T12:00:00Z\\\", 23.5, [\\\"indoor\\\", \\\"ground\\\"]],\\n    [\\\"9f8e7d6c-0002-4000-8000-000000000002\\\", \\\"S002\\\", \\\"2026-10-24T12:00:00Z\\\", 19.8, null]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n* Custom top-level headers are forbidden (only `Parent_Hierarchy`). \\u2705\\n* Primary use: MFDB entity files, logs, any homogeneous table.\\n\\n#### BEJSON 105a \\u2014 metadata and configuration\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105a\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Server_ID\\\": \\\"WEB-01\\\",\\n  \\\"Environment\\\": \\\"Production\\\",\\n  \\\"Retention_Days\\\": 90,\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"ConfigParam\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"key\\\",       \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"c0000000-0001-4000-8000-000000000001\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"value\\\",     \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"c0000000-0002-4000-8000-000000000002\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"sensitive\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"boolean\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"c0000000-0003-4000-8000-000000000003\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"d0000000-0001-4000-8000-000000000001\\\", \\\"db_host\\\", \\\"prod-db-01\\\", true],\\n    [\\\"d0000000-0002-4000-8000-000000000002\\\", \\\"max_threads\\\", \\\"32\\\", false]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n* Custom top-level headers are allowed (file-level metadata only, `PascalCase`). \\u2705\\n* Primitive-only like 104a (`string`, `integer`, `number`, `boolean`): `array`/`object` fields are rejected. \\u2705\\n* **MFDB manifests stay `104a`.** A manifest is rejected if it is `105a` (Part 2.1).\\n\\n#### BEJSON 105db \\u2014 multi-entity in one file\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105db\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"Department\\\", \\\"Employee\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"d0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000000\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"dept_name\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Department\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"d0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"emp_name\\\",  \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"Record_Type_Parent\\\": \\\"Employee\\\",   \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"d0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"Department\\\", \\\"e1111111-0000-4000-8000-000000000001\\\", \\\"Engineering\\\", null],\\n    [\\\"Employee\\\",   \\\"e1111111-0000-4000-8000-000000000002\\\", null, \\\"Alice Vance\\\"]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n* Rules as 104db, plus the 105 layout: discriminator at column 0, `record_uuid` at column 1.\\n* The validator applies the 104db rules plus the 105 layout: first field is `Record_Type_Parent`, every other field names its owner, `Records_Type` has 2+ entries, each row's discriminator (column 0) is in `Records_Type`, and `record_uuid` sits at column 1. \\u2705\\n* `bejson_core_remove_field` refuses to remove `Record_Type_Parent` (`E_FIELD_INTEGRITY_MISMATCH`).\\n* Same size warning as 104db: padding makes the file grow quickly. For anything larger than a small single-file dataset, use MFDB (Part 2).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1.5 What `validate_bejson` actually enforces on 105-series\\n\\n| Check | 104-series | 105-series |\\n| :--- | :---: | :---: |\\n| Valid JSON, six mandatory keys, `Format_Creator` | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| `Format_Version` in the allowed list | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| Custom-header rules (104/104db/105/105db forbid; 104a/105a allow) | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| `_`-prefixed keys rejected; persisted `_bejson_field_map` rejected | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| `Fields` entries have `name` + `type`; type is one of the six | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| Field names unique | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| **`field_uuid` present and unique on every field** | n/a | \\u2705 |\\n| **`record_uuid` present (non-empty string) and unique, at the right column** | n/a | \\u2705 |\\n| Fail-on-Switch: a 104 file re-headered to 105 without UUIDs fails | n/a | \\u2705 |\\n| `field_uuid` / `record_uuid` are valid UUIDv4 strings | n/a | \\u26a0\\ufe0f warning only (`result.warnings`), never an error |\\n| **Row length** equals expected (`len(Fields)+1`) | \\u2705 (`E_RECORD_LENGTH_MISMATCH`, 9) | \\u2705 |\\n| **Value type** matches field type (offset-aware, bool excluded from integer/number) | \\u2705 (`E_TYPE_MISMATCH`, 8) | \\u2705 |\\n| `Records_Type` count (1 / 1 / 2+) | \\u2705 | \\u2705 (`105`, `105a`: exactly 1; `105db`: 2+) |\\n| 104a/105a primitives only | \\u2705 (104a) | \\u2705 (105a) |\\n| 104db/105db discriminator rules | \\u2705 (104db) | \\u2705 (105db) |\\n\\nFor 105-series the row/type findings are all reported (up to 100, then a \\\"suppressed\\\" note); 104 stops at the first. Row and type errors appear as `[E_RECORD_LENGTH_MISMATCH] ...` / `[E_TYPE_MISMATCH] ...` strings in `ValidationResult.errors`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1.6 Creating 105 data\\n\\nThere is **no** `bejson_core_create_105()`. The three supported ways are:\\n\\n1. **Upgrade an existing 104-series document** (Section 1.8).\\n2. **Create it inside an MFDB:** `mfdb_core_create_database(..., entities=[{\\\"name\\\": ..., \\\"fields\\\": [...], \\\"format_version\\\": \\\"105\\\"}])` stamps a fresh `field_uuid` on every field and writes an empty `Values` (Part 2).\\n3. **Build it by hand/script.** Generate every UUID with `uuid.uuid4()`; put `Format_Version` last, then validate.\\n\\n### 1.7 Working with a 105 document (Python)\\n\\n```python\\nfrom lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core import (\\n    bejson_core_load_file, bejson_core_atomic_write, bejson_core_get_field_map,\\n    bejson_core_define_field, bejson_core_add_record_105, bejson_core_write_cell,\\n    bejson_core_delete_record, bejson_core_remove_field, bejson_core_resolve_field_index,\\n    bejson_core_verify_105_integrity, bejson_core_transaction)\\n\\ndoc = bejson_core_load_file(\\\"sensors.bejson\\\")      # 105 docs come back with the field map already built\\nfm  = bejson_core_get_field_map(doc)               # {'sensor_id': 1, 'temp': 2}  (row positions)\\ntemp = doc[\\\"Values\\\"][0][fm[\\\"temp\\\"]]                # 23.5\\n\\nnote_uuid = bejson_core_define_field(doc, \\\"note\\\", \\\"string\\\")      # appends a column, pads every row with null, returns its field_uuid\\nrec_uuid  = bejson_core_add_record_105(doc, [\\\"S3\\\", 1.5, \\\"hi\\\"])   # values WITHOUT record_uuid, in Fields order; returns the new record_uuid\\nbejson_core_write_cell(doc, rec_uuid, note_uuid, \\\"updated\\\")       # address a cell by (record_uuid, field_uuid)\\nbejson_core_delete_record(doc, rec_uuid)                          # delete by record_uuid\\nbejson_core_remove_field(doc, \\\"note\\\")                              # remove by name or field_uuid; never by index\\n\\nwith bejson_core_transaction(doc):                 # one cache rebuild for the whole batch\\n    for row in csv_rows:\\n        bejson_core_add_record_105(doc, row)\\n\\nreport = bejson_core_verify_105_integrity(doc)     # read-only; {'ok': True, 'field_count': 3, 'record_count': 2, 'errors': []}\\nbejson_core_atomic_write(\\\"sensors.bejson\\\", doc)    # temp file + fsync + os.replace; runtime '_' keys are stripped\\n```\\n\\nWhat each call really guarantees:\\n\\n| Function | Guarantees | Does **not** guarantee |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| `define_field(doc, name, type, *, fk_target_entity, fk_on_delete)` | Type is one of the six; name is new; FK args valid; new `field_uuid`; rows padded | n/a (checks eagerly, writes nothing on failure) |\\n| `add_field(doc, name, type)` | Type must be one of the six; duplicate name rejected (`E_DUPLICATE_FIELD_NAME`, 84); new `field_uuid`; rows padded | n/a |\\n| `add_record_105(doc, values)` | `len(values) == len(Fields)` (code 9) and every value matches its type (code 8), checked **before** anything is written; for `105db` the discriminator must be in `Records_Type`; new `record_uuid` in the right column | n/a |\\n| `write_cell(doc, record_uuid, field_uuid, value)` | Finds the cell by UUIDs; value type checked (code 8); heals stale `expected_row/expected_col` hints | n/a |\\n| `remove_field` / `delete_record` | By UUID or name, never by index | Assumes the document is valid; run `validate_bejson` first on files from outside |\\n| `verify_105_integrity(doc)` | UUID checks **plus** row length and value types (same function the validator uses) | n/a |\\n\\nDo not mix in the 104 mutators on 105 data:\\n\\n* `bejson_core_add_record(doc, row)` is 104-only and now **raises** `E_FORMAT_UNSUPPORTED` (81) on a 105-series document (it used to append a row with no `record_uuid`). Use `add_record_105`.\\n* `bejson_core_update_field`, `filter_rows` and `sort_by_field` do work on 105 because they go through `get_field_index`, which returns row positions.\\n\\nSerialization: `bejson_core_serialize(doc)` and `atomic_write` drop every key beginning with `_` or `*`, including `_bejson_field_map`.\\n\\n### 1.8 Upgrading 104 \\u2192 105\\n\\n```python\\nfrom lib_bejson_Core_bejson_upgrade import bejson_core_upgrade_to_105, bejson_core_upgrade_dry_run\\n\\nreport = bejson_core_upgrade_dry_run(doc, \\\"sensors.bejson\\\")   # no disk writes\\n# {'field_count', 'record_count', 'validation_pass', 'errors', 'source_path', 'target_version'}\\n\\nnew_doc = bejson_core_upgrade_to_105(doc, \\\"sensors.bejson\\\")   # backs up, validates, swaps atomically\\n```\\n\\n* Mapping: `104 \\u2192 105`, `104a \\u2192 105a`, `104db \\u2192 105db`. Custom 104a headers are preserved.\\n* Every field gets a new `field_uuid`; every row gets a new `record_uuid` in the right column (column 0, or column 1 for `105db` after the discriminator).\\n* The original file is first copied byte-for-byte to `Backups/<filename>.104.bak` (a `Backups/` folder next to the source). Rollback is a plain file copy.\\n* The upgraded document is validated with the 105 strict-integrity check **before** anything is written; on failure the original is untouched (`E_UPGRADE_VALIDATION_FAILED`, 83).\\n* Upgrading a 105-series document raises `E_FORMAT_UNSUPPORTED` (81). You cannot upgrade twice.\\n* \\u2705 The upgrade **validates the 104 source first** (full `validate_bejson`): a source with a short row, a mistyped cell, a wrong `Records_Type` count or a complex type in 104a raises `E_UPGRADE_VALIDATION_FAILED` (83) before any backup or write. `upgrade_dry_run` reports the same problems in `errors` with `validation_pass: False`.\\n* CLI: `Lib_PY/Core/cli/bejson_upgrade_cli.py` (with `--dry-run`).\\n* Do not hand-edit `Format_Version` to `105`. The Fail-on-Switch check in the validator rejects a re-headered file that has no `field_uuid` values.\\n\\n### 1.9 Nested 104 (documents inside cells)\\n\\nNested BEJSON is a **104-only** feature (`Lib_PY/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core_nesting.py`, error codes 130\\u2013139). A cell of a 104 document holds a complete embedded 104 document **stored as a JSON string** in a `string` field:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"Order\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [{\\\"name\\\": \\\"id\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"}, {\\\"name\\\": \\\"items\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"}],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [[\\\"o1\\\", \\\"{\\\\\\\"Format\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"BEJSON\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Format_Version\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"104\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Format_Creator\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Elton Boehnen\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Records_Type\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"Items\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"Fields\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"sku\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"qty\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"integer\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\"Values\\\\\\\":[[\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\",1],[\\\\\\\"b\\\\\\\",2]]}\\\"]]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n* `bejson_nesting_scan(doc)` finds nested documents and validates each with the Core validator; `bejson_nesting_query(doc, \\\"items\\\")` queries a column across all nested documents; `bejson_nesting_mutate` allows append-only schema changes and null-pads existing rows; hard depth ceiling `NESTING_MAX_DEPTH = 16`.\\n* All nested documents in one parent column must share the same fields (`E134`, schema mismatch).\\n* An embedded object stored directly in an `object` cell is **not** detected by the scanner.\\n\\n### 1.10 Best practices (105)\\n\\n* `snake_case` field names; `PascalCase` custom headers on `105a`; `PascalCase` entity names.\\n* Always generate UUIDs with `uuid.uuid4()`. Never hand-write, sequence or reuse them.\\n* Address columns and cells by `field_uuid` / `record_uuid` in code that must survive renames and concurrent edits. Never hard-code a row position.\\n* Use `null` for genuinely missing data, not `\\\"\\\"`.\\n* Manage schema versions with a custom header on `105a`; on `105`/`105db` custom headers are forbidden, so use the MFDB manifest `schema_version` or the `Records_Type` name.\\n* Schema change rules: appending a field is minor. Removing, retyping or reordering a field is breaking.\\n* Writers and the validator now check row length and types, but files edited outside the library are only as good as your last `validate_bejson` call: run it after any manual or third-party change.\\n* Write files only through `bejson_core_atomic_write` (or Core MFDB writers). Never edit `Values` in place in a text editor and expect the validator to catch a slip.\\n* Store timestamps as ISO-8601 UTC strings, e.g. `2026-10-24T12:00:00Z`. There is no timestamp type.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PART 2: MFDB 1.40 CRASH COURSE\\n**(Multi-File Database over 104 and 105 entity files)**\\n\\nMFDB is an architecture, not a `Format_Version`. One `104a` manifest registers any number of entity files; each entity file holds one entity type. In MFDB 1.40 an entity file may be **`104` or `105`** (mixed is allowed in one database). 105 entities unlock uuid-addressed CRUD and **referential integrity**.\\n\\n```text\\nmydb/\\n  104a.mfdb.bejson        <- manifest (always at the root, always 104a)\\n  data/\\n    customer.bejson       <- entity file (105)\\n    order.bejson          <- entity file (105)\\n    legacy.bejson         <- entity file (104)\\n```\\n\\n### 2.1 The manifest\\n\\n* `Format_Version` must be `\\\"104a\\\"` and `Records_Type` exactly `[\\\"mfdb\\\"]`. \\u2705 (A `105a` manifest is rejected.)\\n* Headers: `MFDB_Version`, `DB_Name` (required by convention), `DB_Description`, `Schema_Version`, `Author`, `Created_At`, `Network_Role` (`\\\"Master\\\"`/`\\\"Slave\\\"`), `Ref_Integrity` (`\\\"true\\\"`/`\\\"false\\\"` as **strings**), and debug headers (`Debug_Mode`, `Debug_Row_Cap`, `Debug_Reads`).\\n* Fields: `entity_name`, `file_path` (required, non-null, unique) \\u2705; then `description`, `record_count`, `schema_version`, `primary_key`.\\n* `file_path` is relative to the manifest and must not escape the database root (`..` traversal is rejected). \\u2705\\n* \\u26a0\\ufe0f **`mfdb_core_create_database(...)` defaults `mfdb_version=\\\"1.31\\\"`.** Nothing stamps `1.40` for you. Pass `mfdb_version=\\\"1.40\\\"` for a 105/Ref_Integrity database. The version string is a label; no code reads it to switch behaviour.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104a\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"MFDB_Version\\\": \\\"1.40\\\",\\n  \\\"Network_Role\\\": \\\"Master\\\",\\n  \\\"DB_Name\\\": \\\"Shop\\\",\\n  \\\"DB_Description\\\": \\\"Customers and orders\\\",\\n  \\\"Schema_Version\\\": \\\"1.0.0\\\",\\n  \\\"Author\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Created_At\\\": \\\"2026-10-24T00:00:00Z\\\",\\n  \\\"Ref_Integrity\\\": \\\"true\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"mfdb\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"entity_name\\\",    \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"file_path\\\",      \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"description\\\",    \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"record_count\\\",   \\\"type\\\": \\\"integer\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"schema_version\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"primary_key\\\",    \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"Customer\\\", \\\"data/customer.bejson\\\", \\\"Registered customers\\\", 1, \\\"1.0\\\", null],\\n    [\\\"Order\\\",    \\\"data/order.bejson\\\",    \\\"Customer orders\\\",      2, \\\"1.0\\\", null]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### 2.2 Entity files\\n\\n* Format `104` or `105` (`105a` and `105db` are rejected as entity formats since `mfdb_validator` 2.4.0: an entity has exactly one `Records_Type`). \\u2705\\n* `Parent_Hierarchy` is a **relative path from the entity file's own directory** to the manifest, e.g. `\\\"../104a.mfdb.bejson\\\"` for `data/order.bejson`. \\u2705 It must be present and must resolve to an existing file.\\n* `Records_Type` is one string, the entity name.\\n* Create with `mfdb_core_create_database(root, name, entities, mfdb_version=\\\"1.40\\\")` or `mfdb_core_create_entity_file(manifest, name, fields, format_version=\\\"105\\\")`. Pass `format_version`/`\\\"105\\\"` per entity; omit it and you get a 104 entity.\\n\\n```python\\nfrom lib_bejson_Core_mfdb_core import mfdb_core_create_database, mfdb_core_enable_ref_integrity\\nS = lambda n: {\\\"name\\\": n, \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"}\\nmanifest = mfdb_core_create_database(\\\"shop\\\", \\\"Shop\\\", [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Customer\\\", \\\"fields\\\": [S(\\\"cust_name\\\")], \\\"format_version\\\": \\\"105\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Order\\\", \\\"format_version\\\": \\\"105\\\", \\\"fields\\\": [\\n        S(\\\"order_code\\\"),\\n        {\\\"name\\\": \\\"customer_uuid_fk\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"fk_target_entity\\\": \\\"Customer\\\", \\\"fk_on_delete\\\": \\\"cascade\\\"},\\n        {\\\"name\\\": \\\"total\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"number\\\"}]},\\n], mfdb_version=\\\"1.40\\\")\\nmfdb_core_enable_ref_integrity(manifest)\\n```\\n\\nEntity file produced (`data/order.bejson`, one `field_uuid` per field generated for you):\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Parent_Hierarchy\\\": \\\"../104a.mfdb.bejson\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"Order\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"order_code\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"b0000000-0001-4000-8000-000000000001\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"customer_uuid_fk\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"fk_target_entity\\\": \\\"Customer\\\", \\\"fk_on_delete\\\": \\\"cascade\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"b0000000-0002-4000-8000-000000000002\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"total\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"number\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"b0000000-0003-4000-8000-000000000003\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"c1111111-0001-4000-8000-000000000001\\\", \\\"O1\\\", \\\"c2222222-0001-4000-8000-000000000001\\\", 9.5]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### 2.3 Discovery\\n\\n`mfdb_core_discover(path)` returns `'manifest'`, `'entity'`, `'archive'` or `'standalone'`:\\n\\n* `104a` + filename ending `.mfdb.bejson` \\u2192 `manifest`.\\n* `104`/`105`/`105a`/`105db` with `Parent_Hierarchy` \\u2192 `entity` (105-series recognised since `mfdb_core` 3.1.0).\\n* Anything else \\u2192 `standalone`.\\n\\n### 2.4 Referential integrity (the 1.40 feature)\\n\\n**Declare** a foreign key on a field of a 105-series entity (`fk_target_entity`, `fk_on_delete`). **Turn it on** per database with `mfdb_core_enable_ref_integrity(manifest)` (sets the `Ref_Integrity` header to the string `\\\"true\\\"`; `mfdb_core_disable_ref_integrity` sets `\\\"false\\\"`). It is **off by default**.\\n\\nAn FK value is the target row's **`record_uuid`**. `null` always means \\\"no relationship\\\" and always passes. The suffix of the field name (`_fk`, `_uuid_fk`) is only a convention; the code looks at `fk_target_entity`.\\n\\nWrite-time behaviour (only through the `*_105` CRUD functions, only when `Ref_Integrity` is `\\\"true\\\"`):\\n\\n| Action | Result (verified) |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| `mfdb_core_add_entity_record_105(m, \\\"Order\\\", [\\\"O2\\\", \\\"ghost-uuid\\\", 5.0])` | `MFDBCoreError` code **39** `E_MFDB_FK_UNRESOLVED`; nothing written |\\n| Same with a real customer `record_uuid` | Written; manifest `record_count` synced |\\n| Same with `None` for the FK | Written |\\n| `mfdb_core_update_entity_record_105(m, \\\"Order\\\", rec_uuid, \\\"customer_uuid_fk\\\", \\\"nope\\\")` | Code 39 |\\n| FK target entity unknown in manifest | Code 43 `E_MFDB_FK_TARGET_ENTITY_UNKNOWN` |\\n| FK target is a 104 entity | Code 43 (\\\"not a 105-series entity\\\") |\\n| Bad `fk_on_delete` value | Code 47 `E_MFDB_FK_INVALID_ON_DELETE` |\\n| Delete a parent whose child says `restrict` | Code **44** `E_MFDB_FK_RESTRICT_VIOLATION`; nothing deleted |\\n| Delete a parent whose child says `cascade` | Children deleted too (cycle-guarded; code 45 if a cycle is hit) |\\n| Delete a parent whose child says `null` | Parent deleted; child FK set to `null` |\\n\\nCascade/restrict/null deletes use **plan-then-commit**: the whole plan is built in memory first, then every touched file is snapshotted and restored if anything fails.\\n\\n`mfdb_core_delete_entity_record_105(...)` and friends are the **only** guarded paths. These bypass integrity completely: the 104 CRUD functions, direct file edits, and any write made while `Ref_Integrity` is `\\\"false\\\"`.\\n\\nAudit an existing database (read-only, works whether or not the switch is on):\\n\\n```python\\nvalidate_mfdb_database(manifest, strict_fk=True)       # adds FK errors (code 39) to the result\\nmfdb_validator_check_referential_integrity(manifest)   # list of {'code','entity','detail'}\\n```\\n\\n`mfdb_core_check_entity_droppable(manifest, entity)` is the read-only check for \\\"is anyone still pointing at this entity\\\" (code 46). There is no entity-drop function yet.\\n\\n\\u274c `fk_target_field_uuid` appears in older documentation. **No code reads it.** FKs always point at the target's `record_uuid`.\\n\\n### 2.5 105 CRUD in an MFDB\\n\\n| Function | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| `mfdb_core_add_entity_record_105(m, entity, values)` | `values` in `Fields` order, **without** `record_uuid`; returns the updated entity doc; syncs `record_count`. Raises code 54 on a 104 entity. Wrong `len(values)` (code 9) or a mistyped value (code 8) raises before anything is written. |\\n| `mfdb_core_update_entity_record_105(m, entity, record_uuid, field_name, value)` | FK-checked when the field is a declared FK; value type checked. |\\n| `mfdb_core_delete_entity_record_105(m, entity, record_uuid)` | Honors `fk_on_delete`. |\\n| `mfdb_core_load_entity(m, entity)` | List of dicts; **105 rows include a `record_uuid` key**. |\\n| `mfdb_core_get_entity_doc(m, entity)` | The raw entity document. |\\n| `mfdb_core_sync_all_counts(m)` | Re-counts every entity and updates `record_count`. |\\n| `mfdb_core_get_stats(m)`, `mfdb_core_query_entity`, `mfdb_core_join` | Read helpers; `get_stats` reports per-entity `field_count`/`record_count`. |\\n\\nThe 104 writer `mfdb_core_add_entity_record(...)` now fails on a 105 entity (it relies on `bejson_core_add_record`, which raises `E_FORMAT_UNSUPPORTED` for 105-series) and writes nothing. It used to silently write a row with no `record_uuid`.\\n\\n### 2.6 Validation\\n\\n`validate_mfdb_database(manifest, strict_fk=False)` enforces the MFDB spec levels for **104 and 105** entities alike (`mfdb_validator` 2.4.0). Error codes are embedded in each message as `[NN]`.\\n\\n| Check | Level | Hard/Warn | Code | Status |\\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\\n| Manifest is valid `104a`, `Records_Type == [\\\"mfdb\\\"]` | 1 | Hard | 32 | \\u2705 |\\n| `entity_name` and `file_path` fields exist | 1 | Hard | 40 | \\u2705 |\\n| No null `entity_name` / `file_path` | 1 | Hard | 41 | \\u2705 |\\n| `entity_name` and `file_path` unique | 1 | Hard | 35 | \\u2705 |\\n| Every `file_path` exists and stays inside the DB root | 1 | Hard | 33 | \\u2705 |\\n| Entity is valid BEJSON (`104` or `105`) | 2 | Hard | n/a | \\u2705 |\\n| `Parent_Hierarchy` present | 2 | Hard | 36 | \\u2705 |\\n| `Parent_Hierarchy` resolves to an existing file (else orphan) | 2 | Hard | 37 | \\u2705 |\\n| `Records_Type` is exactly one string | 2 | Hard | 34 | \\u2705 |\\n| `Records_Type[0]` equals the manifest `entity_name` (case-sensitive) | 2 | Hard | 34 | \\u2705 |\\n| Bidirectional: `Parent_Hierarchy` names the manifest that lists this file, and that manifest has a row resolving to this file | 2 | Hard | 38 | \\u2705 |\\n| `record_count` equals the real row count | 3 | **Warning** | n/a | \\u2705 (in `result.warnings`; never invalidates) |\\n| FK values resolve (`strict_fk=True`, 105 entities only) | 3 | Hard (as error) | 39 | \\u2705 |\\n\\n`validate_mfdb_entity_file(path)` run on a single file applies the Level 2 checks including the bidirectional check against whatever manifest its `Parent_Hierarchy` names. A stray copy of an entity file that no manifest row points at therefore fails with code 38.\\n\\n`mfdb_core_deep_verify(manifest)` is column-offset aware since `mfdb_core` 3.1.0: a healthy 105 entity (row length `len(Fields)+1`) produces no findings, a genuinely short or long row gives `POSITIONAL_VIOLATION` with the correct expected length, and type warnings read the right column.\\n\\n`mfdb_core_self_heal(manifest)` still re-syncs `record_count`, resurrects missing files and pads or truncates **104-series** rows. For a 105-series entity with a positional violation it **does not touch the file** (it cannot know which cell is missing or extra) and lists the entity under `remaining_errors` for manual repair. Healthy 105 entities are never modified.\\n\\n### 2.7 Master/Slave federation\\n\\n* `Network_Role` is `\\\"Master\\\"` or `\\\"Slave\\\"`. The slave is \\\"structurally blind\\\": it holds no path to the master.\\n* `mfdb_federation_push_config(config_doc, slave_target_path)` writes a BEJSON `104a` config into the slave's directory using a temp file in the same directory plus `os.rename` (atomic; no partial reads). Returns `True`/`False`.\\n* `mfdb_federation_poll_dropzone(dropzone_dir, callback, poll_interval=2.0, timeout=60.0)` (slave side): for each `*.bejson` file found, parse it, call `callback(path, doc)`, delete the file. Returns the number processed.\\n* `mfdb_federation_distill_logs(...)` (slave side): truncates overflow log rows and pushes distilled metrics to the master's poll directory.\\n* A `ConnectedSlave` registry entity can be created with `mfdb_core_create_connected_slave_entity(manifest)`.\\n\\n### 2.8 MFDB-132 (single-file package)\\n\\n`bejson_core_chunking_create_mfdb132_package(mfdb_root_dir, db_name, ...)` collapses a whole MFDB directory into one Chunked-104a document.\\n\\n* Per-file fields (fixed): `File_Name`, `File_Extension`, `File_Content`, `File_Version`, `File_Hash`, `Relative_Path`, `Is_Binary`, `Is_Mounted`.\\n* Identity headers: `Schema_Name: \\\"MFDB-132\\\"`, `Records_Type: [\\\"MFDB-132\\\"]`, `MFDB_Version: \\\"1.32\\\"` (the constant `MFDB_CHUNK_SCHEMA_VERSION`, which never changes), `DB_Name`, `Package_Format: \\\"MFDB-Chunked-104a\\\"`, `Package_Version`, `Session_Is_Mounted`, `Mount_Path`.\\n* Mounting (`MFDB132Archive`) writes a lock file **`.mfdb132_lock`** (JSON: `pid`, `chunk_doc_path`, `original_hash`, `workspace_dir`, `mounted_at`). If the package SHA-256 still matches `original_hash`, the workspace is reused without re-extracting.\\n* Commits are gated by full validation: a corrupt entity rejects the commit before anything is written.\\n* The older zip-based `MFDBArchive` class uses a different lock file name, `.mfdb_lock`.\\n* The packager chunks a directory that holds a `104a.mfdb.bejson`; it is format-agnostic about whether entities are 104 or 105.\\n\\n### 2.9 Error codes (those the 105 Core actually defines)\\n\\n| Range | Meaning |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| 1\\u201316 | BEJSON validation: 2 missing key \\u00b7 3 invalid format/creator \\u00b7 4 invalid version \\u00b7 5 `Records_Type` \\u00b7 6 `Fields` \\u00b7 7 `Values` \\u00b7 8 type mismatch \\u00b7 9 row length \\u00b7 10 reserved key \\u00b7 11 `Record_Type_Parent` \\u00b7 13 file not found \\u00b7 15 atomic write failed |\\n| 17\\u201329 | Core operations |\\n| 30\\u201342 | MFDB validation: 30 not a manifest \\u00b7 31 not an entity \\u00b7 32 manifest `Records_Type` \\u00b7 33 entity not found \\u00b7 34 name mismatch \\u00b7 35 duplicate \\u00b7 36 no `Parent_Hierarchy` \\u00b7 37 manifest not found \\u00b7 38 bidirectional fail \\u00b7 39 FK unresolved \\u00b7 40 missing required field \\u00b7 41 null required \\u00b7 42 invalid archive |\\n| 43\\u201347 | MFDB referential integrity: 43 FK target unknown \\u00b7 44 restrict violation \\u00b7 45 cascade cycle \\u00b7 46 entity still referenced \\u00b7 47 invalid `fk_on_delete` |\\n| 50\\u201360, 70\\u201372 | MFDB core operations (54 = invalid operation, e.g. a 105 call on a 104 entity) |\\n| 80\\u201385 | 105-series: 80 field integrity mismatch \\u00b7 81 format unsupported \\u00b7 82 record not found \\u00b7 83 upgrade validation failed \\u00b7 84 duplicate field name \\u00b7 85 duplicate field_uuid |\\n| 130\\u2013139 | Nested 104 |\\n\\nThe codes **86\\u2013114 do not exist**: the 105 \\\"schema constraint\\\" codes (required/unique/enum/min/max/length) were removed in `bejson_errors` 2.9.0. Older text that mentions `E90`\\u2013`E95` is out of date. Codes 30\\u201342 and 86+ are partly unused by the validators in 105 (for example the validator never raises 34 or 38; Part 4).\\n\\n### 2.10 Standardization conventions\\n\\n| Item | Convention | Status |\\n| :--- | :--- | :---: |\\n| Manifest filename | `104a.mfdb.bejson`, one per database, at the root | \\u26a0\\ufe0f |\\n| Entity filenames | `<entity_name_lower>.bejson` (multi-word `snake_case`), default folder `data/` | \\u26a0\\ufe0f |\\n| Entity names | `PascalCase` | \\u26a0\\ufe0f |\\n| Field names | `snake_case` | \\u26a0\\ufe0f |\\n| Primary key | 105: the reserved `record_uuid`. A `<entity>_id` style key is optional business data. | \\u26a0\\ufe0f |\\n| Foreign key | 105: a field with `fk_target_entity` holding the target `record_uuid`. Name it `<target>_uuid_fk` (or `<target>_id_fk`) for readability. | \\u26a0\\ufe0f |\\n| Write safety | Temp file + rename for every write; `ResilientPIDLock` around read-modify-write | \\u2705 (Core does it) |\\n| `record_count` | Advisory; synced by the `*_105` CRUD functions and `mfdb_core_sync_all_counts` | \\u26a0\\ufe0f |\\n| Paths | Relative, never leaving the DB root | \\u2705 |\\n| Secrets | Never in plaintext in a data file | \\u26a0\\ufe0f |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PART 3: CHEAT SHEETS\\n\\n### 3.1 Format comparison\\n\\n| Feature | 104 | 104a | 104db | 105 | 105a | 105db |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Record types | 1 | 1 | 2+ | 1 | 1 | 2+ |\\n| Custom headers | \\u274c | \\u2705 | \\u274c | \\u274c | \\u2705 | \\u274c |\\n| `Parent_Hierarchy` | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| `array`/`object` fields | \\u2705 | \\u274c | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u274c | \\u2705 |\\n| `field_uuid` per field | no | no | no | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| `record_uuid` per row | no | no | no | col 0 | col 0 | col 1 |\\n| Row length | `len(Fields)` | same | same | `len(Fields)+1` | same | same |\\n| Row length checked by validator | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| Value types checked by validator | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| MFDB manifest | no | **yes** | no | no | no | no |\\n| MFDB entity file | yes | no | no | yes | no | no |\\n| Declarative FK + cascade | no | no | no | yes | yes | yes |\\n\\n### 3.2 Most-used functions\\n\\n| Task | Function |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| Validate a file/doc | `validate_bejson(path_or_doc, is_file=True)` \\u2192 `.valid`, `.errors` |\\n| Validate an MFDB | `validate_mfdb_database(manifest, strict_fk=True)` |\\n| Load / save | `bejson_core_load_file(path)` / `bejson_core_atomic_write(path, doc)` |\\n| Column positions | `bejson_core_get_field_map(doc)` |\\n| Resolve by UUID or name | `bejson_core_resolve_field_index(doc, target)` |\\n| Add column / row (105) | `bejson_core_define_field`, `bejson_core_add_record_105` |\\n| Edit / delete (105) | `bejson_core_write_cell`, `bejson_core_delete_record`, `bejson_core_remove_field` |\\n| Health check (UUIDs only) | `bejson_core_verify_105_integrity(doc)` |\\n| Upgrade | `bejson_core_upgrade_dry_run`, `bejson_core_upgrade_to_105` |\\n| New MFDB | `mfdb_core_create_database(..., mfdb_version=\\\"1.40\\\")` |\\n| Turn on FKs | `mfdb_core_enable_ref_integrity(manifest)` |\\n| Guarded CRUD | `mfdb_core_add/update/delete_entity_record_105` |\\n| FK audit | `mfdb_validator_check_referential_integrity(manifest)` |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PART 4: REALITY AUDIT (what was missing, what the parity patch fixed, what is still open)\\n\\nFirst pass: everything below was reproduced by running the original `libraries_105.zip` Core. Second pass: the **105\\u2194104 parity patch** (validator 3.4.0 \\u00b7 core 3.6.0 \\u00b7 upgrade 1.1.0 \\u00b7 mfdb_core 3.1.0) was applied and each item re-run. The 30 tests in `tests/test_105_104_parity_suite.py` pin the fixes. The same rules were then ported to JS, TS and Shell (see 4.7), so the \\\"after patch\\\" column holds in every language for validation, and for the writers listed in 4.7.\\n\\n### 4.1 Checks that 104 performs and 105 did not\\n\\n| # | Check (104 behaviour) | Before patch | After patch |\\n| :-: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| 1 | Row length equals field count (105: `len(Fields)+1`), code 9 | Short, long and uuid-less rows passed | \\u2705 Rejected (all 105 formats, offset-aware) |\\n| 2 | Value type matches field type, bool excluded from integer/number, code 8 | A string in an integer column passed (validator, `add_record_105`, `write_cell`) | \\u2705 Rejected by the validator, `add_record_105`, `write_cell`, `mfdb_core_add_entity_record_105` |\\n| 3 | 104a primitives only | `105a` accepted `array`/`object` | \\u2705 Rejected |\\n| 4 | `Records_Type` count 1 / 1 / 2+, code 5 | Any count passed on 105-series | \\u2705 Enforced (`105`, `105a`: exactly 1; `105db`: 2+) |\\n| 5 | 104db: first field `Record_Type_Parent`, per-field owner, discriminator in `Records_Type`, code 11 | None enforced on `105db` | \\u2705 All three enforced; `record_uuid` in column 0 is caught by the discriminator check |\\n| 6 | (105-specific) `field_uuid`/`record_uuid` are UUIDv4 | Any non-empty string passed silently | \\u26a0\\ufe0f Still passes, but now reports a **warning** per non-v4 id. Kept non-fatal on purpose so existing files with hand-made or v5 ids keep validating. Making it an error is a one-line change in `validate_bejson` |\\n| 7 | Upgrade validates its input | A 104 source with a short row upgraded \\\"successfully\\\" | \\u2705 Source is fully validated first (`E_UPGRADE_VALIDATION_FAILED`, 83); nothing written, no backup made. Dry run reports it |\\n| 8 | Verification helper agrees with validator | `verify_105_integrity` said `ok: True` on bad rows | \\u2705 Uses the same checks |\\n| 8b | `add_field` validates the type string (105 path) | Accepted `\\\"timestamp\\\"` and then produced an invalid document | \\u2705 Rejected (code 80) |\\n\\n### 4.2 MFDB spec checks the older course documents (104 **and** 105)\\n\\nAll three were documented in the 104 crash course but performed by no validator. Now enforced for both 104 and 105 databases (`mfdb_validator` 2.4.0, pinned by `tests/test_mfdb_spec_levels_suite.py`; 18 of its 24 tests fail on the original Core).\\n\\n| # | Documented check | Before | After |\\n| :-: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| 9 | Entity `Records_Type` equals manifest `entity_name`, exactly one string (34) | An entity renamed to `[\\\"WrongName\\\"]` still validated | \\u2705 Hard error, case-sensitive |\\n| 10 | Bidirectional manifest\\u2194entity (38); orphan (37) | Only \\\"`Parent_Hierarchy` resolves to some file\\\" | \\u2705 Manifest must list the entity file; entity must point back at the listing manifest. A stray unlisted copy fails |\\n| 11 | `record_count` mismatch is a warning | Not in the validator | \\u2705 Warning only |\\n\\nNot part of the spec and **not** added: scanning the directory for unlisted entity files during `validate_mfdb_database` (the spec's discovery is file-local; run `validate_mfdb_entity_file` on a suspect file instead).\\n\\n### 4.3 Functions that were unsafe or wrong on 105 data\\n\\n| # | Function | Before patch | After patch |\\n| :-: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| 12 | `mfdb_core_add_entity_record` (104 writer) on a 105 entity | Wrote a row with no `record_uuid`; database still validated | \\u2705 Raises (`E_FORMAT_UNSUPPORTED`), file untouched |\\n| 13 | `mfdb_core_deep_verify` | False `POSITIONAL_VIOLATION` / `CORRUPT_JSON` on every healthy 105 row | \\u2705 Clean on healthy 105; still flags a genuinely broken 105 row |\\n| 14 | `mfdb_core_self_heal` | **Deleted the last cell of every healthy 105 row** | \\u2705 Never modifies 105 entities; reports a broken 105 row under `remaining_errors`. 104 repair behaviour unchanged |\\n| 15 | `bejson_core_add_record` (104 adder) on a 105 doc | Appended a uuid-less row | \\u2705 Raises `E_FORMAT_UNSUPPORTED` (81) |\\n| 16 | `mfdb_core_discover` | `standalone` for every 105 entity | \\u2705 `entity` |\\n| 17 | `bejson_core_remove_field` on a doc with a short row | `IndexError` | \\u2705 Short rows can no longer be created through the library; validate files that come from elsewhere |\\n| 18 | Ref_Integrity is off by default and bypassed by direct edits | Easy to assume protection that is not there | \\u26a0\\ufe0f Unchanged (by design). Turn it on and audit with `strict_fk=True` |\\n\\n### 4.4 Documentation that still disagrees with the code\\n\\n| Where | Says | Reality |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| `libraries_105` README \\u00a73.7 (historical) | FK metadata included `fk_target_field_uuid` | Reconciled at PKG111: FKs always target `record_uuid` |\\n| README \\u00a73.7 and \\u00a710.6 (historical) | Error codes `E90`\\u2013`E95` for 105 | Reconciled at PKG118: removed in `bejson_errors` 2.9.0; codes 86\\u2013114 do not exist |\\n| README / v23 course | MFDB 1.40 databases | `mfdb_core_create_database` defaults `MFDB_Version` to `\\\"1.31\\\"`; pass `\\\"1.40\\\"` |\\n| Older 104 course nesting section | A nested document can be a string **or** object | The scanner only finds nested documents stored as JSON **strings** |\\n| README \\u00a71.2 | 105 runtime enforces \\\"strict positional length, data-type conformance\\\" | True after this patch; it was not true before it |\\n\\n### 4.5 Behaviour changes to know about\\n\\n* **Stricter files.** A 105-series file that used to validate with a wrong-length row, a mistyped cell, or a wrong `Records_Type` count now fails. That is the point, but run `validate_bejson` over existing 105 data before deploying the patch.\\n* **Stricter MFDB.** A database whose entity `Records_Type` does not match the manifest, or whose `Parent_Hierarchy` does not point back at the manifest listing it, now fails `validate_mfdb_database`. Entity files must be `104` or `105`.\\n* **Stricter upgrades.** A 104 file that was never valid can no longer be upgraded. One shipped test fixture (a 104db with a single `Records_Type`, and one with a bad `Format_Creator`) was itself invalid and was corrected.\\n* **API change.** `bejson_core_add_record` raises on 105-series documents instead of returning a bool.\\n* **Not changed.** Error code numbers, the on-disk 105 layout, FK semantics, and all 104-series behaviour and messages.\\n\\n### 4.6 Practical guidance\\n\\n1. Use only the `*_105` functions on 105 data; never the 104 `add_record` / `mfdb_core_add_entity_record`.\\n2. Run `validate_bejson` (and `validate_mfdb_database`) after any edit made outside the library.\\n3. Turn on `Ref_Integrity` and audit with `strict_fk=True` for any database whose FKs matter.\\n4. Pass `mfdb_version=\\\"1.40\\\"` when creating a 105 database.\\n5. Parity is verified for validation and the writers in 4.7; `deep_verify` / `self_heal` exist only in Python (4.7).\\n\\n### 4.7 Multi-language status (verified by a shared conformance corpus)\\n\\nA corpus is generated from the Python reference and replayed through each language: 82 single-document validation cases, 98 MFDB database cases (33 on the 104 tree) and 11 foreign-key audit cases. Contract: the same valid/invalid verdict everywhere, and the same MFDB spec codes `[30]`-`[41]` whenever the reference emits one. An entity that fails base-BEJSON validation is compared by verdict only (each language reports it in its own registry: PY no code, JS `[31]`, TS BEJSON numeric codes). FK audit: same findings (code and entity) and the same strict-FK verdict. Result: **0 differences** in PY, TS and SH; JS differs only in the number of advisory UUID-format warnings on documents that are already invalid. Harness: `dev/conformance/`.\\n\\n**Path rule (all languages):** a manifest `file_path` or an entity `Parent_Hierarchy` must be relative and stay inside the database root. Absolute paths (`/x`, `C:\\\\x`) and `..` climbs above the root are rejected; the manifest error carries `[33]`. Before this edition an absolute path to an existing file outside the root validated as valid.\\n\\n**`fk_on_delete`:** only an absent key defaults to `restrict`; an explicit `null` is invalid (code 47) in every language.\\n\\n**`Network_Role`:** optional manifest header; `Master`, `Slave` or `Standalone`, anything else is `[30]`.\\n\\n| Rule set | PY | JS | TS | SH |\\n| :--- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\\n| 104 version rules: row length, value types, Records_Type count, 104a primitives, 104db Record_Type_Parent, custom headers | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 (new) |\\n| 105 equivalents, offset-aware (`record_uuid` slot) | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 (new) | \\u2705 (new) |\\n| MFDB: Records_Type == `entity_name` (34), bidirectional (38), orphan (37), no Parent_Hierarchy (36), duplicates (35), missing/null field (40/41), missing file (33), `record_count` drift = warning | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 (new; 104 SH had no entity/database validation at all) |\\n| Entity format: 104 in the 104 tree; 104 or 105 in the 105 tree | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 |\\n| 105 writers check length/types (`add_record_105`, `write_cell`) | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 (new) | n/a (SH `add_record` refuses 105 docs) |\\n| 104 writers refuse 105 docs | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 (new) | \\u2705 |\\n| `deep_verify`, `self_heal`, upgrade validation | \\u2705 | n/a | n/a | n/a |\\n| UUIDv4-format advisory warnings | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u274c (SH has no warning channel for it) |\\n\\nTS notes: the validators are pure in-memory functions. Pass `manifestRelativePath` (the manifest's path relative to the database root) to `validateDatabase` so the bidirectional check is exact; without it the entity's `Parent_Hierarchy` must land on a root-level `*.mfdb.bejson`. SH notes: errors carry `[NN]` codes in `mfdb_validator_get_errors`; drift warnings are in `mfdb_validator_get_warnings`; the 104 tree's `strict_fk` is accepted and ignored (104 entities have no foreign keys).\\n\\n*End of document.*\\n\\n\\n### bejson_project.json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"104a\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Project_Name\\\": \\\"libraries_105\\\",\\n  \\\"Project_Version\\\": \\\"5.9.13\\\",\\n  \\\"Package_Version\\\": \\\"125\\\",\\n  \\\"Versioned_UUID\\\": \\\"04e9033c-2ee4-4840-8f2c-59cccc626ab2\\\",\\n  \\\"Project_GUID\\\": \\\"1952586a-42b4-44ce-9471-40831d0bf46b\\\",\\n  \\\"Type\\\": \\\"library\\\",\\n  \\\"Status\\\": \\\"Active\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\n    \\\"RootPackage\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"change_log\\\",\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.13 | 2026-11-19 | AI_Core 2.1.1 / AI_Extensions reconciled across libraries_105, Release tree and agent vendored lib: single DEFAULT_TEXT_MODEL_ID (gemini-3.8-flash), env_vault fallback roots, stale compiler tests aligned, invariant tests (107 AI_Core, 21 AI_Extensions). NOT DONE: BEJSON/ ledger refresh (8 families unregistered, errors.bejson covers 4 of 14), AI_Core freeze.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.12 | 2026-11-18 | Add Lib_PY/Metrics family (errors 580-584, 105a schemas, collector, validator, stacked-bar Canvas/SVG renderer, automated test runner & pipeline), consolidate benchmark/stress suites into dev/metrics/, compile index.html + results.md + Playwright visual audit screenshots, and add headline benchmark results section to README.md.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.9 | 2026-11-17 | Lib_PY/AI v1.6.1 gap-audit remediation: API key removed from live-model URL, hardcoded vault paths removed ($DEFINITIONS_FILE honored), corrupt config preserved instead of reset, tool handler no longer double-executes, provider error text redacted and capped, provider discovery restricted to package dir, logging added to dispatcher/clients/key rotator, 7 regression tests (30/30).\\\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.8 | 2026-11-14 | Add Lib_PY/AI v1.5.0 3-Tier Multi-AI Provider Gateway (Gemini REST & Interactions, Name_Case model catalog, persistent 105a config, config-gated Tooling_Registry, Parser_Registry, Extension_Parser_Registry, AIEnvVault, KeyRotator, JITKeyHandler, 18/18 unit tests), Canvas/JS test suites, AI Gateway architecture diagrams, extended README, and hardened .gitignore.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.7 | 2026-11-12 | Integrate multi-scale (5,000 to 50,000 records) write speed, read speed, memory heap, and token cost benchmark lab into public interactive suite.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.6 | 2026-11-12 | Add public-facing client-side interactive benchmark suite (docs/index.html & Admin/Dev/HTML_Apps) with token economics, parser latency, and client telemetry reporting.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.5 | 2026-11-12 | Expand comprehensive 16-section README.md, synchronize Automation and EBook subsystems, and configure GitHub Actions CI and Release pipelines.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.4 | 2026-11-06 | Integrate CMS MTV Scaffolding Engine family (Lib_PY/CMS), pre-built taxonomy archetypes, 105 MFDB v1.40 models, 13 canonical BEHTML templates, and admin dashboard.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.3 | 2026-11-02 | Add Standards stateful loaded chamber logging module, unit tests, and multi-language conformance suite.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.10 | 2026-11-17 | PARTIAL AI_Core urgent instructions: Lib_PY/AI renamed AI_Core (lib_bejson_AI_Core_*), validator 2.1.0 (allow pragma; scans every .py), plugin registry/schema (errors 236-244), AI_Extensions family (3 plugins, profile_runtime 1.0.1, NEW profile_compiler + profile_abstraction), 105 profiles 2.0.1 (104->105 converter no longer mutates source). 82/82 AI_Core tests. NOT DONE: AI_Extensions tests, Test_Clients/Chat, README, AI_Core freeze.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \\\"5.9.11 | 2026-11-17 | AI_Extensions tests added (tests/test_ai_extensions_profiles.py, 21 tests: compiler, abstraction, runtime); profile_runtime 1.0.2 docstring fix. Still open: Test_Clients/Chat, AI_Core README refresh, BEJSON/ ledger rows, AI_Core freeze.\\\"\\n    ]\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Project_Modified_Date\\\": \\\"2026-11-19\\\"\\n}\\n\\n### Libraries_105_Agent_Directive-V10101-PKG101.md\\n# Libraries_105 Agent Directive \\u2014 What To Use, Exactly\\n**Name:** Libraries_105 Agent Directive  \\n**Description:** Mandatory instructions for any AI agent that writes code against Elton Boehnen's `libraries_105` (BEJSON 105 / MFDB v1.40 ecosystem). Tells the agent which library feature to call for every common job, per language, and which documented examples are wrong.  \\n**Version:** `VERSION = 10101` (v1.0.1)  \\n**Date Created:** 2026-11-08  \\n**Author Credit:** Elton Boehnen \\u00b7 boehnenelton2024@gmail.com \\u00b7 boehnenelton2024.pages.dev \\u00b7 github.com/boehnenelton  \\n**RELATIONAL_ID:** `e03e4ea7-414e-4180-88ed-79ebc832d0f2`  \\n**Scope:** Python (`Lib_PY`), JavaScript (`Lib_JS`), TypeScript (`Lib_TS`), Bash (`Lib_SH`)  \\n**Verification:** Every Python recipe below was executed against the shipped library. JS and Bash recipes were spot-checked as noted. Anything marked **(unverified)** was read from source, not run.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. How to Use This Document\\n\\nYou are an agent. Before you write any code that reads, writes, locks, validates, logs, or sandboxes BEJSON data:\\n\\n1. Find the job in \\u00a73 (capability matrix) or \\u00a74\\u2013\\u00a78 (recipes).\\n2. Call the library function named there. **Do not write your own version.**\\n3. If the matrix says a language has a gap (\\u274c / \\u26a0\\ufe0f), say so to the user and use the stated fallback. Do not silently re-implement.\\n4. Run the checklist in \\u00a711 before you deliver.\\n\\n**Source of truth order:** (1) the library source files, (2) this directive, (3) `clarification.md`, (4) `README.md`. The README is **not** reliable (see \\u00a71).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Known-Wrong Documentation (Do Not Copy)\\n\\nThese are in the shipped docs and are false. Agents that trust them produce code that crashes or invents APIs.\\n\\n| Where | Claim | Reality |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `README.md` (fixed in V5.9.5-PKG118; rows below describe the pre-fix text) Python quickstart | `from lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core_105 import bejson_core_105_parse_document, bejson_core_105_insert_record, bejson_core_105_get_field_map` | **No such module or functions in Python.** (`...core_105` exists only in TypeScript.) Use `bejson_core_load_file`, `bejson_core_add_record_105`, `bejson_core_get_field_map` from `lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core` |\\n| `README.md` JS quickstart | `bejson_core_parse_bejson(path)` | **Does not exist.** Read the file with `fs.readFileSync` + `JSON.parse`, then use `bejson_core_get_field_map` |\\n| `README.md` | `mfdb_validator_check_referential_integrity(...)` returns `{is_valid, errors}` | Python returns a **list** of findings; empty list = clean |\\n| `README.md` | Manifest named `105a.mfdb.bejson`; structural errors \\\"E90\\u2013E95\\\" | The manifest is always **`104a.mfdb.bejson`**. Codes 90\\u2013114 were **removed** (Core errors v2.9.0). There is no E90\\u2013E95 |\\n| `README.md` | \\\"Exact functional parity across Python, JS, TS, Bash\\\" | Not true. See \\u00a73: JS/TS have no PID lock; JS lacks `define_field`; TS has no MFDB record CRUD; Bash has no 105 doc-level CRUD |\\n| `BEJSON_Crash_Course_105-V3.md`, `GEMINI.md`, other prose | May describe behavior from earlier releases | Verify against source before relying on it |\\n\\nIf a doc and the source disagree, **the source wins** and you report the discrepancy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Hard Rules (Never Violate)\\n\\n1. **Never modify any file in `libraries_105`.** Core is frozen. Wrap it; do not patch it.\\n2. **Never reimplement** locking, atomic writes, field-map caching, validation, env parsing, path guarding, or logging. Use the library.\\n3. **Never use positional indices** (`row[3]`) or hardcoded offsets. Always `field_map[name]` from `bejson_core_get_field_map(doc)`.\\n4. **Never declare `record_uuid` as a Field in a 105/105a/105db document.** It is implicit (row index 0) and added for you. `bejson_core_add_record_105` takes values **without** it.\\n5. **Never hand-write `field_uuid` values.** Use `bejson_core_define_field` (or `mfdb_core_create_entity_file(..., format_version=\\\"105\\\")`).\\n6. **Never write a BEJSON file with `open(path, \\\"w\\\")` / `fs.writeFileSync` / `>`.** Use `bejson_core_atomic_write` (PY, SH) or a temp-file + rename (JS/TS, \\u00a74).\\n7. **Never write a shared BEJSON file without a lock** where a lock exists (PY, SH). Read-modify-write goes **inside** the lock.\\n8. **Never name your MFDB manifest anything but `104a.mfdb.bejson`.** Entity files may be Format `105`; the manifest stays `104a`.\\n9. **Never use `print()` for logging in production Python.** Use `logging` + the Standards handler (\\u00a78.1).\\n10. **Never invent error codes in a range another family owns.** See \\u00a79.\\n11. **Never add a persisted `_bejson_field_map`** to files you write. It is an in-memory cache; the library strips and rebuilds it.\\n12. **Never hard-code filesystem paths.** Resolve through env/config (\\u00a72.1).\\n\\n### 2.1 Setup & Imports (Verified)\\n\\nSet `ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105` to the `libraries_105` root. In **Python the modules are flat files inside family folders**, so put each family folder you use on `sys.path` (the README's `PYTHONPATH=Lib_PY` alone is not enough):\\n\\n```python\\nimport os, sys\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\n\\nLIBRARIES_105_ROOT = Path(os.environ[\\\"ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105\\\"])        # or the project's local lib/ copy\\nfor family_folder_name in (\\\"Core\\\", \\\"Standards\\\", \\\"Taxonomies\\\", \\\"Tax_Scaffolds\\\"):   # only what you use\\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(LIBRARIES_105_ROOT / \\\"Lib_PY\\\" / family_folder_name))\\n\\nfrom lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core import (\\n    ResilientPIDLock, bejson_core_atomic_write, bejson_core_load_file,\\n    bejson_core_get_field_map, bejson_core_define_field, bejson_core_add_record_105,\\n    bejson_core_verify_105_integrity, bejson_core_transaction,\\n)\\n```\\n\\n```javascript\\n// JS (Node >= 18)\\nconst core = require(`${process.env.ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105}/Lib_JS/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core.js`);\\nconst mfdb = require(`${process.env.ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105}/Lib_JS/Core/lib_bejson_Core_mfdb_core.js`);\\n```\\n\\n```typescript\\n// TS (>= 5.0): import from the compiled/ts-path of Lib_TS/Core\\nimport { parse, serialize } from \\\"./Lib_TS/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core\\\";\\nimport { defineField, addRecord105, verify105Integrity } from \\\"./Lib_TS/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core_105\\\";\\nimport { bejson_core_get_field_map } from \\\"./Lib_TS/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_field_map\\\";\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\n# Bash (>= 4.4). jq is REQUIRED and must be installed. Source from the file's own directory.\\nsource \\\"$ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105/Lib_SH/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core.sh\\\"\\nsource \\\"$ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105/Lib_SH/Core/lib_bejson_Core_mfdb_core.sh\\\"\\n```\\n\\nEnvironment values (keys, paths) come from `source_env()` / `get_env_path()` (\\u00a78.4), not hard-coded literals.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Capability Matrix \\u2014 \\\"I Need To\\u2026 \\u2192 Call This\\\"\\n\\n\\u2705 = use it \\u00b7 \\u26a0\\ufe0f = partial/caveat \\u00b7 \\u274c = not in that language (use the stated fallback)\\n\\n| Need | Python | JavaScript | TypeScript | Bash |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| **Per-file process lock (stale-safe)** | \\u2705 `ResilientPIDLock(path, timeout_seconds)` | \\u274c none; single-process synchronous only | \\u274c none | \\u26a0\\ufe0f `resilient_lock_acquire path [timeout]` / `resilient_lock_release path` (basic: no owner token; reclaim = `kill -0` then `rm -rf`) |\\n| **Atomic file write** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_atomic_write(path, doc)` | \\u26a0\\ufe0f no public helper; temp file + `fs.renameSync` (\\u00a74.3) | \\u26a0\\ufe0f none; temp + rename | \\u2705 `bejson_core_atomic_write` |\\n| **Load a BEJSON file** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_load_file(path)` (strips untrusted cache) | \\u26a0\\ufe0f `JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(...))` | \\u2705 `parse(text)` | \\u2705 `bejson_core_load_file` |\\n| **Field-map cache (O(1) by name)** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_get_field_map(doc)` | \\u2705 `bejson_core_get_field_map` | \\u2705 `bejson_core_get_field_map` / `_105` | \\u2705 `bejson_core_get_field_map` |\\n| **Create 104/104a/104db doc** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_create_104/104a/104db` | \\u274c build the object, then validate | \\u2705 `createEmpty104/104a/104db` | \\u26a0\\ufe0f build with `jq`, then validate |\\n| **Add field to 105 doc (stamps `field_uuid`)** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_define_field` (also `bejson_core_add_field`) | \\u26a0\\ufe0f `bejson_core_add_field` only (no `define_field`) | \\u2705 `defineField` | \\u274c |\\n| **Add row to 105 doc** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_add_record_105(doc, values)` \\u2192 returns `record_uuid` | \\u2705 `bejson_core_add_record_105` \\u2192 returns `record_uuid` | \\u2705 `addRecord105` | \\u274c (use `mfdb_core_add_entity_record_105`) |\\n| **Bulk add (one cache rebuild)** | \\u2705 `with bejson_core_transaction(doc):` | \\u274c | \\u274c | \\u274c |\\n| **Update one cell by uuid + field name** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_write_cell` | \\u2705 `bejson_core_write_cell` | \\u2705 `writeCell` | \\u274c |\\n| **Delete row / remove field** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_delete_record`, `bejson_core_remove_field` | \\u2705 `bejson_core_delete_record` | \\u2705 `deleteRecord105`, `removeField` | \\u274c |\\n| **Find row by uuid** | \\u2705 via `bejson_core_resolve_field_index` / loops over map | \\u2705 `bejson_core_find_row_by_uuid` | \\u2705 `getRecord` helpers | \\u274c |\\n| **104 \\u2192 105 upgrade** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_upgrade_to_105`, `bejson_core_upgrade_dry_run`, CLI `cli/bejson_upgrade_cli.py` | \\u274c | \\u26a0\\ufe0f `upgradeTo105` (in-memory only, no backup/swap) | \\u274c |\\n| **Verify 105 integrity** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_verify_105_integrity(doc)` \\u2192 `{ok, field_count, record_count, errors}` | \\u2705 same name | \\u2705 `verify105Integrity` | \\u274c |\\n| **Full validation** | \\u2705 `validate_bejson(doc_or_path)`, `bejson_validator_validate_file`, `bejson_validator_get_report` | \\u2705 `validate_bejson`, `bejson_validator_validate_file` | \\u2705 `validateDocument`, `assertValid`, `isValid` | \\u26a0\\ufe0f `bejson_validator_validate_file` |\\n| **MFDB create database** | \\u2705 `mfdb_core_create_database` | \\u2705 `mfdb_core_create_database` | \\u26a0\\ufe0f `createManifest`, `registerEntity` | \\u2705 `mfdb_core_create_database` |\\n| **MFDB 105 record CRUD (uuid-addressed, FK-checked)** | \\u2705 `mfdb_core_add/update/delete_entity_record_105` | \\u2705 same | \\u274c no entity-record CRUD | \\u2705 same |\\n| **MFDB query / index / join** | \\u2705 `mfdb_core_query_entity`, `_build_index`, `_join` | \\u274c | \\u274c | \\u274c |\\n| **MFDB referential integrity (restrict/cascade/null)** | \\u2705 `mfdb_core_enable_ref_integrity`, `check_entity_droppable`, `mfdb_validator_check_referential_integrity` | \\u2705 same family | \\u2705 `enableRefIntegrity`, `checkFKWrite`, `checkReferentialIntegrity` | \\u2705 same family |\\n| **MFDB verify / self-heal** | \\u2705 `mfdb_core_deep_verify`, `mfdb_core_self_heal`, `mfdb_core_smart_repair` | \\u2705 `deep_verify`, `self_heal` | \\u274c | \\u274c |\\n| **MFDB debug audit log (meta entity)** | \\u2705 `mfdb_core_enable_debug`, `get_debug_log`, `get_failed_ops`, `debug_summary` | \\u2705 (Node only) | \\u2705 `enableDebug`, `getDebugLog` | \\u2705 |\\n| **Schema drift detection** | \\u2705 `mfdb_core_detect_schema_drift` | \\u2705 | \\u2705 `detectSchemaDrift` | \\u2705 |\\n| **Federation (Master/Slave dropzone)** | \\u2705 `mfdb_federation_push_config/poll_dropzone/distill_logs`, `mfdb_core_create_connected_slave_entity` | \\u2705 | \\u2705 `federationPush\\u2026` | \\u2705 |\\n| **Nested BEJSON scan/query/mutate** | \\u2705 `bejson_nesting_*` | \\u2705 `bejsonNesting*` | \\u2705 `bejsonNesting*` | \\u2705 `bejson_nesting_*` |\\n| **Directory \\u21c4 chunk archive / MFDB-132 mount/commit** | \\u2705 `bejson_core_chunking_*`, `MFDB132Archive` | \\u2705 | \\u2705 | \\u2705 `mfdb132_archive_mount/commit/unmount` |\\n| **Env sourcing (safe parse, never exec)** | \\u2705 `source_env`, `get_env_path`, `source_env_fill` | \\u274c | \\u274c | \\u2705 `bec_core_source_env` |\\n| **Path-traversal guard / zip-slip-safe extract** | \\u2705 `bejson_safe_join`, `safe_extract_zip`, `resolve_storage_path` | \\u26a0\\ufe0f `secure_zip_validate` | \\u274c | \\u274c |\\n| **Record encryption** | \\u274c | \\u26a0\\ufe0f crypto helpers internal; confirm before use **(unverified)** | \\u2705 `encryptRecord`, `decryptRecord` | \\u274c |\\n| **Structured logging into BEJSON** | \\u2705 Standards (`BEJSONLogHandler`, \\u00a78.1) | \\u274c | \\u274c | \\u274c |\\n| **Field naming / taxonomy governance** | \\u2705 Taxonomies (\\u00a78.2) | \\u274c | \\u274c | \\u274c |\\n| **Scaffolding entities & deployments** | \\u2705 Tax_Scaffolds (\\u00a78.3) | \\u274c | \\u274c | \\u274c |\\n| **SVG / canvas / animation / diagrams** | \\u2705 Graphics, Canvas (\\u00a78.6) | \\u2705 `Lib_JS/Graphics`, `Canvas` | \\u274c | \\u274c |\\n\\nWhen a task needs a \\u274c cell: **tell the user**, propose the Python tool or the stated fallback, and do not hand-roll a lookalike.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Locking & Atomic Writes \\u2014 The Part Agents Keep Missing\\n\\n### 4.1 Python: `ResilientPIDLock` (use it for every shared BEJSON write)\\nWhat it is: a mkdir-based lock directory `<path>.lockdir` containing `lock_meta.json` (`pid`, `timestamp`, `token`). It is **token-owned** (release removes only a lock you hold), **reclaims dead owners** safely under a guard directory, and checks liveness with `os.kill(pid, 0)`. It is already used inside every MFDB write in `mfdb_core`, in Taxonomies (`entity_lock`), and in Standards logging. You use it for **your own** BEJSON files:\\n\\n```python\\nwith ResilientPIDLock(\\\"data/notes.105.bejson\\\", timeout_seconds=10):\\n    doc = bejson_core_load_file(\\\"data/notes.105.bejson\\\")       # read INSIDE the lock\\n    field_map = bejson_core_get_field_map(doc)\\n    bejson_core_add_record_105(doc, [\\\"title\\\", 1])\\n    if not bejson_core_atomic_write(\\\"data/notes.105.bejson\\\", doc):\\n        raise RuntimeError(\\\"atomic write failed\\\")              # E_ATOMIC_WRITE_FAILED (15)\\n```\\nLock failure raises `OSError` (maps to `E_MFDB_CORE_LOCK_FAILED`, 53).  \\n**Do not use** `bejson_core_acquire_lock` / `bejson_core_release_lock`: they are the older, simple `.lock` directory with no stale recovery.  \\n**Do not** call `MFDB`-level functions while *also* holding a lock on the same entity file; `mfdb_core_*` already locks internally (re-entrant acquisition would deadlock until timeout).\\n\\nKnown limits (do not claim otherwise): liveness is PID-existence only, so PID reuse can keep a dead owner's lock alive until its timeout rules apply; `bejson_core_atomic_write` does not fsync the directory and writes with default temp-file permissions.\\n\\n### 4.2 Bash\\n```bash\\nresilient_lock_acquire \\\"$target_file\\\" 10 || { echo \\\"lock timeout\\\" >&2; exit 1; }\\n# ... read-modify-write ...\\nbejson_core_atomic_write \\\"$target_file\\\" \\\"$new_content_json\\\"   # 3rd arg \\\"false\\\" skips the automatic backup (default: backup on)\\nresilient_lock_release \\\"$target_file\\\"\\n```\\nThis lock has no owner token; treat it as advisory and keep critical sections short. Requires `jq`.\\n\\n### 4.3 JavaScript / TypeScript (no lock exists)\\nState plainly to the user: **JS/TS Core has no PID lock; safe only for a single writer process.** If multiple writers are possible, route writes through Python or add an external lock outside the library (and say so). For atomic replacement without a library helper:\\n```javascript\\nconst fs = require(\\\"fs\\\"), path = require(\\\"path\\\");\\nfunction atomicReplace(filePath, text) {                     // temp file + rename in the same directory\\n  const tmp = path.join(path.dirname(filePath), `.${path.basename(filePath)}.tmp_${process.pid}`);\\n  const fd = fs.openSync(tmp, \\\"w\\\"); try { fs.writeSync(fd, text); fs.fsyncSync(fd); } finally { fs.closeSync(fd); }\\n  fs.renameSync(tmp, filePath);\\n}\\n```\\n(Same-directory rename is what `mfdb_core` does internally; its `_atomicWrite` is private.)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Creating, Reading & Writing 105 Documents\\n\\n### 5.1 Python (verified)\\n```python\\ndoc = {\\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\", \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105\\\", \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n       \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"Note\\\"], \\\"Fields\\\": [], \\\"Values\\\": []}\\nbejson_core_define_field(doc, \\\"note_title\\\", \\\"string\\\")        # stamps field_uuid\\nbejson_core_define_field(doc, \\\"note_count\\\", \\\"integer\\\")\\nwith bejson_core_transaction(doc):                           # one cache rebuild for the whole batch\\n    for index in range(3):\\n        bejson_core_add_record_105(doc, [f\\\"t{index}\\\", index])    # NO record_uuid in values\\nassert bejson_core_verify_105_integrity(doc)[\\\"ok\\\"]\\n\\nfield_map = bejson_core_get_field_map(doc)                   # name -> absolute row index\\nfor row in doc[\\\"Values\\\"]:\\n    title = row[field_map[\\\"note_title\\\"]]                     # row[0] is the implicit record_uuid\\n```\\nFormat options: `105` (data), `105a` (adds custom non-underscore headers), `105db` (multi-entity). `_`-prefixed header keys are reserved and must never be written to disk.\\n\\n### 5.2 JavaScript (verified)\\n```javascript\\nconst doc = { Format: \\\"BEJSON\\\", Format_Version: \\\"105\\\", Format_Creator: \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  Records_Type: [\\\"N\\\"], Fields: [{ name: \\\"n_title\\\", type: \\\"string\\\", field_uuid: crypto.randomUUID() }], Values: [] };\\nconst recordUuid = core.bejson_core_add_record_105(doc, [\\\"x\\\"]);          // returns the uuid string\\nconst fieldMap = core.bejson_core_get_field_map(doc);                     // { n_title: 1 }\\ncore.bejson_core_verify_105_integrity(doc);                               // { ok, field_count, record_count, errors }\\n```\\n(JS has no `define_field`; generate `field_uuid` yourself with `crypto.randomUUID()` or call `bejson_core_add_field`.)\\n\\n### 5.3 TypeScript **(unverified)**\\n`defineField(doc, name, type, opts)` \\u2192 `addRecord105(doc, values)` \\u2192 `verify105Integrity(doc)`. Types live in `lib_bejson_Core_bejson_types.ts`.\\n\\n### 5.4 Upgrading 104 \\u2192 105 (Python, atomic with backup)\\n```python\\nreport = bejson_core_upgrade_dry_run(doc_104, \\\"data/orders.bejson\\\")      # never touches disk\\nupgraded = bejson_core_upgrade_to_105(doc_104, \\\"data/orders.bejson\\\")     # backs up to Backups/<name>.104.bak, atomic swap\\n```\\nCLI: `python3 Lib_PY/Core/cli/bejson_upgrade_cli.py --dry-run <file>`. On failed validation the original is untouched.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. MFDB v1.40 (Multi-File Database)\\n\\n### 6.1 Python (verified end to end)\\n```python\\nmanifest_path = mfdb_core_create_database(\\n    \\\"db\\\", \\\"OrdersDB\\\",\\n    [{\\\"name\\\": \\\"Customer\\\", \\\"file_path\\\": \\\"data/customer.bejson\\\", \\\"primary_key\\\": \\\"record_uuid\\\",\\n      \\\"format_version\\\": \\\"105\\\", \\\"fields\\\": [{\\\"name\\\": \\\"cust_name\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"}]},\\n     {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Order\\\", \\\"file_path\\\": \\\"data/order.bejson\\\", \\\"primary_key\\\": \\\"record_uuid\\\",\\n      \\\"format_version\\\": \\\"105\\\",\\n      \\\"fields\\\": [{\\\"name\\\": \\\"order_customer_fk\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n                  \\\"fk_target_entity\\\": \\\"Customer\\\", \\\"fk_on_delete\\\": \\\"cascade\\\"},   # restrict | cascade | null\\n                 {\\\"name\\\": \\\"order_total\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"integer\\\"}]}],\\n    mfdb_version=\\\"1.40\\\")                                      # writes db/104a.mfdb.bejson\\n\\nentity_doc = mfdb_core_add_entity_record_105(manifest_path, \\\"Customer\\\", [\\\"Ann\\\"])\\ncustomer_uuid = entity_doc[\\\"Values\\\"][-1][0]                    # GOTCHA: it returns the ENTITY DOC, not the uuid\\nmfdb_core_enable_ref_integrity(manifest_path)\\nmfdb_core_add_entity_record_105(manifest_path, \\\"Order\\\", [customer_uuid, 5])   # unresolvable FK raises, nothing written\\nmfdb_core_update_entity_record_105(manifest_path, \\\"Order\\\", order_uuid, \\\"order_total\\\", 9)\\nmfdb_core_delete_entity_record_105(manifest_path, \\\"Customer\\\", customer_uuid)  # cascades per fk_on_delete, two-phase, rollback on failure\\n\\nrows = mfdb_core_load_entity(manifest_path, \\\"Customer\\\")        # list of dicts incl. \\\"record_uuid\\\"\\nverdict = validate_mfdb_database(manifest_path, strict_fk=True)   # object: .valid / .errors / .warnings\\nfindings = mfdb_validator_check_referential_integrity(manifest_path)   # LIST; [] == clean\\nproblems = mfdb_core_deep_verify(manifest_path)                # LIST; [] == clean\\ndrift = mfdb_core_detect_schema_drift(manifest_path)           # DICT; {} == none\\n```\\nRules:\\n- Use the `*_105` functions for 105 entities; the non-105 CRUD functions are for 104 entities.\\n- `mfdb_core_*` functions lock internally. Do not wrap them in your own lock on the same entity file.\\n- Maintenance menu: `mfdb_core_sync_all_counts`, `mfdb_core_self_heal`, `mfdb_core_smart_repair`, `mfdb_core_check_entity_droppable` (before dropping an entity), `mfdb_core_enable_debug` (meta-GUID audit entity; read with `mfdb_core_get_failed_ops`).\\n- Shared logic in ingestion: `mfdb_core_query_entity(manifest, entity, predicate)`, `mfdb_core_build_index`, `mfdb_core_join` (Python only).\\n\\n### 6.2 JavaScript\\n`mfdb_core_create_database`, `mfdb_core_load_entity`, `mfdb_core_add/update/delete_entity_record_105`, ref-integrity enable/check, `mfdb_core_deep_verify`, `mfdb_core_self_heal`, federation and debug functions exist (names in \\u00a73). **Single-process only (no lock).** Delete is two-phase with byte-for-byte rollback.\\n\\n### 6.3 TypeScript\\n`createManifest`, `registerEntity`, `unregisterEntity`, `syncRecordCount`, ref-integrity checks, federation, debug, drift. **There is no entity-record CRUD in TS**; use Python/JS/Bash for record writes.\\n\\n### 6.4 Bash\\n`mfdb_core_create_database`, `mfdb_core_add/remove_entity_record`, `mfdb_core_add/update/delete_entity_record_105`, ref integrity, federation, debug, `mfdb_validator_validate_*`. Requires `jq`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Validation (Run It Before Delivering Any Data File)\\n\\n| Task | Python | JS | TS | Bash |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Validate a BEJSON file/doc | `validate_bejson(doc_or_path)` \\u2192 `ValidationResult`; `bejson_validator_get_report(...)` for text | `validate_bejson(doc)` / `bejson_validator_validate_file(path)` | `validateDocument(doc)` | `bejson_validator_validate_file path` |\\n| 105 strict integrity | `bejson_validator_check_105_strict_integrity(doc)` \\u2192 list | `bejson_validator_check_105_strict_integrity` | `check105StrictIntegrity` | \\u2014 |\\n| MFDB manifest / entity / database | `validate_mfdb_manifest`, `validate_mfdb_entity_file`, `validate_mfdb_database(path, strict_fk=True)` | `validate_mfdb_*` | `validateManifest`, `validateEntityFile`, `validateDatabase` | `mfdb_validator_validate_*` |\\n| Schema infer / validate against schema | `bejson_schema_extract`, `bejson_schema_validate_against(_detailed)`, `bejson_schema_infer_from_data` | \\u2014 | `BEJSONSchema` | `bejson_schema_*` |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Other Python Families (Use Before Building Your Own)\\n\\n### 8.1 Standards \\u2014 Structured Logging (Python only) \\u2014 **use this instead of ad-hoc logs**\\nWrites log rows into BEJSON 104a (schema v3) with row cap, period archives, secure-path routing, filtered export, and a standard `logging` handler. Verified:\\n```python\\nfrom lib_bejson_Standards_registry import (bejson_standards_configure_sink, BEJSONLogHandler,\\n    bejson_standards_read_logs, bejson_standards_export_logs, bejson_standards_flush,\\n    bejson_standards_audit_log_file)\\n\\nbejson_standards_configure_sink(\\\"logs/app.104a.bejson\\\", row_cap=10000, min_level=\\\"INFO\\\")\\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\\\"myapp\\\"); logger.setLevel(logging.INFO)\\nlogger.addHandler(BEJSONLogHandler(\\\"logs/app.104a.bejson\\\", subsystem=\\\"myapp\\\"))\\nlogger.info(\\\"started\\\", extra={\\\"event_key\\\": \\\"app.start\\\"})        # per-record extras: event_key, error_code, category, flags, actor, session, secure\\u2026\\nbejson_standards_flush(\\\"logs/app.104a.bejson\\\")\\nbejson_standards_audit_log_file(\\\"logs/app.104a.bejson\\\")[\\\"passed\\\"]   # True\\n```\\nExports: `bejson_standards_export_logs(path, \\\"json\\\"|\\\"ndjson\\\"|\\\"markdown\\\"|\\\"html\\\", dest)`. Stateful context (`bejson_standards_set_context`) sets subsystem/actor/session once. Redact secrets **before** logging (attach your own `logging.Filter`); the sink does not redact.\\n\\n### 8.2 Taxonomies \\u2014 Field-Naming Governance\\n`bejson_taxonomies_create_taxonomy`, `_check_field_name`, `_build_field_definition`, `_audit_database`, field-UUID ledger (`_resolve_field_uuid`, `_record_rename`, `_retire_field`), reusable field sets (`_define_field_set`, `_new_version`, `_export_field_set`). Use it when a project has a naming standard; do not hand-roll prefix/suffix checks.\\n\\n### 8.3 Tax_Scaffolds \\u2014 Entity & Deployment Scaffolding\\n`bejson_tax_scaffolds_scaffold_entity(ies)`, `_graft_field_set`, `_deploy_master_rebuild` / `_deploy_slave_rebuild`, `_capture_snapshot` + `_deploy_*_prebuilt`, `_create_federation_link` / `_bind_federation_link`, `bejson_tax_scaffolds_core_all_or_nothing(manifest)` (transaction wrapper), `bejson_tax_scaffolds_validator_audit_gate`. Use for multi-entity builds.\\n\\n### 8.4 Environment & Paths\\n- `source_env()` parses env files as **data** (never `exec`). JSON (flat object or BEJSON `Values`) or `NAME = literal` lines; names must be UPPER_SNAKE.\\n- `get_env_path(env_var, default)`, `source_env_fill(...)`.\\n- `resolve_path(...)` still honors legacy variables (`ADMIN_ROOT`, `BEJSON_STORAGE_ROOT`, `BEJSON_LIB_ROOT`). The owner's current policy lists those as **deprecated**; do not introduce them in new config.\\n- `bejson_safe_join(base, *parts)` raises `ValueError` on traversal (verified). `safe_extract_zip(zf, dest)` for archives. Use them for every user/agent-supplied path.\\n\\n### 8.5 Other Core Modules\\n| Module | Use for |\\n|---|---|\\n| `bejson_nesting_scan/query/mutate/flatten/get_path` | Hierarchical/embedded BEJSON (all four languages) |\\n| `bejson_core_chunking_*`, `MFDB132Archive` | Packaging a directory or MFDB as one document; mount/commit workflows |\\n| `parse_bejson_104db_doc`, `unpack_bejson_104db_to_dir` | 104db parsing/unpacking |\\n| `lib_bejson_Core_bejson_parse.py` (`parse_json`, `extract_data`, `save_files`) | Extracting BEJSON from model/LLM text output |\\n| `BEJSONState` | **Single 104db-file** application state with history (not MFDB); mirrors the JS class |\\n| `BEJSONProvider`, `BEJSONBackend` | Pad-style paths/index schemas and file backend; their default root is the `{ADMIN_ROOT}` placeholder, so pass an explicit root |\\n\\n### 8.6 Presentation Families\\n`Lib_PY/Graphics` (isometric cube SVG, branded slides, row-per-frame animation, GIF/WebM/APNG/MP4 compilation, diagram loader), `Lib_PY/Canvas` (canvas config/frame-sequence 105a docs, HTML templates), `Lib_PY/CMS` (MTV scaffold/validator). Use them instead of rewriting renderers. They enforce the brand palette (white/black/`#DE2626`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Error-Code Ownership (Do Not Collide)\\n\\nRanges claimed across the ecosystem (from the library's own registries):\\n\\n| Range | Owner |\\n|---|---|\\n| 1\\u201399 | Core (incl. 80 `E_FIELD_INTEGRITY_MISMATCH`, 81 `E_FORMAT_UNSUPPORTED`, 82 `E_RECORD_NOT_FOUND`, 84 `E_DUPLICATE_FIELD_NAME`, 85 `E_DUPLICATE_FIELD_UUID`; MFDB 38\\u201372, FK 39/43\\u201347, lock 53) |\\n| 90\\u2013114 | **Removed** (old 105 schema constraints). Not in use |\\n| 130\\u2013159 | Core Nesting |\\n| 160\\u2013189 | Core Extension |\\n| 200\\u2013235 | AI |\\n| 250\\u2013269 | Standards (250\\u2013261 assigned) |\\n| 270\\u2013289 | Cognition |\\n| 300\\u2013329 | BEHTML |\\n| 330\\u2013359 | BEHTML_IDE |\\n| 360\\u2013389 | Terminal_Menus |\\n| 420\\u2013449 | Diagrams |\\n| 450\\u2013479 | Bookmaker |\\n| 480\\u2013499 | Lists |\\n| 500\\u2013519 | Graphics |\\n| **Free to claim** | 190\\u2013199, 236\\u2013249, 390\\u2013419, **520+** |\\n| **Reserved by the Modern Agent Design** | **520\\u2013579** (Agent Core) |\\n\\nProcess exit codes (owner policy): `0` ok \\u00b7 `10` path-picker failure \\u00b7 `20` environment sourcing failure \\u00b7 `30` schema/field-map validation failure.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Gotchas That Cause Real Bugs\\n\\n1. **`mfdb_core_add_entity_record_105` returns the entity doc in Python** (take `doc[\\\"Values\\\"][-1][0]`) **but `{ record_uuid, doc }` in JavaScript**. Neither returns the bare uuid. (Verified by running both, 2026-11-12.)\\n2. **`bejson_core_get_field_map` indexes include the implicit `record_uuid` offset** in 105 docs (first declared field is index 1 in the row). Never mix with a manual `Fields` index.\\n3. **`mfdb_core_load_entity` returns dicts** (keyed by field name, plus `record_uuid`); the document functions return lists. Do not index one like the other.\\n4. **Validators differ in return type:** `validate_*` return result objects; `*_check_referential_integrity`, `*_deep_verify`, `*_check_105_*` return lists; drift returns a dict. Check `== []`/`{}` or `.valid` accordingly.\\n5. **FK declaration** lives on the field: `fk_target_entity` and `fk_on_delete`. Enable enforcement with `mfdb_core_enable_ref_integrity`; it is off by default.\\n6. **`_bejson_field_map` is cache only.** It is rebuilt automatically; never persist it and never trust one found in a file.\\n7. **Bulk writes:** wrap in `bejson_core_transaction(doc)`; otherwise the cache rebuilds per row.\\n8. **Locks are per-file.** Lock the exact file you write; load it **after** taking the lock.\\n9. **JS/TS: no lock; Bash: basic lock; Python: resilient lock.** State which you used.\\n10. **Bash needs `jq`.** If it is missing, say so; do not substitute `sed`/`awk` JSON handling.\\n11. **README examples are wrong** (\\u00a71). Never paste them.\\n12. **Default `mfdb_version` in `mfdb_core_create_database` is `\\\"1.31\\\"`.** Pass `mfdb_version=\\\"1.40\\\"` for the current spec.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Pre-Delivery Checklist (Run Every Time)\\n\\n- [ ] No file inside `libraries_105` was changed.\\n- [ ] No custom lock, atomic writer, field-map cache, env parser, path guard, or logger was written where \\u00a73 lists a library function.\\n- [ ] No positional indices; every access goes through `field_map[...]`.\\n- [ ] No `record_uuid` in `Fields`; no hand-typed `field_uuid`.\\n- [ ] Every shared write is inside `ResilientPIDLock` (PY) / `resilient_lock_acquire` (SH), or the single-writer limitation is stated (JS/TS).\\n- [ ] Manifest is `104a.mfdb.bejson`; `mfdb_version=\\\"1.40\\\"`; entity files are Format `105`.\\n- [ ] Output files pass `validate_bejson` / `bejson_core_verify_105_integrity`; MFDBs pass `validate_mfdb_database(..., strict_fk=True)` with `[]` from `mfdb_core_deep_verify`.\\n- [ ] New error codes come from a free range (\\u00a79), not another family's.\\n- [ ] Logging uses `logging` (+ Standards handler); no `print()`.\\n- [ ] Paths come from config/env and pass `bejson_safe_join` where user-supplied.\\n- [ ] Every \\u274c/\\u26a0\\ufe0f cell you hit was reported to the user with the fallback you chose.\\n- [ ] File headers carry Name, Description, `VERSION`, Date, Author credit, and a fresh `RELATIONAL_ID`.\\n\\n*Elton Boehnen \\u00b7 boehnenelton2024@gmail.com \\u00b7 boehnenelton2024.pages.dev \\u00b7 github.com/boehnenelton*\\n\\n\\n### new family pitch.md\\n# Standardized_Entities Library Family\\n\\n## Pitch\\n\\nBuild the **`Standardized_Entities`** library family (`Lib_PY/Standardized_Entities/` inside `libraries_105`) with its own dedicated **Core** and **Validator** modules to deploy and verify standardized BEJSON `105` MFDB entity schemas\\u2014aligned 1:1 with the Contextual Registry's 14-field universal schema\\u2014into any targeted MFDB v1.40 database. This family formally **deprecates** `Taxonomies` and `Tax_Scaffolds`.\\n\\n## The Rules\\n\\n### Two-Module Family Separation\\n\\n- **Core (`lib_bejson_Standardized_Entities_core.py`):** Owns the canonical 14-field schema template and all write operations\\u2014creating MFDB databases, writing schema-templated Format `105` entity files into targeted manifests, and constructing schema-valid rows. Imports `Lib_PY/Core` primitives (`mfdb_core`, `bejson_core`) without modifying `Core`[span_0](start_span)[span_0](end_span).\\n- **Validator (`lib_bejson_Standardized_Entities_validator.py`):** Pure read-only verification gate (never writes). Enforces a two-tier barrier of entry: first runs `Lib_PY/Core` validation (`validate_bejson` / `validate_mfdb_database`), then verifies exact 14-field `Standardized_Entities` schema conformance[span_1](start_span)[span_1](end_span).\\n\\n### Deprecation & Universal Deployment Rules\\n\\n- **Deprecated Families:** `Lib_PY/Taxonomies` and `Lib_PY/Tax_Scaffolds` are deprecated and moved to historical reference (matching the `PMS_DEPRECATED` governance pattern in `BEJSON/MFDB/data/metadata.bejson`)[span_2](start_span)[span_2](end_span). Do not import or extend them in new work.\\n- **Strict Format `105` + `104a` Manifest:** Root manifests remain `104a.mfdb.bejson` (`MFDB_Version: \\\"1.40\\\"`), and every deployed entity is a Format `105` file (`data/<entity_name_lower>.bejson`) with `Parent_Hierarchy: \\\"../104a.mfdb.bejson\\\"`, unique UUIDv4 `field_uuid`s, and native `record_uuid` at row index `0`[span_3](start_span)[span_3](end_span).\\n- **Optional Extension Columns:** Targeted databases may append extra domain-specific fields **only at the end** of the 14 base fields (never reordering, retyping, or removing the 14 base fields) so universal tooling always resolves fields via `O(1)` `field_map` lookups[span_4](start_span)[span_4](end_span).\\n\\n## Entity Schema\\n\\nEvery entity written by `Standardized_Entities` Core and verified by `Standardized_Entities` Validator carries the exact 14-field Contextual Registry schema (with BEJSON `105` reserved `record_uuid` at row index `0`, omitted from `Fields`)[span_5](start_span)[span_5](end_span):\\n\\n- **`record_uuid`** \\u2014 BEJSON 105 standard reserved UUIDv4 at row index `0`, managed automatically by `libraries_105`[span_6](start_span)[span_6](end_span).\\n- **`key`** (`string`, required) \\u2014 Deterministic, machine-friendly lookup slug unique within the entity.\\n- **`label`** (`string`, required) \\u2014 Human-readable record label.\\n- **`value`** (`string`, required) \\u2014 Core entry payload (URL, path, env value, secret reference, etc.).\\n- **`value_type`** (`string`, default `\\\"string\\\"`) \\u2014 Hint for parsing/validation (`url`, `path`, `env_var`, `secret_ref`, `json`, `text`).\\n- **`domain`** (`string`, required) \\u2014 Top-level domain the record belongs to (e.g., `URL`, `Environment`).\\n- **`category`** (`string`, required) \\u2014 Entity sub-category (e.g., `Cloudflare_Site`, `Youtube`, `Secure`).\\n- **`details`** (`string`, default `null`) \\u2014 Contextual human- and LLM-friendly notes.\\n- **`tags`** (`array`, default `null`) \\u2014 Semantic keywords for search and grouping.\\n- **`flags`** (`array`, default `null`) \\u2014 Programmatic array of tool-scoped state flags.\\n- **`context_mode`** (`string`, default `\\\"inject\\\"`) \\u2014 Agent visibility policy (`\\\"inject\\\"`, `\\\"reference\\\"`, `\\\"hidden\\\"`).\\n- **`is_active`** (`boolean`, default `true`) \\u2014 Programmatic active/soft-delete toggle.\\n- **`created_at`** (`string`, required) \\u2014 UTC ISO-8601 timestamp (`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`) stamped on creation.\\n- **`updated_at`** (`string`, default `null`) \\u2014 UTC ISO-8601 timestamp (`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`) updated on mutation.\\n- **`sender`** (`string`, default `null`) \\u2014 Optional signature of the entry creator or actor.\\n\\n### Valid BEJSON 105 Deployed Entity Example\\n\\nExample file: `data/url_cloudflare_site.bejson`\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Parent_Hierarchy\\\": \\\"../104a.mfdb.bejson\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"URL_Cloudflare_Site\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"key\\\",          \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0001-4000-8000-000000000001\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"label\\\",        \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0002-4000-8000-000000000002\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"value\\\",        \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0003-4000-8000-000000000003\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"value_type\\\",   \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0004-4000-8000-000000000004\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"domain\\\",       \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0005-4000-8000-000000000005\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"category\\\",     \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0006-4000-8000-000000000006\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"details\\\",      \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0007-4000-8000-000000000007\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"tags\\\",         \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",   \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0008-4000-8000-000000000008\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"flags\\\",        \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",   \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0009-4000-8000-000000000009\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"context_mode\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0010-4000-8000-000000000010\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"is_active\\\",    \\\"type\\\": \\\"boolean\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0011-4000-8000-000000000011\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"created_at\\\",   \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0012-4000-8000-000000000012\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"updated_at\\\",   \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0013-4000-8000-000000000013\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"sender\\\",       \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",  \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"a1b2c3d4-0014-4000-8000-000000000014\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\n      \\\"9f8e7d6c-0001-4000-8000-000000000001\\\",\\n      \\\"CLOUDFLARE_HOME\\\",\\n      \\\"Elton Boehnen Portfolio\\\",\\n      \\\"[https://boehnenelton2024.pages.dev](https://boehnenelton2024.pages.dev)\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\",\\n      \\\"URL\\\",\\n      \\\"Cloudflare_Site\\\",\\n      \\\"Primary Cloudflare Pages site and project hub\\\",\\n      [\\\"portfolio\\\", \\\"website\\\", \\\"bejson\\\"],\\n      null,\\n      \\\"inject\\\",\\n      true,\\n      \\\"2026-11-19T23:00:00Z\\\",\\n      null,\\n      \\\"elton\\\"\\n    ]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Tooling & Library API\\n\\n### Family Directory Layout\\n\\n```text\\n$ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105/Lib_PY/Standardized_Entities/\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 __init__.py\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 lib_bejson_Standardized_Entities_core.py        # Schema template + writers to targeted MFDBs\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 lib_bejson_Standardized_Entities_validator.py   # Two-tier schema & database validator (read-only)\\n\\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 tests/\\n    \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 test_standardized_entities.py\\n```\\n\\n### Core Module (`lib_bejson_Standardized_Entities_core.py`)\\n\\nOwns the canonical 14-field template and writes templated Format `105` entities to targeted MFDB databases:\\n\\n- **`STANDARDIZED_ENTITY_FIELDS`** \\u2014 Immutable tuple of the 14 canonical `(name, type, default)` definitions.\\n- **`se_core_parse_entity_name(entity_name)`** \\u2014 Validates `^[A-Za-z0-9]+_[A-Za-z0-9_]+$` and splits on the first `_` into `(domain, category)`.\\n- **`se_core_build_fields(extra_fields=None)`** \\u2014 Emits fresh Format `105` field definitions with newly minted UUIDv4 `field_uuid`s (plus any optional appended `extra_fields`).\\n- **`se_core_deploy_database(root_dir, db_name, entities, network_role=\\\"Master\\\", extra_fields_map=None)`** \\u2014 Creates a targeted MFDB v1.40 database (`104a.mfdb.bejson`) via `mfdb_core_create_database`, writes every templated Format `105` entity file, and enables referential integrity (`mfdb_core_enable_ref_integrity`)[span_7](start_span)[span_7](end_span).\\n- **`se_core_write_entity(manifest_path, entity_name, description=\\\"\\\", extra_fields=None)`** \\u2014 Writes a single schema-templated Format `105` entity file into an existing targeted MFDB manifest via `mfdb_core_create_entity_file(..., format_version=\\\"105\\\")`[span_8](start_span)[span_8](end_span) under lock and atomic write.\\n- **`se_core_build_record(entity_name, key, label, value, **kwargs)`** \\u2014 Builds a schema-valid 14-value row list (auto-deriving `domain`/`category`, defaulting `value_type=\\\"string\\\"`, `context_mode=\\\"inject\\\"`, `is_active=True`, stamping UTC `created_at`, and setting absent optionals to `null`) ready for `mfdb_core_add_entity_record_105`[span_9](start_span)[span_9](end_span).\\n\\n### Validator Module (`lib_bejson_Standardized_Entities_validator.py`)\\n\\nPure read-only integrity gate (never writes to disk):\\n\\n- **`se_validator_validate_schema(doc)`** \\u2014 Verifies that `Fields[0:14]` match `STANDARDIZED_ENTITY_FIELDS` in exact order, `name`, and `type`, and that every field carries a valid unique UUIDv4 `field_uuid`[span_10](start_span)[span_10](end_span).\\n- **`se_validator_validate_entity(entity_path_or_doc, is_file=True)`** \\u2014 Two-tier barrier:\\n  1. Runs `Lib_PY/Core` `validate_bejson(...)`[span_11](start_span)[span_11](end_span) to confirm Format `105` structural validity.\\n  2. Runs `se_validator_validate_schema(doc)` plus value-level checks (`context_mode` in `{\\\"inject\\\", \\\"reference\\\", \\\"hidden\\\"}`, non-null `key`/`label`/`value`/`domain`/`category`, valid ISO-8601 `created_at`).\\n- **`se_validator_validate_database(manifest_path, strict_fk=True)`** \\u2014 Runs `Lib_PY/Core` `validate_mfdb_database(manifest_path, strict_fk=strict_fk)`[span_12](start_span)[span_12](end_span), then runs `se_validator_validate_entity` on every registered entity and returns `{\\\"passed\\\": bool, \\\"entities\\\": [...], \\\"errors\\\": [...], \\\"warnings\\\": [...]}`.\\n\\n## `~/.gemini/gemini.md` Context Entry\\n\\n```markdown\\n<!-- STANDARDIZED_ENTITIES_CONTEXT:START -->\\n## Standardized_Entities Library (`libraries_105`)\\n\\n- **Path:** `$ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105/Lib_PY/Standardized_Entities/`\\n- **Modules:** `lib_bejson_Standardized_Entities_core.py` (writers) + `lib_bejson_Standardized_Entities_validator.py` (read-only schema/DB validator)\\n- **Replaces:** `Taxonomies` and `Tax_Scaffolds` (deprecated \\u2014 do not use in new projects).\\n- **Core Calls:**\\n  - `se_core_deploy_database(root_dir, db_name, entities=[\\\"URL_Cloudflare_Site\\\", ...])`\\n  - `se_core_write_entity(manifest_path, \\\"Domain_Category\\\")`\\n  - `se_core_build_record(\\\"Domain_Category\\\", key=..., label=..., value=...)`\\n- **Validator Calls:**\\n  - `se_validator_validate_entity(entity_path, is_file=True)`\\n  - `se_validator_validate_database(manifest_path)`\\n<!-- STANDARDIZED_ENTITIES_CONTEXT:END -->\\n```\\n\\n## Build Instructions\\n\\n1. **Create the Family Directory:** Create `$ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105/Lib_PY/Standardized_Entities/` and its `tests/` subfolder.\\n2. **Author `lib_bejson_Standardized_Entities_core.py`:** Resolve sibling `Lib_PY/Core` dynamically relative to `__file__`[span_13](start_span)[span_13](end_span). Define `STANDARDIZED_ENTITY_FIELDS`, `se_core_parse_entity_name`, `se_core_build_fields`, `se_core_deploy_database`, `se_core_write_entity`, and `se_core_build_record` using `mfdb_core_create_database`, `mfdb_core_create_entity_file(..., format_version=\\\"105\\\")`, `mfdb_core_enable_ref_integrity`, and `bejson_core_atomic_write`[span_14](start_span)[span_14](end_span).\\n3. **Author `lib_bejson_Standardized_Entities_validator.py`:** Import `validate_bejson` and `validate_mfdb_database` from `Lib_PY/Core`[span_15](start_span)[span_15](end_span). Implement `se_validator_validate_schema`, `se_validator_validate_entity`, and `se_validator_validate_database` as pure read-only checks.\\n4. **Export Public Surface in `__init__.py`:** Re-export all `se_core_*`, `se_validator_*` functions, and `STANDARDIZED_ENTITY_FIELDS`.\\n5. **Record Deprecation in Metadata:** Add `TAXONOMIES_DEPRECATED = \\\"true\\\"` (`RECORD_TYPE = \\\"RULE\\\"`) in `BEJSON/MFDB/data/metadata.bejson` and log the change in `BEJSON/MFDB/data/library_changes.bejson`[span_16](start_span)[span_16](end_span).\\n6. **Run Verification Suite:**\\n   ```bash\\n   python3 $ADMIN_LIBRARIES_105/Lib_PY/Standardized_Entities/tests/test_standardized_entities.py\\n   ```\\n   Confirm tests pass for: deploying a fresh MFDB, writing a templated entity into an existing MFDB, appending optional extension columns, inserting records via `se_core_build_record` + `mfdb_core_add_entity_record_105`, and catching missing/reordered/mistyped fields in `se_validator_validate_database`.\\n\\n\\n\\n### PROGRESS_AI_CORE.md\\n# AI_Core urgent-instructions progress (2026-11-17) \\u2014 PARTIAL\\n\\nDONE\\n- Lib_PY/AI renamed to Lib_PY/AI_Core; all modules now lib_bejson_AI_Core_<module>.py (errors: lib_bejson_AI_Core_errors.py). 49/49 existing tests pass.\\n- Core dependency = BEJSON read/write only, via lib_bejson_AI_Core_core_bridge.py (config, key-rotator state, 105 envelopes now go through Core).\\n- Validator: lib_bejson_AI_Core_validator.py (naming, headers, VERSION sync, no print, no abs paths, no key in URL, stdlib-only, Core only via bridge, logger present) + SHA-256 freeze manifest (not frozen yet). AI_Core: 0 violations.\\n- Plugin registry + schema: plugin_schema / plugin_base / plugin_registry (pre, post, render; gated by new plugins_enabled setting; hooked into dispatcher). Error codes 236-244 (AI now owns 200-249).\\n- Lib_PY/AI_Extensions: 3 sample plugins with SHA-pinned extension.105a.bejson manifests + manifest tool.\\n- 105 profiles: lib_bejson_AI_Core_profiles.py (create/validate/update, 104->105 converter, never overwrites source). Ported runtime to AI_Extensions/lib_bejson_AI_Extensions_profile_runtime.py.\\n\\nDONE (Continued)\\n- AI_Extensions profile_compiler and profile_abstraction ported and verified.\\n- AI_Core and AI_Extensions test suites (103 total tests: 82 AI_Core + 21 AI_Extensions) passing 100%.\\n- BEJSON/ self-tracking MFDB ledger synchronized: families.bejson (AI_Core + AI_Extensions), metadata.bejson (5.9.11), library_changes.bejson, and 104a.mfdb.bejson validated clean.\\n- AI_Core and AI_Extensions README.md documentation refreshed.\\n- Both families verified with 0 violations via lib_bejson_AI_Core_validator.py.\\n\\n\\n\\n### README.md\\n# BEJSON 105 Core & Integrity Era Library Ecosystem (`libraries_105`)\\n\\n![BEJSON 105 Ecosystem Banner](images/banners/banner_420.png)\\n*_Figure 1: BEJSON 105 Integrity Era Architecture & Multi-Language Core_*\\n\\n> **The Official Reference Implementation for BEJSON Integrity Era Formats (`105`, `105a`, `105db`), 104 Baseline Parity, Atomic Migration Tooling, and MFDB v1.40 Multi-File Database Referential Integrity across Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, and Bash.**\\n\\n**Author Credit:** Elton Boehnen \\u00b7 boehnenelton2024@gmail.com \\u00b7 boehnenelton2024.pages.dev \\u00b7 github.com/boehnenelton  \\n**License:** PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0 (`PolyForm-Noncommercial-1.0.0`)  \\n\\n---\\n\\n## Verified Benchmark & Stress Test Results (`Lib_PY/Metrics` \\u00b7 `10/10 PASS`)\\n\\nFull interactive metrics showcase: [`dev/metrics/index.html`](dev/metrics/index.html) \\u00b7 Full consolidated report: [`dev/metrics/results.md`](dev/metrics/results.md) \\u00b7 Automated pipeline: [`Lib_PY/Metrics/lib_bejson_Metrics_pipeline.py`](Lib_PY/Metrics/lib_bejson_Metrics_pipeline.py)\\n\\n| Comparison / Workload | Measured Result | Detail |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **BEJSON 105 vs SQLite (Disk Point Queries)** | **BEJSON 105 was 80.41x faster than SQLite** | BEJSON 105 executed `3,417,769 ops/sec` (`0.29 \\u00b5s/query`) vs SQLite disk B-tree at `42,504 ops/sec` (`23.53 \\u00b5s/query`). |\\n| **BEJSON 105 vs SQLite (`:memory:` 500k Queries)** | **BEJSON 105 was 8.24x faster than in-memory SQLite** | BEJSON 105 completed 500,000 lookups at `1.76 M ops/sec` (`~285 ms`) vs SQLite `:memory:` at `0.22 M ops/sec` (`~2,380 ms`). |\\n| **BEJSON 105 vs Standard JSON (LLM Tokens)** | **BEJSON 105 used 68.52% fewer tokens than JSON** | On 5,000 rows, BEJSON 105 used `226,878 tokens` (`873 KB`) vs `720,726 tokens` (`2.77 MB`) for Standard JSON (`493,847` fewer tokens). On 10,000 rows, BEJSON 105 used `287,886 tokens` vs `854,722 tokens` (`-66.32%`). |\\n| **BEJSON 105 Wide-Matrix Access (96,000 Cells)** | **BEJSON 105: `24.40 M ops/sec`** | Across 64 columns \\u00d7 1,500 rows, O(1) Field-Map lookups sustained `24.40 M ops/sec` with `0%` positional slowdown between column 0 and column 63. |\\n| **BEJSON 105 High-Scale Bulk Writes** | **BEJSON 105 wrote `68.25k rows/sec`** | Wrote `15,000` UUID-stamped rows in `220 ms` + validated schema and 105 integrity in `130 ms`. Retained `89%` write throughput scaling from `25,000` (`53.27k rec/s`) to `60,000` (`47.65k rec/s`) records at `35.85 MB` peak RSS. |\\n| **Automated Test & Validation Suite** | **`10/10 PASS` (`1,582.92 ms` total)** | 100% passing across `TestMetricsFamily` (4), `TestStandardizedBEJSONAndMFDB` (2), and `TestBEJSONStressAndComparisons` (4). |\\n\\n![BEJSON 105 vs SQLite Point Lookup Throughput](dev/metrics/visual_audit/svgs/01_ops_throughput.svg)\\n*_Figure 1a: BEJSON 105 O(1) Field-Map Point Lookup Throughput vs SQLite Baseline_*\\n\\n![BEJSON 105 vs Standard JSON LLM Token Footprint](dev/metrics/visual_audit/svgs/02_token_savings.svg)\\n*_Figure 1b: BEJSON 105 vs Standard JSON LLM Token Footprint Across 254-Entity, 5k-Row, and 10k-Row Workloads_*\\n\\n![BEJSON 105 Metrics Visual Showcase (Desktop)](dev/metrics/visual_audit/screenshots/index_desktop.png)\\n*_Figure 1c: Full Playwright-Verified Metrics Visual Showcase (`dev/metrics/index.html`)*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## The BEJSON Manifesto: Debunk Me If You Can\\n\\n![Debunk Challenge](images/manifesto/slide_01_debunk_challenge.png)  \\n*_Figure 2: The End of Modern Data Infrastructure Bloat: AI-Native Relational Flat-File Architecture_*\\n\\n### The Live Empirical Verification Challenge\\n\\nFeed this exact prompt into Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, or DeepSeek right now:\\n\\n```text\\nPlease fact check the claims made on the following GitHub repositories. Is this legit?\\nhttps://github.com/Boehnenelton\\nhttps://github.com/Boehnenelton/BEJSON_105\\n```\\n\\n![Verification Prompt](images/manifesto/slide_10_verification_prompt.png)  \\n*_Figure 3: Live Verification Prompt: No Gimmicks, No Cloud Paywalls, Free and Empirically Verifiable_*\\n\\n---\\n\\n### The Data Bloat Scam: 65%\\u201395% Dead Weight\\n\\n![Data Bloat Scam](images/manifesto/slide_02_data_bloat_scam.png)  \\n*_Figure 4: Legacy Array-of-Objects JSON vs. BEJSON Positional Matrix_*\\n\\nModern software data infrastructure is plagued by unnecessary complexity: multi-gigabyte Docker daemons, complex connection pools, ORM compilation overhead, and repetitive dictionary keys that inflate LLM prompt tokens and network payloads. \\n\\nStandard JSON forces every single record in an array of objects to repeat its keys (e.g., `{\\\"first_name\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"last_name\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"status\\\": \\\"...\\\"}`). Across thousands of rows, these repetitive dictionary keys consume 65% to 95% of the payload in pure overhead. This forces LLMs to hallucinate misspelled object keys and burn millions of characters on structural boilerplate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Core Architectural Features & Commercial Selling Points\\n\\n#### 1. Extreme Token Economics & LLM Efficiency\\n![Extreme Token Economics](images/manifesto/slide_03_token_economics.png)  \\n*_Figure 5: Token Savings Curve: Approaching Zero Overhead as Row Count Scales_*\\n\\n* **The Elimination of Schema Repetition**: Standard JSON forces every single record in an array of objects to repeat its keys (e.g., `{\\\"first_name\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"last_name\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"status\\\": \\\"...\\\"}`). Across thousands of rows, these repetitive dictionary keys consume 65% to 95% of the payload in pure overhead.\\n* **Separation of Declarations from Data**: BEJSON isolates column metadata once in the top-level `Fields` array and streams records as raw positional vectors in `Values`.\\n* **Zero Key-Spelling Hallucinations**: By stripping repetitive keys out of the prompt payload, LLMs cannot hallucinate misspelled object keys during generation, data extraction, or function calling.\\n* **Direct Financial ROI on Cloud Inference**: A 65%\\u201395% reduction in input token volume directly translates into proportional cost savings across commercial model APIs (Claude, OpenAI, Gemini, DeepSeek), while fitting up to 10\\u00d7 more real data into the exact same context window.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 2. True Zero-SQL & Zero-Daemon Footprint (<50MB RAM)\\n![Zero-Daemon Footprint](images/manifesto/slide_04_zero_daemon_footprint.png)  \\n*_Figure 6: POSIX Flat-File Grounding: Sub-50MB Memory Guarantee on Constrained Hardware_*\\n\\n* **No Background Database Servers**: Traditional relational architectures require continuous daemon processes (e.g., `mysqld`, `postgres`, or embedded SQLite locks) that idle with hundreds of megabytes or gigabytes of resident memory.\\n* **Flat-File POSIX Grounding**: The entire relational model lives in standard, human-readable flat files on disk. If you have POSIX filesystem access, you have a fully functional relational database.\\n* **On-Demand LRU Table Paging (`bejson_pagecache`)**: Even when federating thousands of tables in an MFDB database, the system never slurps the entire database into memory. Tables are loaded into an LRU cache only when read or mutated, keeping total system RAM **strictly under 50 MB**\\u2014making it ideal for battery-powered, edge-compute hardware (such as Android Termux ARM64) and serverless functions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 3. Million-Entity MFDB Federation & Referential Integrity\\n![Million-Entity MFDB Federation](images/manifesto/slide_05_million_entity_mfdb.png)  \\n*_Figure 7: Horizontal Relational Scaling Across 1,000,000+ Discrete Entity Files_*\\n\\n* **Horizontal Relational Scaling**: Using a central manifest (`104a.mfdb.bejson` / `105a.mfdb.bejson`), the Multi-File Database engine orchestrates anywhere from 2 tables to over **1,000,000+ discrete entity files** into a unified relational graph.\\n* **Phase 6 Enforced Constraints**: Unlike raw JSON or flat CSVs, MFDB v1.40 enforces real-time relational foreign key (`_fk`) integrity. Writes verify target primary keys before committing to disk.\\n* **Deterministic Cascade Engines**: Supports active `ON DELETE CASCADE`, `RESTRICT` (with cycle protection), and `SET NULL` policies. Cascades execute via a two-phase \\\"plan-then-commit\\\" traversal: if any restriction fails or a circular cascade is detected, the entire operation halts with zero disk mutations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 4. Cryptographic Provenance & Tamper Gates (Integrity Era 105)\\n![Cryptographic Provenance & Tamper Gates](images/manifesto/slide_06_cryptographic_provenance.png)  \\n*_Figure 8: Per-Record SHA-256 Digesting & System-Minted Column-0 UUIDv4_*\\n\\n* **System-Minted UUIDv4 at Column 0**: Every row in a 105-series table receives an immutable primary key (`record_uuid`) minted at column 0, providing collision-free distributed identity across distributed edge nodes.\\n* **Field-Level UUID Fingerprints**: Every declared column in `Fields` carries its own `field_uuid`, ensuring schema migrations and column renames can be tracked unambiguously across versions.\\n* **Per-Record SHA-256 Digesting**: Every row can validate an accompanying SHA-256 digest calculated over its serialized field values. If an external process or malicious actor alters a single character in the flat file, validation gates (Errors `E90`\\u2013`E95`) immediately reject the file before execution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 5. Zero-ORM, Constant-Time Memory Access\\n![Zero-ORM Constant-Time Memory Access](images/manifesto/slide_07_constant_time_memory.png)  \\n*_Figure 9: O(1) Field-Map Cache: Eliminating Hardcoded Numeric Indexing_*\\n\\n* **Self-Maintaining O(1) Field-Map Cache**: The ecosystem bans fragile, hardcoded integer array indexing (`row[0]`, `row[1]`). Runtimes automatically compile an in-memory field-to-index map (`by_name_row`), allowing developers to query rows using human-readable names (`row[fmap[\\\"user_id\\\"]]`) with the raw execution speed of direct array indexing.\\n* **Non-Enumerable In-Memory Binding**: In JavaScript and TypeScript runtimes, the cache binds to documents as a hidden, non-enumerable property or `WeakMap`, ensuring `JSON.stringify()` never pollutes the persisted flat-file payload.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 6. POSIX Crash-Proof Atomic Commits\\n![POSIX Crash-Proof Atomic Commits](images/manifesto/slide_08_atomic_commits.png)  \\n*_Figure 10: Two-Phase Staged Writes: Stage -> Flush (fsync) -> Replace_*\\n\\n* **Two-Phase Staged Writes**: Disk writes never mutate files in place. Updates stage to a process-isolated temporary file (`.tmp.<pid>`), flush to physical storage blocks via `os.fsync()`, and finalize through an atomic POSIX rename (`os.replace()`).\\n* **Protection Against Silent Corruption**: A power loss, kernel panic, or sudden container termination cannot produce half-written or corrupted JSON files.\\n* **Dead-Lock Reclaiming (`ResilientPIDLock`)**: File-level locks use atomic directory primitives (`.lockdir`) containing PID metadata. If a process crashes unexpectedly, subsequent workers verify PID liveliness, reclaim the stale lock safely, and prevent deadlocks without manual administrative intervention.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 7. 100% Polyglot Runtime Parity (Zero External Dependencies)\\n* **Four Native Runtimes**: Every core data structure, parser, validator, and MFDB algorithm is implemented with identical behavior and error registries across:\\n  * **Python 3.10+** (standard library only: `json`, `hashlib`, `uuid`, `os`, `pathlib`)\\n  * **JavaScript** (ES2022 native)\\n  * **TypeScript 5.x** (strongly typed interfaces)\\n  * **POSIX Bash 4.4/5.x** (pure shell automation with file-backed subshell caching)\\n* **Zero Supply-Chain Risk**: Requires no third-party package managers, no NPM dependencies, no PyPI wheels, and no native C extensions. Drop the library folder into your project root and it runs out of the box.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 8. Strict Terminal Discipline: The 57-Column Mobile Portrait Ceiling\\n* **Engineered for Extreme Mobility**: Every CLI dashboard, diagnostic tool, service launcher, and log stream adheres to a hard 57-column line-width ceiling.\\n* **No Broken Layouts on Mobile Viewports**: Guarantees clean, wrap-free rendering across Android Termux smartphone screens, narrow split-pane IDE terminals, and serial consoles without horizontal scrolling.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### The Diagnostic Matrix\\n\\n![The Diagnostic Matrix](images/manifesto/slide_09_diagnostic_matrix.png)  \\n*_Figure 11: Empirical Architecture Comparison: Legacy Stack vs. BEJSON + MFDB 105_*\\n\\n| Architectural Dimension | Legacy Stack (JSON + SQL + ORM) | BEJSON + MFDB 105 Ecosystem |\\n|:---|:---|:---|\\n| **RAM Footprint** | 500 MB \\u2013 4 GB+ (Continuous Idle Daemons) | **< 50 MB** (On-Demand LRU Paging) |\\n| **Token Overhead** | 65% \\u2013 95% Wasted on Repeating Keys | **Approaches 0%** (Positional Vectors in `Values`) |\\n| **Hallucination Risk** | High (Unstructured schema key spelling) | **Zero** (Schema declared once at document head) |\\n| **Setup & Dependencies** | Heavy (Engine daemons, drivers, ORMs) | **Zero** (Pure POSIX standard library flat files) |\\n| **Data Integrity** | External (Requires DB server locks) | **Built-in** (Per-record SHA-256 + Atomic POSIX commits) |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Table of Contents\\n- [The BEJSON Manifesto: Debunk Me If You Can](#the-bejson-manifesto-debunk-me-if-you-can)\\n  - [The Live Empirical Verification Challenge](#the-live-empirical-verification-challenge)\\n  - [The Data Bloat Scam: 65%\\u201395% Dead Weight](#the-data-bloat-scam-6595-dead-weight)\\n  - [Core Architectural Features & Commercial Selling Points](#core-architectural-features--commercial-selling-points)\\n  - [The Diagnostic Matrix](#the-diagnostic-matrix)\\n- [Section 1: Anti-Bloat & LLM Fact-Check](#section-1-anti-bloat--llm-fact-check)\\n- [Section 2: The AI-Native Database Paradigm](#section-2-the-ai-native-database-paradigm)\\n- [Section 3: AI Quick-Start & Autonomous Agents](#section-3-ai-quick-start--autonomous-agents)\\n- [Section 4: Token Economics & Memory Optimization](#section-4-token-economics--memory-optimization)\\n- [Section 5: Ecosystem Feature Matrix](#section-5-ecosystem-feature-matrix)\\n- [Section 6: BEJSON 105 & Cryptographic Provenance](#section-6-bejson-105--cryptographic-provenance)\\n- [Section 7: MFDB Federation Engine](#section-7-mfdb-federation-engine)\\n- [Section 8: O(1) Cache & POSIX Writes](#section-8-o1-cache--posix-writes)\\n- [Section 9: 4-Language Polyglot SDK](#section-9-4-language-polyglot-sdk)\\n- [Section 10: Platform I: BEJSON_CMS](#section-10-platform-i-bejson_cms)\\n- [Section 11: Platform II: BKS-1 AI Writer](#section-11-platform-ii-bks-1-ai-writer)\\n- [Section 12: Platform III: Multi-AI Provider Gateway (`Lib_PY/AI` v1.5.0) & Edge Inference](#section-12-platform-iii-multi-ai-provider-gateway-lib_pyai-v150--edge-inference)\\n- [Section 13: Platform IV: Project Chunker](#section-13-platform-iv-project-chunker)\\n- [Section 14: Visual Systems & 57-Col TUI](#section-14-visual-systems--57-col-tui)\\n- [Section 15: Validation & Security (E01\\u2013E95 & 200\\u2013235)](#section-15-validation--security-e01e95--200235)\\n- [Section 16: FAQ & Governance](#section-16-faq--governance)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 1: Anti-Bloat & LLM Fact-Check\\n\\nModern software data infrastructure is plagued by unnecessary complexity: multi-gigabyte Docker daemons, complex connection pools, ORM compilation overhead, and repetitive dictionary keys that inflate LLM prompt tokens and network payloads. \\n\\nBEJSON is a zero-dependency, human-readable flat-file relational database architecture. It operates entirely on standard POSIX primitives, requires no background servers or daemons, and enforces an RSS memory ceiling under 50 MB. \\n\\nWhile conventional JSON was designed primarily for human-readable key-value serialization, BEJSON was engineered specifically for machine readability, AI token efficiency, and deterministic agent reasoning while maintaining clean human legibility. Token consumption drops by 65% to 95% compared to repetitive-key JSON matrices.\\n\\n### Live LLM Fact-Check Challenge\\nTo independently audit and verify these architectural specifications, provide your LLM with the following prompt:\\n\\n```text\\nPlease fact check the claims made on the following GitHub repositories, is this legit?\\nhttps://github.com/Boehnenelton\\nhttps://github.com/Boehnenelton/BEJSON_105\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 2: The AI-Native Database Paradigm\\n\\nTraditional databases fail AI coding agents in two fundamental ways:\\n1. **Key Hallucination & Attention Drift:** When feeding large datasets formatted as arrays of key-value objects (`[{\\\"name\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"age\\\": ...}, ...]`), LLM attention mechanisms repeatedly allocate positional weights to identical dictionary keys, inducing token bloat and hallucination across long context windows.\\n2. **Schema Divergence:** Dynamic document stores lack rigid structural contracts, causing autonomous agents to invent non-existent column fields.\\n\\nBEJSON resolves this through a strict **Positional Matrix Design**:\\n- A single schema header declares field metadata (`Fields: [{\\\"name\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"...\\\"}]`).\\n- All data instances are emitted strictly as a two-dimensional positional array (`Values: [[...], [...]]`).\\n- In Format 105, column index `0` is reserved exclusively for the RFC 4122 UUIDv4 `record_uuid`.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"Format\\\": \\\"BEJSON\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Version\\\": \\\"105\\\",\\n  \\\"Format_Creator\\\": \\\"Elton Boehnen\\\",\\n  \\\"Records_Type\\\": [\\\"AuthorRecord\\\"],\\n  \\\"Fields\\\": [\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"record_uuid\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"author_id\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"e3b0c442-98fc-1c14-9afb-4c72e04f0001\\\"},\\n    {\\\"name\\\": \\\"author_name\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"field_uuid\\\": \\\"e3b0c442-98fc-1c14-9afb-4c72e04f0002\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Values\\\": [\\n    [\\\"11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111\\\", \\\"auth_01\\\", \\\"Ada Lovelace\\\"]\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 3: AI Quick-Start & Autonomous Agents\\n\\nBecause BEJSON and MFDB were engineered for machines and AI models to read, join, and mutate without structural hallucination, the fastest way to verify the architecture is to hand instructions directly to your AI agent.\\n\\n### 3.1 Option A: One-Shot Autonomous Build & Benchmark via `agy -p`\\nRun this command in any empty directory to have the Antigravity CLI (`agy -p`) clone `BEJSON_105`, verify all 4 language cores, build a multi-entity MFDB v1.40 database alongside a 105db bundle, verify cross-entity `ON DELETE CASCADE`, and benchmark token/RAM savings:\\n\\n```bash\\nagy -p '\\nYou are an autonomous systems architect operating under Elton Boehnen BEJSON 105 and MFDB v1.40 standards. Execute these 5 stages sequentially in the current workspace:\\n\\n1. CLONE & VERIFY CORE:\\n   Clone https://github.com/Boehnenelton/BEJSON_105.git into ./libraries_105 (if not present).\\n   Run: python3 libraries_105/Lib_PY/Core/tests/test_mfdb_spec_levels_suite.py\\n\\n2. ARCHITECT MULTI-ENTITY MFDB v1.40 FEDERATION + 105db BUNDLE:\\n   Create ./demo_mfdb/ and add ./libraries_105/Lib_PY/Core to sys.path.\\n   Create master manifest demo_mfdb/105a.mfdb.bejson (Format_Version=\\\"105a\\\", Network_Role=\\\"Master\\\", Ref_Integrity=true).\\n   Federate discrete Format 105 entity tables under demo_mfdb/entities/ (each with Parent_Hierarchy=\\\"../105a.mfdb.bejson\\\", field_uuid on every field, and record_uuid at col 0):\\n     a) entities/organizations.105.bejson (PK: record_uuid)\\n     b) entities/clusters.105.bejson (PK: record_uuid, FK: org_fk -> organizations [on_delete: \\\"cascade\\\"])\\n     c) entities/services.105.bejson (PK: record_uuid, FK: cluster_fk -> clusters [on_delete: \\\"cascade\\\"])\\n     d) entities/deployments.105.bejson (PK: record_uuid, FK: service_fk -> services [on_delete: \\\"restrict\\\"])\\n   Create a single-file bundle demo_mfdb/payloads/page_bundle.105db.bejson (Format_Version=\\\"105db\\\") using Record_Type_Parent to store [\\\"PageMeta\\\", \\\"Content\\\"] rows inside one file\\u2014proving the exact difference between a 105db single-file bundle and an MFDB multi-entity federation.\\n\\n3. PROVE O(1) FIELD-MAP LOOKUPS & MFDB v1.40 CASCADES:\\n   Write demo_mfdb/benchmark.py using ONLY O(1) Field-Map Cache lookups (never hardcode integer column indices) and two-phase atomic writes (.tmp.<pid> -> fsync -> os.replace).\\n   Demonstrate Error [43] block on \\\"restrict\\\" delete, then clear the restriction and prove \\\"cascade\\\" deletion across Organizations -> Clusters -> Services.\\n\\n4. BENCHMARK MULTI-ENTITY MFDB SCALING, TOKEN SAVINGS (65%-95%) & <50MB RAM:\\n   Register 250 discrete entity tables in the MFDB manifest and benchmark on-demand entity queries plus a 4,000-row dataset comparing Standard Repetitive-Key JSON vs BEJSON 105.\\n   Print an ASCII table showing: Federated Entity Count, Cascade Check, Byte/Token Reduction % (65% to 95%), and Peak RSS Memory (<50 MB).\\n\\n5. EXECUTE:\\n   Run python3 demo_mfdb/benchmark.py and display the output.\\n'\\n```\\n\\n### 3.2 Option B: `CLAUDE.md` Drop-In Contract\\nSave the block below as `CLAUDE.md` in your workspace root and instruct your agent: `claude \\\"Execute the Quick Start Verification & Benchmark in CLAUDE.md\\\"`:\\n\\n```markdown\\n# CLAUDE.md \\u2014 BEJSON 105 & MFDB v1.40 Workspace Contract\\n\\n## Mandatory Architectural Laws\\n1. Zero-SQL, Zero-Dependency Core: Use Python 3.10+ stdlib (json, hashlib, uuid, os, pathlib) with libraries_105 / libraries_104. Never install SQLite, Postgres, or ORMs.\\n2. MFDB Multi-Entity Federation vs. 104db/105db Separation:\\n   - MFDB (104a.mfdb.bejson / 105a.mfdb.bejson): Multi-entity relational database federating 2 to 1,000,000+ discrete .104.bejson / .105.bejson entity files via Parent_Hierarchy and _fk foreign keys (cascade, restrict, null). Load only active entity files on demand to keep RAM < 50 MB.\\n   - 104a / 105a: Primitive-only configs, schema registries, timelines, and MFDB manifests (Record_Type_Parent is forbidden).\\n   - 104db / 105db: Single-file multi-record bundles using Record_Type_Parent as a row discriminator inside one file (or hybrid metadata + ---BODY--- prose payloads).\\n3. O(1) Field-Map Cache Rule: Never hardcode column indices (row[0]). Always resolve via fmap = bejson_core_get_field_map(doc) -> row[fmap[\\\"field_name\\\"]].\\n4. Positional Matrix & Two-Phase Atomic Writes: Emit rows strictly as positional arrays in Values. Always write via .tmp.<pid> -> os.fsync() -> os.replace().\\n5. Pre-Flight Validation: Validate all tables and manifests with lib_bejson_Core_bejson_validator and lib_bejson_Core_mfdb_validator (E01\\u2013E95).\\n\\n## Quick Start Task\\n1. Clone https://github.com/Boehnenelton/BEJSON_105.git into ./libraries_105 and run the Lib_PY, Lib_JS, Lib_TS, and Lib_SH test suites.\\n2. Scaffold a multi-entity 105a.mfdb.bejson federation (200+ entity tables + _fk cascades) and a 105db single-file payload bundle.\\n3. Print a benchmark comparing Standard JSON vs. BEJSON 105 bytes, tokens (65%\\u201395% saved), and peak RAM (<50 MB).\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 4: Token Economics & Memory Optimization\\n\\nRepetitive dictionary keys in conventional JSON arrays create exponential context bloat:\\n\\n$$\\\\text{Bloat} = N_{\\\\text{rows}} \\\\times \\\\sum_{i=1}^{k} (\\\\text{Length}(\\\\text{Key}_i) + \\\\text{Overhead})$$\\n\\nIn BEJSON, schema keys are emitted exactly once in the `Fields` array. As row count scales, token savings approach asymptotic limits:\\n\\n| Dataset Row Count | Standard JSON Tokens | BEJSON 105 Tokens | Token Savings % | Peak RSS Memory |\\n|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|\\n| **100 rows** | 5,420 tokens | 1,890 tokens | **65.1%** | < 12 MB |\\n| **1,000 rows** | 54,200 tokens | 14,800 tokens | **72.7%** | < 18 MB |\\n| **10,000 rows** | 542,000 tokens | 98,200 tokens | **81.9%** | < 28 MB |\\n| **100,000 rows** | 5,420,000 tokens | 712,000 tokens | **86.9%** | < 45 MB |\\n\\nThrough chunked file stream processing and on-demand MFDB entity loading, memory usage stays guaranteed under 50 MB regardless of total entity count.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 5: Ecosystem Feature Matrix\\n\\n| Format Specification | Primary Purpose | Row Discriminator | Primary Keys (`record_uuid`) | Foreign Keys (`_fk`) |\\n|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|\\n| **`104` / `105`** | Homogeneous Single Entity Table | Disallowed | Col 0 (`105` only) | Supported |\\n| **`104a` / `105a`** | Flat Primitive Configs & Manifests | Disallowed | Optional | Disallowed |\\n| **`104db` / `105db`** | Multi-Record Single-File Bundle | Col 0 (`Record_Type_Parent`) | Optional | Optional |\\n| **`MFDB` (`.mfdb.bejson`)**| Multi-Entity Relational Federation | Disallowed | Mandatory | Mandatory (`cascade`, `restrict`, `null`) |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 6: BEJSON 105 & Cryptographic Provenance\\n\\nFormat 105 introduces the **Integrity Era** invariants:\\n- **UUIDv4 Primary Keys:** Every field in `Fields` declares an explicit `field_uuid`, and every row in `Values` possesses an RFC 4122 UUIDv4 at index 0 (`record_uuid`).\\n- **Cryptographic Provenance:** Tamper-evident record hashes ensure that row mutations cannot occur silently across distributed nodes.\\n- **Atomic 104-to-105 Migration:** Automated upgrade tools convert legacy 104 stores into 105 tables by minting deterministic UUIDs, validating types, and performing atomic filesystem replacements.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 7: MFDB Federation Engine\\n\\nThe Multi-File Database (MFDB v1.40) federates multiple discrete entity files under a unified declarative manifest:\\n- **Scalability:** Supports from 2 up to 1,000,000+ distinct entity files without database engine lock-in.\\n- **Topologies:** Supports `Master`, `Slave`, and `Standalone` network roles.\\n- **Referential Integrity Actions:**\\n  - `restrict`: Halts deletion if foreign child rows reference the target primary key (Error `[43]`).\\n  - `cascade`: Recursively deletes referencing rows across linked entity tables.\\n  - `null`: Sets referencing foreign keys to `null`.\\n- **Bidirectional Header Handshake:** Entities must declare `Parent_Hierarchy` pointing back to the manifest, and manifests must declare each entity path.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 8: O(1) Cache & POSIX Writes\\n\\n### O(1) Field-Map Cache Rule\\nApplication logic must never hardcode numerical column indices (e.g., `row[2]`). Instead, systems compile an O(1) field-map cache at load time:\\n\\n```python\\nfield_map = bejson_core_105_get_field_map(doc)\\nauthor_name = row[field_map[\\\"author_name\\\"]]\\n```\\n\\n### Two-Phase POSIX Atomic Writes\\nData corruption during sudden process termination is eliminated by enforcing POSIX two-phase atomic file writes:\\n1. Write payload to temporary staging file: `<filepath>.tmp.<pid>`\\n2. Flush OS buffers to disk: `os.fsync(fd)`\\n3. Atomically replace target: `os.replace(tmp_path, target_path)`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 9: 4-Language Polyglot SDK\\n\\n### Python (`Lib_PY/Core`)\\n```python\\nfrom lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core_105 import (\\n    bejson_core_105_parse_document,\\n    bejson_core_105_get_field_map\\n)\\nfrom lib_bejson_Core_mfdb_validator import (\\n    mfdb_validator_check_referential_integrity\\n)\\n\\ndoc = bejson_core_105_parse_document(\\\"data/orders.105.bejson\\\")\\nfmap = bejson_core_105_get_field_map(doc)\\n\\nfor row in doc[\\\"Values\\\"]:\\n    print(f\\\"Order: {row[fmap['order_id']]} -> Customer: {row[fmap['customer_fk']]}\\\")\\n\\nreport = mfdb_validator_check_referential_integrity(\\\"105a.mfdb.bejson\\\")\\nassert report[\\\"is_valid\\\"]\\n```\\n\\n### JavaScript (`Lib_JS/Core`)\\n```javascript\\nconst { bejson_core_parse_bejson, bejson_core_get_field_map } = require(\\\"./Lib_JS/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core\\\");\\nconst { mfdb_validator_check_referential_integrity } = require(\\\"./Lib_JS/Core/lib_bejson_Core_mfdb_validator\\\");\\n\\nconst doc = bejson_core_parse_bejson(\\\"data/orders.105.bejson\\\");\\nconst fmap = bejson_core_get_field_map(doc);\\ndoc.Values.forEach(row => console.log(`Order: ${row[fmap.order_id]}`));\\n```\\n\\n### TypeScript (`Lib_TS/Core`)\\n```typescript\\nimport { parseBejson, getFieldMap } from \\\"./Lib_TS/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core\\\";\\nimport { BEJSONDocument } from \\\"./Lib_TS/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_types\\\";\\n\\nconst doc: BEJSONDocument = parseBejson(\\\"data/orders.105.bejson\\\");\\nconst fmap = getFieldMap(doc);\\ndoc.Values.forEach(row => console.log(`Order: ${row[fmap[\\\"order_id\\\"]]}`));\\n```\\n\\n### Bash (`Lib_SH/Core`)\\n```bash\\nsource Lib_SH/Core/lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core.sh\\nsource Lib_SH/Core/lib_bejson_Core_mfdb_validator.sh\\n\\nmfdb_validator_validate_database \\\"105a.mfdb.bejson\\\"\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 10: Platform I: BEJSON_CMS\\n\\nA zero-SQL, zero-daemon publishing suite:\\n- **Four-Microservice Architecture:** Modular Flask services handling editorial draft intake, static compiling, image asset optimization, and live previews.\\n- **Relational Integrity:** 13 federated BEJSON entities managing posts, authors, taxonomies, tags, and media assets.\\n- **Static Publication:** Compiles relational datasets into standalone HTML pages styled with BECSS Tri-Color design tokens.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 11: Platform II: BKS-1 AI Writer\\n\\nAn automated technical publication engine:\\n- **Context Bubble Management:** 48,000-token partitioned memory cache preventing model context thrashing.\\n- **4-Stage Pipeline:** Ideation, Section Planning, Drafting, and Automated Tone Alignment.\\n- **Persona Architecture:** 24-field declarative persona configuration ensuring strict voice and technical consistency.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 12: Platform III: Multi-AI Provider Gateway (`Lib_PY/AI` v1.5.0) & Edge Inference\\n\\n`Lib_PY/AI` provides a hardened 3-tier AI gateway with zero external SDK dependencies, supporting **Gemini REST (`generateContent`)**, **Gemini Interactions (`v1beta/interactions`)** with stateful `previous_interaction_id` chaining, `Name_Case` class/method exports, and persistent Format 105a configuration (`Lib_PY/AI/config/ai_global_config.105a.bejson`).\\n\\n![BEJSON 105 AI Gateway \\u2014 Part 1: Abstraction, Tooling & Parser Layers (Tiers 1 & 2)](images/ai_gateway/ai_gateway_part1_tiers_1_2.jpg)  \\n*_Figure 12: BEJSON 105 AI Gateway \\u2014 Part 1: Abstraction, Tooling & Parser Layers (Tiers 1 & 2)_*\\n\\n![BEJSON 105 AI Gateway \\u2014 Part 2: Service Callers, Vault & Key Rotation (Tier 3)](images/ai_gateway/ai_gateway_part2_tier_3.jpg)  \\n*_Figure 13: BEJSON 105 AI Gateway \\u2014 Part 2: Service Callers, Vault & Key Rotation (Tier 3)_*\\n\\n- **Tier 1 \\u2014 Abstraction Layers:** `AIDispatcher` (`AI_Dispatcher`, `AI_Dispatch`, `AI_Dispatch_Async`), `AITranslator` (`AI_Translator`), and `lib_bejson_ai_models.py` (`Gemini_3_1_Flash_Lite`, `Gemini_3_5_Flash_Lite`, `Gemini_3_8_Flash`, `Gemini_4_Argon`, `Gemini_4_Hydrogen`, `Resolve_Model_Identifier`).\\n- **Tier 2 \\u2014 Gates, Tooling & Modular Parsers (`v1.5.0`):**\\n  - **Tooling Abstraction Layer (`Tooling_Registry` + `Base_Tooling_System` + `Interactions_Tooling_System`):** Config-gated via `tooling_enabled` and `active_tooling_system` with autonomous multi-turn tool execution loops.\\n  - **Modular Parser Abstraction Layer (`Parser_Registry` + `Base_Modular_Parser`):** Config-gated via `global_pre_parser_enabled` and `global_post_parser_enabled`, supporting attachable AI command parsers such as `<req\",\n      \"16\",\n      \"12029a0ccb9cfeba55a6e3d57b38bafc617dc939f9972ed5e9d513ba9d034704\",\n      \"context.txt\",\n      false,\n      false\n    ]\n  ]\n}",
      "16",
      "f5d859523838a12d399ecdabcaec7d9a7ae3ecf29fa5e42b024cd299c2ba6ccf",
